window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jemas-2024"] = {"id": "jemas-2024", "title": "JEMAS(PG) 2024", "fullTitle": "JEMAS(PG) 2024, West Bengal, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Symbol search subtest of WAIS-IV assesses:", "options": {"a": "Auditory processing.", "b": "Concentration.", "c": "Mental manipulation.", "d": "Processing speed."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: Symbol Search is a core subtest of the Processing Speed Index of the WAIS-IV, so it measures processing speed. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, fourth edition (2008), descends from the Wechsler-Bellevue scale of David Wechsler (1939). It has 10 core subtests grouped into four indices. Verbal Comprehension: Similarities, Vocabulary and Information (Comprehension supplemental). Perceptual Reasoning: Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles (Figure Weights and Picture Completion supplemental). Working Memory: Digit Span and Arithmetic (Letter-Number Sequencing supplemental). Processing Speed: Symbol Search and Coding (Cancellation supplemental). In Symbol Search the person looks at one or two target symbols, scans a row of symbols and marks whether a target is present, working against a strict time limit. In Coding the person copies symbols paired with numbers from a key. Both tasks are easy in themselves, so the score mostly reflects how quickly and accurately someone can scan, compare and respond to simple visual information. Processing speed is sensitive to brain injury, ageing and depression. Example: Meera, aged 68, recovering from a stroke, answers every Symbol Search item she attempts correctly but completes far fewer items than others her age; her low Processing Speed score points to slowed mental speed, not poor reasoning. Option d is right because Symbol Search is one of the two core subtests of the Processing Speed Index. Exam trap: \"mental manipulation\" sounds like any demanding task, but holding and manipulating information in mind is working memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic, Letter-Number Sequencing); and concentration, though every timed task needs it, is not a WAIS-IV index.", "topic": "WAIS-IV Processing Speed Index", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In his observation of motor skills of young children, Arnold Gesell found infant motor skills developing in a consistent sequence. According to his observations, an infant typically:", "options": {"a": "Walks alone at nine months.", "b": "Rolls over at one month.", "c": "Sits without support between five and six months.", "d": "Stands alone at seven months."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Arnold Gesell's norms an infant typically sits without support at about five to six months, after rolling over and well before standing or walking. Arnold Gesell (1880 to 1961), at the Yale Clinic of Child Development, filmed thousands of infants and turned the ages at which behaviours appeared into norms (1925, 1940), later used in the Gesell Developmental Schedules. He found that motor skills unfold in the same order in almost every child: head control, rolling over, sitting, crawling, standing and then walking. This follows the cephalocaudal principle, in which control spreads from head to foot. Gesell explained the order by maturation: a genetically programmed timetable in nerves and muscles, so practice adds little until the child is ready. His co-twin study with Helen Thompson (1929), in which one twin was trained in stair climbing and soon matched by the untrained twin, made this point. Rough norms: steady head control at about 6 weeks to 3 months, rolling over at about 3 to 6 months, sitting without support at about 6 months, crawling at about 7 to 9 months, standing alone at about 11 to 12 months and walking alone at about 12 months (normal range roughly 9 to 17). Example: baby Kabir rolls over at 4 months, sits on the floor without cushions at 6 months and takes his first steps just after his first birthday, an ordinary Gesell sequence. Option c is right because it is the only option whose age matches the sitting norm. Exam trap: the distractors keep a believable order but move each skill months too early. Key note: modern norms (WHO Multicentre Growth Reference Study, 2006) put unsupported sitting at about 6 months with a wide normal range, so \"five to six months\" is at the early end; it is still the only option in the right range, and c stands.", "topic": "Gesell's motor milestones", "fig": "Arnold Gesell"}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that:", "options": {"a": "Moral children always adhere to generally recognized moral standards.", "b": "Children seem to apply situational morality, acting differently in separate situations involving the same moral principle.", "c": "Moral children never cheat.", "d": "Boys generally have stronger conscience than girls."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty depended heavily on the situation, so the same child could cheat in one setting and stay honest in another that tested the same moral principle. In the Character Education Inquiry at Teachers College, Columbia University, reported in Studies in Deceit, they asked a simple question: is honesty a general trait that a child carries everywhere? They gave thousands of American schoolchildren disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in class tests, party games, athletic contests and home tasks: for instance, marking their own test papers when they could secretly change answers, or keeping coins from a box. Almost every child cheated somewhere, and few cheated everywhere. Honesty in one situation predicted honesty in another only weakly, and knowing the moral rule, or attending character education or Sunday school, barely predicted conduct. What mattered was the situation: the risk of being caught, the effort needed and what classmates were doing. They called this the doctrine of specificity. Walter Mischel (1968) later built on it in his critique of trait consistency, which started the person-situation debate; Seymour Epstein (1979) replied that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more stable. Example: Riya peeks at a classmate's answer sheet when the teacher steps out, yet on the way home returns the extra change a shopkeeper gave her by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situation-specific (situational) morality. Exam trap: the absolute words \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c describe exactly the consistent moral character that the study failed to find.", "topic": "Hartshorne and May: situational honesty", "fig": "Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May"}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "options": {"a": "Voluntary movement, intuitive thought, object permanence.", "b": "Concrete operations, mental representation, information processing.", "c": "Mental representation, symbolic thought, intuitive thought.", "d": "Conservation, object permanence, intuitive thought."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "topic": "Piaget: sequence of cognitive development", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "The play pattern most prevalent among three-year old children is:", "options": {"a": "Solitary play.", "b": "Associative play.", "c": "Cooperative play.", "d": "Parallel play."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: parallel play, playing beside other children with similar toys but not with them, is the play pattern most typical of three-year-olds. Mildred Parten (1932) watched nursery-school children aged two to five and sorted their free play by how socially involved it was. Her six categories, from least to most social, are: unoccupied behaviour (not playing, watching whatever catches the eye); solitary play (playing alone, absorbed in one's own toys); onlooker behaviour (watching others play, perhaps talking to them, without joining in); parallel play (playing alongside others with the same kind of toys, without trying to influence them); associative play (playing together and sharing materials, but with no common goal or division of roles); and cooperative play (organised play with a shared goal and roles, such as building a house together or playing a team game). The more social forms rise with age, but the earlier forms do not vanish: solitary and parallel play remain common in older preschoolers. Example: three-year-olds Arjun and Meera sit side by side in the sandpit, each filling a bucket and now and then glancing at the other, but neither joins the other's game. Option d is right because parallel play is the pattern most characteristic of the third year. Exam trap: associative play looks similar because the children are close together, but in associative play they share toys and talk about the activity, and it rises mainly from about 3 to 4. Key note: parallel play peaks around 2 to 3 and associative play rises through 3 to 4, so age 3 sits near the boundary; child development texts give parallel play as the typical pattern of the three-year-old, and d stands.", "topic": "Parten's stages of social play", "fig": "Mildred Parten"}, {"q": "The positive psychology movement:", "options": {"a": "Took root within psychodynamic theory.", "b": "Had its early beginning in social behaviorism.", "c": "Tends to de-emphasize the importance of adaptation.", "d": "Found thematic parallels in humanistic psychology."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: positive psychology shares its central themes of growth, strengths, meaning and well-being with the humanistic psychology of Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1961). Positive psychology is the scientific study of positive experiences, positive traits and the institutions that help people flourish. Martin Seligman (1998) launched it in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, and he and Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi set out its programme in a special issue of the American Psychologist (2000). Seligman argued that psychology had spent decades on disease and repair and should also study what makes life worth living. Its topics include happiness, character strengths, hope, gratitude, resilience, flow (Csikszentmihalyi's absorbing state when challenge matches skill) and the PERMA model of well-being (positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment). These themes echo humanistic psychology, the \"third force\" of the 1950s and 1960s, which stressed self-actualisation and the fully functioning person; Maslow even used the phrase \"positive psychology\" in Motivation and Personality. The main difference is method: positive psychology insists on measurement and controlled research, which humanists were often accused of neglecting. Example: instead of only asking why Kabir's anxiety keeps returning, a positive psychologist also measures his strengths, such as kindness and curiosity, and helps him use them every day. Option d is right because the two movements share clear thematic parallels, even though positive psychology claims a more empirical style. Exam trap: c reverses the movement, which emphasises adaptation, resilience and the strengths that help people cope, rather than playing them down.", "topic": "Positive psychology and humanism", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "Language comprehension is traditionally associated with which area of the brain:", "options": {"a": "Recorla's.", "b": "Broca's.", "c": "Gage's.", "d": "Wernicke's."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: language comprehension is traditionally linked to Wernicke's area, in the back part of the left superior temporal gyrus. The classic map of language in the brain came from patients with strokes. Paul Broca (1861) studied a man nicknamed \"Tan\", who understood speech but could say little beyond that one syllable; at autopsy the damage lay in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area and linked with speech production and grammar. Carl Wernicke (1874) then described patients with damage further back, in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant (usually left) hemisphere. They spoke fluently, with normal rhythm, but their speech was full of wrong or invented words and they understood little of what was said to them. This is Wernicke's (fluent, receptive) aphasia, and the region is Wernicke's area. A bundle of fibres, the arcuate fasciculus, joins the two areas; damage to it causes conduction aphasia, with very poor repetition. Example: after a stroke, Arjun talks easily and quickly, but when his daughter asks him to close his eyes he smiles and describes his breakfast; he has lost comprehension, which points to Wernicke's area. Option d is right because Wernicke's area is the classic centre for understanding spoken and written language. Exam trap: Broca's area is the famous near miss, since it is also a left-hemisphere language area, but it serves production, so Broca's aphasia is halting, effortful speech with fairly good understanding.", "topic": "Wernicke's area and comprehension", "fig": "Carl Wernicke"}, {"q": "In the Jungian view of personality, key functions include:", "options": {"a": "Will to power, will to pleasure.", "b": "Sensing, feeling, intuiting.", "c": "Prototaxic, parataxic, syntaxic.", "d": "Extroversion, introversion, interposition."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: sensing, feeling and intuiting are three of the four psychological functions in Carl Jung's analytical psychology. In Psychological Types, Carl Jung (1921) asked why people meet the same world so differently. His first answer was two attitudes, which describe the direction of psychic energy: extraversion, in which energy flows outward to people and things, and introversion, in which it flows inward to one's own thoughts and feelings. His second answer was four functions, the ways the mind takes in and judges experience, arranged in two opposed pairs. Thinking judges whether something is true or false, and feeling judges whether it is pleasant or valuable; because both evaluate, Jung called them the rational functions. Sensing registers what is actually there through the senses, like a camera, and intuiting grasps possibilities and hunches beyond the evidence; because both perceive without evaluating, he called them the irrational functions. One function usually dominates while its opposite stays undeveloped in the unconscious. Combining the 2 attitudes with the 4 functions gives his 8 psychological types, such as the introverted thinking type or the extraverted feeling type. Example: on a holiday, Meera notices every colour, smell and price in the market (sensing), while her brother Kabir keeps imagining what the old town could become (intuiting). Option b is right because it lists three genuine Jungian functions, with only thinking left out. Exam trap: d uses Jung's real words extraversion and introversion, but these are attitudes, not functions, and \"interposition\" is a depth cue from perception.", "topic": "Jung's four psychological functions", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "options": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working.", "b": "Working harder in groups than individually.", "c": "Working harder individually than in groups.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "topic": "Social loafing", "fig": "Bibb Latane"}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "options": {"a": "Taste.", "b": "Sight.", "c": "Hearing.", "d": "Touch."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "topic": "Somatosensory cortex and touch", "fig": ""}, {"q": "REM sleep behavior disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterized as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "options": {"a": "Atonia.", "b": "Rapid eye movements.", "c": "High frequency, low amplitude EEG.", "d": "Penile erections (in males)."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "topic": "REM sleep behaviour disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin.", "b": "Dopamine.", "c": "Epinephrine.", "d": "Insulin."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "topic": "Hormones versus neurotransmitters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is not classified as an opioid?", "options": {"a": "Cocaine.", "b": "Codeine.", "c": "Heroin.", "d": "Morphine."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "topic": "Opioids versus stimulants", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "options": {"a": "Hit.", "b": "Miss.", "c": "False alarm.", "d": "False positive."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "topic": "Signal detection outcomes", "fig": "David Green and John Swets"}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "options": {"a": "Anomalous conditioning.", "b": "Distinctive conditioning.", "c": "Counterconditioning.", "d": "Higher-order conditioning."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "topic": "Higher-order conditioning", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an accurate learning acquisition sequence?", "options": {"a": "Drive-cue-response-reinforcement.", "b": "Cue-drive-response-reinforcement.", "c": "Cue-response-drive-reinforcement.", "d": "Drive-response-cue-reinforcement."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the learning theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941, 1950), learning runs from drive to cue to response to reinforcement. At Yale, in Social Learning and Imitation and later Personality and Psychotherapy, they restated Sigmund Freud's clinical ideas in the language of the drive-reduction theory of Clark Hull (1943). They said learning needs four things, often summed up as \"want something, notice something, do something, get something\". A drive is a strong stimulus that pushes the person to act; it can be primary (hunger, pain) or learned (fear, the need for approval). A cue is a weaker stimulus that tells the person when, where and how to respond. A response is the behaviour itself. Reinforcement (reward) is the drive reduction that follows, which strengthens the link between that cue and that response so it is more likely next time. Their key idea was the learned drive: Miller's rats, once shocked in a white box, learned to turn a wheel to escape it even with no shock present, reinforced only by fear reduction. They used the same scheme to explain repression (not-thinking reinforced by relief from fear) and approach-avoidance conflict. Example: Riya is hungry (drive), sees the college canteen sign (cue), walks in and orders idli (response), and her hunger falls (reinforcement), so next time the sign draws her in faster. Option a is right because it lists the four elements in their causal order. Exam trap: the distractors reuse the same four words in a shuffled order; because a cue only guides behaviour that a drive has already set going, any order that puts the cue first, or the response before the cue, is wrong.", "topic": "Dollard and Miller's learning sequence", "fig": "John Dollard and Neal Miller"}, {"q": "An animal receives a shock but learns that pressing a bar will turn off the shock. However, if there is no response that will turn off the shock, the situation is described by the term:", "options": {"a": "Avoidance learning.", "b": "Second-order conditioning.", "c": "Learned helplessness.", "d": "Latent learning."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: when no response can end an aversive event, an animal learns that it has no control and stops trying, a state called learned helplessness. The stem contrasts two situations. In the first, pressing a bar turns off a shock that is already on; this is escape learning, a form of negative reinforcement, and the animal learns it quickly. In the second, nothing works. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs shocks they could switch off by pressing a panel, a second, yoked group identical shocks they could not control, and a third group no shock. Later all the dogs were placed in a shuttle box, where jumping a low barrier ended the shock. Most dogs in the escapable and no-shock groups learned to jump, but most yoked dogs lay down and whined, passively taking shocks they could easily have escaped. Lack of control, not the shock itself, produced the deficit. Seligman offered learned helplessness as a model of depression; Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later recast it in attributional terms, and Seligman turned it around into learned optimism. Example: after failing three maths tests despite studying hard, Kabir stops revising for the fourth, convinced nothing he does matters, even though a good tutor is now available. Option c is right because it names the state produced by uncontrollable aversive events. Exam trap: avoidance learning sounds close, but it means responding to a warning signal so the shock never arrives; it needs a response that works, which is exactly what is missing here.", "topic": "Learned helplessness", "fig": "Martin Seligman and Steven Maier"}, {"q": "One is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, four is a door. This is part of improving memory and the method is known as:", "options": {"a": "Pegword.", "b": "Loci.", "c": "Visual imagery.", "d": "Flashbulb."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: \"one is a bun, two is a shoe\" is the pegword (peg-word) method, a mnemonic that hangs each item to be remembered on a rhyming number peg. A mnemonic is any device that adds organisation, meaning or imagery so that material is easier to recall. In the pegword method you first overlearn a fixed rhyme: one is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, four is a door, and so on up to ten. These words are the pegs. To remember a list, you form a vivid, interacting image of each item with its peg. Because the pegs are numbered and never change, you can recall the list in order, start in the middle, or say at once which item was seventh. The method works mainly through visual imagery: the dual coding theory of Allan Paivio (1971) holds that concrete words stored both as an image and as a verbal label are remembered better than words stored only one way. Example: to remember milk, onions and soap, Meera pictures a bun soaked in milk, a shoe stuffed with onions and a tree hung with bars of soap; in the shop she runs through the rhyme and each image comes back. Option a is right because a rhyme of numbered pegs is the defining feature of the pegword method. Exam trap: the method of loci also uses vivid images, but it places them at locations along a familiar route, not on number rhymes.", "topic": "Pegword mnemonic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is a stage in the Stages of Change Model?", "options": {"a": "Relaxation.", "b": "Contemplation.", "c": "Deliberation.", "d": "Meditation."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: contemplation is the second stage of the Stages of Change (transtheoretical) model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983). They built the model from studies of how smokers quit, with and without therapy, and found that people change through a predictable series of stages rather than in one leap. In precontemplation there is no intention to change within the next six months, and the person may not see a problem at all. In contemplation the person knows there is a problem and is seriously thinking about change, but is ambivalent, weighing pros and cons, and can stay stuck here for a long time. In preparation the person intends to act within the next month and takes small steps. In action the behaviour has been changed within the past six months. In maintenance the change has lasted more than six months and the work is preventing relapse. Termination, added later, means there is no temptation left. Relapse is common, and people often cycle back through earlier stages. The model guides treatment: a person in precontemplation needs awareness raising, not a quit plan, and motivational interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, 1991) is often paired with it to resolve ambivalence. Example: Arjun says, \"I know my drinking is hurting my family and I should cut down, but not yet; it helps me relax.\" He is in contemplation. Option b is right because contemplation is a named stage of the model. Exam trap: deliberation sounds like a synonym for contemplation, but it is not the model's term; the stage is called contemplation.", "topic": "Stages of Change model", "fig": "James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente"}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client-centered therapist most likely to use?", "options": {"a": "Analysis.", "b": "Giving advice.", "c": "Reflection.", "d": "Aversive conditioning."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) named the core conditions that let clients heal themselves: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "topic": "Client-centred reflection", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "options": {"a": "Judgement.", "b": "Perception.", "c": "Intelligence.", "d": "Thinking."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "topic": "Hallucination as perceptual disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "options": {"a": "Affective flattening.", "b": "Bizarre behaviour.", "c": "Circumstantiality.", "d": "Delusions."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "topic": "Positive versus negative symptoms", "fig": "Nancy Andreasen"}, {"q": "When same group is studied over a period of time; the study method is known as?", "options": {"a": "Longitudinal.", "b": "Sequential.", "c": "Cross-sectional.", "d": "Recapitulation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a longitudinal study follows the same group of people and measures them repeatedly over a period of time. Developmental psychologists have three main designs for studying how behaviour changes with age. In a longitudinal design the same individuals are re-tested over months or years, so the researcher sees real change within each person and can test stability, such as whether a shy toddler becomes a shy teenager. The gifted children study of Lewis Terman (1921) followed about 1,500 children into old age. Its drawbacks are cost, time, loss of participants (attrition), practice effects from repeated testing, and results that may be tied to one generation. In a cross-sectional design, different age groups are tested once, at the same time; it is quick and cheap, but it confounds age with cohort (generation) effects and shows only group differences, not individual change. A sequential design (K. Warner Schaie, 1965) combines the two by following several cohorts over time; his Seattle Longitudinal Study, begun in 1956, found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood. Example: a researcher tests Riya and 200 other children on vocabulary at ages 5, 10 and 15 to see how each child's language grows; because the same children are tested each time, the study is longitudinal. Option a is right because studying the same group repeatedly over time is the definition of a longitudinal design. Exam trap: sequential designs also follow people over time, but they track several cohorts at once, so \"same group studied over time\" points to longitudinal.", "topic": "Longitudinal research design", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which psychologist thought that our behaviours are learnt through interaction with, and observation of, the environment?", "options": {"a": "Cattell.", "b": "Hull.", "c": "Sigmund Freud.", "d": "Albert Bandura."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Albert Bandura (1977) held that behaviour is learned through interaction with, and observation of, the social environment. Bandura argued that strict behaviourism, in which a person must respond and be reinforced, could not explain how quickly people pick up complex behaviour. Much learning is observational: we watch a model, notice what happens to that model (vicarious reinforcement or punishment) and later copy the behaviour. In the Bobo doll studies of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961, 1963), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later imitated the same acts, often word for word, while children who saw a calm model rarely did. Bandura described four processes needed for modelling: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. In Social Learning Theory (1977) and later social cognitive theory (1986) he added reciprocal determinism: behaviour, personal factors (thoughts, expectations, self-efficacy) and the environment all influence one another, so people both shape and are shaped by their surroundings. Example: Kabir watches his older sister earn praise for greeting guests politely and starts greeting them himself, though nobody has ever rewarded him directly. Option d is right because learning through observation of, and interaction with, the environment is the core of Bandura's theory. Exam trap: Clark Hull is also a learning theorist, but his drive-reduction theory explains learning through reinforcement of one's own responses, not through watching others.", "topic": "Bandura's observational learning", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "The procedure in which people learn to modify internal responses such as heart rate and body temperature is known as:", "options": {"a": "Biofeedback.", "b": "Hypnosis.", "c": "Sublimation.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: biofeedback is the procedure in which people learn to change internal bodily responses, such as heart rate or skin temperature, by watching a live signal of them. Normally we cannot sense our blood pressure or muscle tension precisely, so we cannot learn to control them. In biofeedback, sensors measure a bodily process and an instrument turns it into a tone, graph or light that changes moment by moment. When the signal moves in the desired direction, that change is reinforced, so the person gradually learns which thoughts, breathing patterns or ways of relaxing produce it. Common types include electromyographic (EMG) feedback of muscle tension for tension headache, skin temperature (thermal) feedback for migraine, heart-rate feedback for anxiety, and EEG feedback (neurofeedback) for attention problems. The idea rests on the claim of Neal Miller (1969) that autonomic responses, once thought purely involuntary, can be operantly conditioned; his early animal results proved hard to replicate, but human biofeedback is useful, especially when combined with relaxation training. Example: Meera has tension headaches; with EMG sensors on her forehead she hears a tone that falls as her muscles relax, and over several sessions she learns to lower the tone without the machine. Option a is right because learning to modify internal responses using feedback about them is the definition of biofeedback. Exam trap: hypnosis can also change bodily experience, but through suggestion, with no feedback signal to learn from, so it is not this procedure.", "topic": "Biofeedback", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"First Rank Symptoms\" of schizophrenia given by Schneider include all except:", "options": {"a": "Depersonalisation.", "b": "Running commentary of one's thoughts.", "c": "Primary delusion.", "d": "Somatic passivity."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: depersonalisation is not one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia. Kurt Schneider (1939), a German psychiatrist, wanted symptoms that clinicians could rate reliably and that, in the absence of brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia; his Clinical Psychopathology (English edition 1959) made them famous as first-rank symptoms (FRS). Their common theme is a breakdown of the boundary between self and the outside world. Three kinds of voices: audible thoughts (thought echo), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity (made) experiences: made feelings, made impulses and made volitional acts, felt as controlled by an outside force, plus somatic passivity, bodily sensations imposed from outside. Finally, delusional perception: a normal perception suddenly given an unrelated delusional meaning. Depersonalisation, the feeling that one's self is unreal or detached, is common in anxiety, depersonalisation-derealisation disorder and after drug use, and insight is kept. Example: Arjun says his neighbour's thoughts are being pushed into his head (thought insertion, an FRS), while Kabir says that during panic attacks he feels as if he is watching himself from outside (depersonalisation, not an FRS). Option a is right because depersonalisation is the only option missing from Schneider's list. Exam trap: depersonalisation sounds like passivity, but in passivity the person believes an outside agency controls them, while in depersonalisation they know the strangeness is a feeling. Key note: the booklet's wording is loose: the symptom is voices giving a running commentary on one's actions, and the delusion on the list is delusional perception, which is one kind of primary delusion (Karl Jaspers, 1913); neither changes the answer, and a stands.", "topic": "Schneider's first-rank symptoms", "fig": "Kurt Schneider"}, {"q": "The Indian Psychoanalytic Society was founded by:", "options": {"a": "Girindra Sekhar Bose.", "b": "Brajendra Nath Seal.", "c": "N. N. Sengupta.", "d": "Sigmund Freud."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Girindrasekhar Bose (1922) founded the Indian Psychoanalytic Society in Calcutta. Bose (1887 to 1953) trained first in medicine and then in psychology at Calcutta University, and worked out psychoanalytic ideas largely on his own; his doctoral thesis, Concept of Repression (1921), began his long correspondence with Sigmund Freud. The letters show real disagreement. Bose proposed his theory of opposite wishes, in which every wish is paired with its opposite, one expressed and the other repressed, and he did not give the Oedipus complex the central place that Freud did. He is called the father of psychoanalysis in India. Modern Indian psychology itself began at Calcutta University. The philosopher Brajendra Nath Seal drew up the syllabus of India's first psychology department there (1915 to 1916). Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), who had trained under Hugo Munsterberg at Harvard, set up its experimental laboratory and later led the founding of the Indian Psychological Association in the mid-1920s. Example: if Riya is asked who brought Freud's method to India and argued about it with Freud himself, the answer is Bose; if she is asked who brought the experimental laboratory, it is Sengupta. Option a is right because Bose founded the society and led it as its first president. Exam trap: all four names belong to the same early Calcutta story, so examiners count on you mixing up the founder of psychoanalysis in India (Bose) with the founder of experimental psychology in India (Sengupta), or assuming Freud founded the society himself.", "topic": "Indian Psychoanalytic Society founder", "fig": "Girindrasekhar Bose"}, {"q": "\"Kluver-Bucy Syndrome\" is characterised by all except:", "options": {"a": "Visual Agnosia.", "b": "Hypersexuality.", "c": "Compulsive licking and biting.", "d": "Delusions."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: delusions are not part of Kluver-Bucy syndrome, the cluster of behavioural changes that follows damage to both temporal lobes, especially both amygdalae. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both anterior temporal lobes, amygdalae included, in rhesus monkeys. Wild, aggressive monkeys became tame and placid, and approached snakes and people without fear. The monkeys showed a set of signs now named after the two researchers: psychic blindness, a visual agnosia in which objects are seen but their meaning is not recognised; hyperorality, examining everything by mouth (licking, biting, mouthing objects); hypersexuality, sexual behaviour directed at unusual partners and objects; placidity and loss of fear; hypermetamorphosis, a compulsion to attend to and touch every visual stimulus; and dietary change, eating things they normally refuse, such as meat. Most of these follow from losing the amygdala, which attaches emotional meaning and fear to what we perceive. In humans, partial forms follow herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia, often with aphasia and amnesia from wider damage. Delusions, false fixed beliefs, belong to psychosis and are not part of the syndrome. Example: after herpes encephalitis, Kabir puts coins and keys in his mouth, cannot tell what a comb is just by looking at it, and shows no fear of a barking dog, a partial Kluver-Bucy picture. Option d is right because delusions are the only option missing from the syndrome. Exam trap: \"compulsive licking and biting\" is simply hyperorality in plain words, so do not choose it as the exception just because the technical term is absent.", "topic": "Kluver-Bucy syndrome features", "fig": "Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy"}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum.", "b": "Cerebellum.", "c": "Motor Cortex.", "d": "Limbic system."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. His cerebellum and basal ganglia were intact. The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "topic": "Procedural memory and cerebellum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to social-exchange theory we will help when:", "options": {"a": "The benefits are external and the costs are internal.", "b": "The benefits are greater than the costs.", "c": "The benefits and costs are proportional.", "d": "The benefits are smaller than the costs."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: social exchange theory predicts that we help when the benefits of helping are greater than the costs. Social exchange theory, developed by George Homans (1958) and by John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), treats social life as a kind of trade in which people try, often without noticing, to maximise rewards and minimise costs. Applied to helping, a bystander weighs what helping would bring against what it would cost. Rewards can be external, such as praise, payment or a good reputation, or internal, such as relief from one's own distress at seeing someone suffer, or feeling good about oneself. Costs include time, effort, danger and embarrassment. On this view even helping that looks unselfish is partly self-interested, so the theory sides with psychological egoism in the debate about true altruism. The arousal: cost-reward model of Jane Piliavin and colleagues (1981) uses the same logic: seeing someone in distress causes unpleasant arousal, which bystanders reduce by the cheapest route, so they help when the costs of helping are low and the costs of not helping (guilt, blame) are high. In their New York subway studies (1969), a collapsed man with a cane was helped far more often than one who seemed drunk. Example: Meera stops to help an elderly man pick up his spilt fruit because it takes a minute and she feels good, but drives past a stranded car on a dark highway because the risk feels too high. Option b is right because helping occurs when perceived rewards outweigh perceived costs. Exam trap: a borrows the theory's real external and internal language but garbles it; benefits and costs can each be external or internal, and what matters is which side is larger.", "topic": "Social exchange theory of helping", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by:", "options": {"a": "Terman.", "b": "Allport.", "c": "William Stern.", "d": "Watson."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was introduced by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test in Paris, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's result as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child could pass. Mental age alone is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. Stern's solution was to divide mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove the decimals in his Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100, and made the abbreviation IQ popular. So if Arjun is 8 years old with a mental age of 10, his IQ is 10/8 x 100 = 125. The ratio IQ later ran into trouble with adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and its name. Exam trap: Terman is the most common wrong answer because the Stanford-Binet made IQ famous, but he popularised the term rather than introducing it. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient, mental age divided by chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and made the abbreviation IQ popular, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "What does MMSE test help to assess?", "options": {"a": "Phobias.", "b": "Depression.", "c": "ADHD.", "d": "Dementia."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) is a brief screening test for cognitive impairment, used chiefly to detect and track dementia. Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh (1975) designed it as a ten-minute bedside test. It is scored out of 30: orientation to time and place (10), registration of three words (3), attention and calculation, such as serial sevens (5), recall of the three words (3), and language and copying (9), which covers naming, repeating a phrase, following a three-step command, reading, writing a sentence and copying intersecting pentagons. A score of 23 or below (cut-off 23/24) suggests cognitive impairment, though education and age affect scores; the Hindi Mental State Examination (Mary Ganguli, 1995) adapted it for rural Indian elders with little schooling. It is a screen, not a diagnosis: a low score calls for a full assessment. Example: Riya's 74-year-old grandmother cannot name the year, recalls none of the three words and cannot copy the pentagons, scoring 18, so her doctor refers her for a dementia work-up. Option d is right because the MMSE is designed to screen for dementia. Exam trap: depression can lower MMSE scores (depressive pseudodementia), but the MMSE does not measure mood; depression needs its own scales, such as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961).", "topic": "MMSE as a dementia screen", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the full form of the test ISAA?", "options": {"a": "Indian scale for autism in adults.", "b": "International scale for assessment of autism.", "c": "Indian scale for assessment of Autism.", "d": "Indian scale for adult autism."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: ISAA stands for the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism. It is a rating scale built in India to identify autism spectrum disorder and to grade how severe it is. The National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped (NIMH, Secunderabad, now called NIEPID, the National Institute for the Empowerment of Persons with Intellectual Disabilities) developed it in 2009, partly drawing on items of the Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS) of Eric Schopler and colleagues (1980). ISAA has 40 items spread over six domains: social relationship and reciprocity, emotional responsiveness, speech-language and communication, behaviour patterns, sensory aspects and the cognitive component. The clinician observes the child and interviews the parents, then rates each item from 1 (rarely) to 5 (always). Totals therefore run from 40 to 200; a total below 70 means no autism, and higher totals grade mild, moderate or severe autism with a disability percentage. This is why the scale matters in practice: ISAA is the instrument prescribed for certifying autism as a disability in India. For example, when Kabir, aged 6, is brought to a government hospital for a disability certificate, the psychologist scores ISAA, and his total decides whether he is certified and at what percentage. Option c is right because every word of the name fits: Indian (developed and standardised in India), Scale, for Assessment (it measures and grades), of Autism (the condition as a whole, not only in adults). Exam trap: each distractor changes one word, swapping \"Indian\" for \"International\" or \"Assessment\" for \"adults\"; ISAA is not an adult-only tool (it is mostly used with children), and no international scale goes by this name.", "topic": "ISAA autism scale", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is not a defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Displacement.", "b": "Catastrophizing.", "c": "Reaction formation.", "d": "Humor."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: catastrophising is not a defence mechanism; it is a cognitive distortion. A defence mechanism, an idea Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced and Anna Freud systematised in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), is an unconscious strategy of the ego that keeps anxiety out of awareness by distorting or disguising a threatening impulse or fact. Displacement moves a feeling from a dangerous target to a safer one, as when Arjun, scolded by his boss, shouts at his younger brother. Reaction formation hides an unacceptable impulse behind its exaggerated opposite, such as gushing over a rival one secretly resents. Humour counts as a defence too: George Vaillant (1977), in his hierarchy of defences, placed it among the mature defences, alongside sublimation, suppression, altruism and anticipation, because it lets a person face something painful while easing the tension. Catastrophising works the other way. It means expecting the worst possible outcome and treating it as unbearable: Meera gets one low mark and concludes she will fail the course, lose her career and ruin her life. Albert Ellis (1962) called this \"awfulising\" in rational emotive therapy, Aaron Beck (1967) described related thinking errors such as magnification, and David Burns (1980) listed it in Feeling Good. It is a habitual, largely conscious thinking error that raises anxiety rather than lowering it, and therapists target it with cognitive restructuring. Option b is right because catastrophising belongs to the cognitive therapy list of distortions, not to the psychoanalytic list of ego defences. Exam trap: both lists describe distorted thinking, so examiners mix them; ask whether the process unconsciously protects the ego from anxiety (a defence) or creates extra anxiety through an exaggerated appraisal (a distortion).", "topic": "Defences versus cognitive distortions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease.", "b": "Korsakoff's disease.", "c": "Parkinson's disease.", "d": "Huntington's disease."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness marked by profound retrograde amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian neuropsychiatrist, described it in long-term heavy drinkers. The cause is thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency: heavy drinkers often eat poorly, and alcohol also interferes with the absorption and use of thiamine. The acute stage is often Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881), with confusion, ataxia (an unsteady gait) and eye-movement palsy; if thiamine is not given quickly, the chronic Korsakoff stage follows, so the two are named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the medial (mediodorsal) thalamus. The memory picture has three parts. There is dense anterograde amnesia, so new events are lost within minutes. There is retrograde amnesia reaching back years, with a temporal gradient: recent years are lost more than childhood. And there is confabulation, sincere invented stories that fill the gaps. For example, Rajesh, aged 55, tells his doctor he drove to the office this morning, though he has been in hospital for a month and left his job years ago. Immediate memory span and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option b is right because it is the only option caused by chronic alcohol use, and its retrograde loss is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is an amnestic syndrome caused by a vitamin deficiency, not a primary degenerative dementia, so early thiamine can prevent it; and confabulation is not lying or malingering, because the patient believes the story.", "topic": "Korsakoff's syndrome", "fig": "Sergei Korsakoff"}, {"q": "After recent catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Controlled experiment.", "b": "Field experiment.", "c": "Ex post facto field research.", "d": "Psychometric research."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: interviewing flood survivors after the event is ex post facto field research. \"Ex post facto\" is Latin for \"after the fact\". Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, defined it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred, or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher starts from the effect and looks back at the presumed cause. It is also called causal-comparative research. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, assign some people to it and spare others, so manipulation and random assignment are impossible. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards, into people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, and measure outcomes such as fear, sleep problems or grief, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. That combination, an independent variable that happened on its own plus data gathered in the natural setting, is ex post facto field research. Imagine Ananya, a clinical psychologist, interviewing 120 families in Assam two months after the floods to see how many show post-traumatic stress symptoms. Option c is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the study took place in the field. The design has known weaknesses: people were not randomly exposed, other differences between groups may explain the results, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused what followed (the post hoc fallacy). Exam trap: the word \"field\" pulls students toward field experiment, but every experiment, in the laboratory or in the field, requires the researcher to manipulate the independent variable; without manipulation it is ex post facto research, not an experiment.", "topic": "Ex post facto field research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Memory can be distorted by being influenced by the individual's expectation rather than what has actually happened. The term is called:", "options": {"a": "Confirmation bias.", "b": "Mood dependent memory.", "c": "Mood congruence effect.", "d": "Flash bulb memory."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: memory distorted by what a person expected, rather than by what actually happened, is described here as confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember information that fits what we already believe or expect, and to overlook what does not. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task: people guessed a rule such as \"numbers rising by two\" and tested only sequences that fitted their guess, so they never found the true rule (any increasing numbers). The same bias acts on memory. Expectations work like a filter when we store an event and like a template when we recall it, so details that fit are kept and sharpened while details that clash are dropped or bent. Suppose Kabir expects his new colleague Riya to be careless. After a month he clearly recalls the one report she submitted late but has no memory of the five she submitted early. The broader mechanism is reconstructive memory: Frederic Bartlett (1932), in Remembering, found that English students retelling the Native American story \"The War of the Ghosts\" reshaped it to fit their own cultural expectations, for instance turning canoes into boats. Option a is right because it is the only option that names a distortion driven by expectation; the other three concern mood or vivid emotional events. Exam trap: mood-dependent memory and the mood-congruence effect also bend recall, but their driver is the person's emotional state, not a belief about what should have happened. Key note: the stricter textbook name for memory reshaped by expectations is schema-driven, reconstructive memory (Bartlett, 1932); confirmation bias is the broader tendency to seek, notice and recall what fits our expectations. It is the only option about expectations, so a stands.", "topic": "Expectation-driven memory distortion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Elisabeth Kubler-Ross, most people experience predictable stages when they face impending death. Which is the correct sequence of the stages?", "options": {"a": "Anger, sadness, bargaining, denial, acceptance.", "b": "Denial, Anger, bargaining, sadness, acceptance.", "c": "Bargaining, anger, denial, sadness, acceptance.", "d": "Acceptance, anger, bargaining, denial, sadness."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) gave the sequence denial, anger, bargaining, depression (here called sadness) and acceptance. Kubler-Ross, a Swiss-American psychiatrist, interviewed terminally ill hospital patients in Chicago and described, in On Death and Dying, how people often react on learning that they are dying. First comes denial (\"This cannot be happening; the reports must be wrong\"), which cushions the shock. As denial fails, anger appears (\"Why me?\"), often aimed at doctors, family or God. Next is bargaining, an attempt to postpone death by striking a deal (\"If I live to see my daughter's wedding, I will ask for nothing more\"). When the illness can no longer be denied, depression or sadness sets in, over losses already suffered and losses still to come. Finally, many reach acceptance, a calm, sometimes tired readiness. A memory aid is DABDA. For example, Meera's father, told he has advanced cancer, first insists the scan was mixed up, then shouts at the nurses, then promises to give up smoking if he can attend his grandson's naming ceremony, then withdraws and weeps, and at last calmly starts settling his affairs. Option b is right because it is the only option that lists the five stages in Kubler-Ross's order. Her work helped launch the hospice and palliative care movement and changed how doctors speak to dying patients. Exam trap: the stages are not a fixed ladder; Kubler-Ross herself said people can skip stages, move back and forth or feel two at once, and later research has not confirmed a set sequence. That caution does not change the textbook order, which is always DABDA, so any option that starts with anger, bargaining or acceptance is wrong.", "topic": "Kubler-Ross stages of dying", "fig": "Elisabeth Kubler-Ross"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big Five personality factors?", "options": {"a": "Extroversion.", "b": "Agreeableness.", "c": "Submissiveness.", "d": "Openness to experience."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: submissiveness is not one of the Big Five personality factors. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, is the most widely accepted map of broad personality traits. It grew from the lexical hypothesis: the important ways people differ become words in everyday language, so factor-analysing trait words should reveal the main dimensions. Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) found five factors in US Air Force ratings, Warren Norman (1963) confirmed them, and Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five. Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) reached the same five with questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory (revised as the NEO-PI-R, 1992). Remember them as OCEAN: Openness to experience (curious, imaginative), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable), Extraversion (sociable, energetic), Agreeableness (kind, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious and moody, as against emotionally stable). Each is a continuum, and most people score near the middle. For example, Arjun may score high on conscientiousness, never missing a deadline, but low on extraversion, preferring a quiet evening at home. Submissiveness, a tendency to yield to others and avoid confrontation, is a narrower quality. In the NEO-PI-R, assertiveness is a facet of extraversion and compliance is a facet of agreeableness, so submissiveness overlaps with pieces of two factors but is not a factor itself. Option c is right because extraversion, agreeableness and openness to experience are three of the five named factors, while submissiveness is not on the list. Exam trap: dominance versus submissiveness does appear in other trait systems, for example as factor E (dominance) in Raymond Cattell's 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949), so it sounds like a standard trait; it is still not one of the Big Five.", "topic": "Big Five personality factors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The tendency to organize things to make a balanced or symmetrical figure that includes all parts is called:", "options": {"a": "Principle of continuation.", "b": "Principle of similarity.", "c": "Law of good figure.", "d": "Law of closure."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the tendency to organise what we see into the most balanced, symmetrical and complete figure is the law of good figure, also called the law of Pragnanz. It is the master principle of Gestalt psychology, the German school which held that we perceive organised wholes rather than collections of separate elements. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual grouping, and Kurt Koffka (1935), in Principles of Gestalt Psychology, stated Pragnanz as the rule behind them: every pattern is seen in the simplest, most regular, stable and symmetrical organisation that the stimulus allows. \"Pragnanz\" is German for conciseness or good form. Two overlapping rings, for instance, are seen as two complete circles, not as three odd-shaped pieces. When Neha glances at the Olympic rings she sees five neat circles linked together, not a tangle of curved fragments. The other grouping laws can be read as special cases of this pull toward good form: proximity (near items group), similarity (alike items group), continuation (items on a smooth line or curve group) and closure (gaps are filled). Option c is right because balance, symmetry and the simplest complete form are exactly what good figure describes. Exam trap: the phrase \"includes all parts\" pulls students toward closure, but closure is narrower, the filling-in of gaps in an incomplete outline. Key note: closure is the near miss because of \"includes all parts\"; closure is filling gaps so a broken figure looks whole, while the drive toward the balanced, symmetrical, simplest form is the definition of good figure, so c stands.", "topic": "Gestalt law of Pragnanz", "fig": ""}, {"q": "As per ICD-10 Classification of mental and behavioural disorders what is the diagnostic code for Schizophrenia:", "options": {"a": "F20.", "b": "F90.", "c": "F00.", "d": "F22."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: in ICD-10, schizophrenia is coded F20. The International Classification of Diseases, tenth revision, published by the World Health Organization as The ICD-10 Classification of Mental and Behavioural Disorders (1992), puts mental disorders in chapter V, with codes beginning with F. The first digit after F marks the block: F0 organic disorders (including the dementias), F1 substance use, F2 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders, F3 mood disorders, F4 neurotic and stress-related disorders, F5 behavioural syndromes with physical factors (such as eating and sleep disorders), F6 personality disorders, F7 mental retardation, F8 developmental disorders and F9 behavioural and emotional disorders of childhood. Inside the F2 block, F20 is schizophrenia (with subtypes such as F20.0 paranoid), F21 schizotypal disorder, F22 persistent delusional disorders, F23 acute and transient psychotic disorders and F25 schizoaffective disorders. So if Rohan's case file reads \"F20.0\", Rohan has a diagnosis of paranoid schizophrenia. Option a is right because F20 is the code that opens the psychotic block and stands for schizophrenia itself. Exam trap: examiners offer look-alike codes, such as F22 (delusional disorder) from the same block and F00 (dementia in Alzheimer's disease) from the organic block; also remember that ICD-11, in use from 2022, has a new coding system in which schizophrenia is 6A20.", "topic": "ICD-10 code for schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In research the following is not a type of sampling method:", "options": {"a": "Snowball.", "b": "Systematic.", "c": "Stratified.", "d": "Closure."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: closure is not a sampling method; it is a Gestalt law of perception, the tendency to fill gaps so that an incomplete figure looks whole. Sampling is the way a researcher picks a smaller group, the sample, from the larger population so that results can be generalised. Methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen. Simple random sampling gives everyone an equal chance, like drawing names from a hat. Systematic sampling picks every kth person from a list after a random start: from a list of 1,000 students, choose a random number from 1 to 10, then take every 10th name after it. Stratified sampling splits the population into subgroups (strata) such as gender or school type, then samples randomly within each, so every subgroup is properly represented. In non-probability sampling, selection is not random. Snowball sampling, or chain referral, asks each participant to name others like them, which suits hidden groups such as drug users. For example, Priya, studying homeless people who inject drugs in Kolkata, finds three participants through a clinic, and each introduces her to more. Option d is right because snowball, systematic and stratified are all recognised sampling methods, while closure belongs to the psychology of perception. Exam trap: examiners slip in a familiar term from another chapter; do not confuse closure with cluster sampling, in which whole groups such as schools are selected at random.", "topic": "Types of sampling methods", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In WAIS-IV which of the following subtest belongs to the Perceptual Reasoning Index scale?", "options": {"a": "Digit span.", "b": "Visual puzzles.", "c": "Symbol search.", "d": "Letter number sequencing."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Visual Puzzles is a core subtest of the WAIS-IV Perceptual Reasoning Index. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, fourth edition (WAIS-IV, 2008), descends from David Wechsler's Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939) and gives a Full Scale IQ plus four index scores. Verbal Comprehension measures verbal reasoning and knowledge (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information; Comprehension supplemental). Perceptual Reasoning measures non-verbal, visual-spatial reasoning: its core subtests are Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles, with Figure Weights and Picture Completion supplemental. Working Memory measures holding and manipulating information in mind (Digit Span, Arithmetic; Letter-Number Sequencing supplemental). Processing Speed measures quick visual scanning and copying (Symbol Search, Coding; Cancellation supplemental). In Visual Puzzles the person looks at a completed design and must choose, within a time limit, the three pieces that together would make it, which means mentally rotating and fitting shapes without touching any blocks. For example, Aditi sees a square design and picks three of six fragments that would rebuild it; she is using spatial reasoning, not words or memory span. Visual Puzzles, Figure Weights and Cancellation were new subtests in the WAIS-IV. Option b is right because Visual Puzzles is one of the three core Perceptual Reasoning subtests. Exam trap: Digit Span and Letter-Number Sequencing feel like puzzles, but they belong to Working Memory, and Symbol Search, though visual, belongs to Processing Speed because it is scored for speed.", "topic": "WAIS-IV Perceptual Reasoning Index", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to John Bowlby what are the 4 types of attachment styles?", "options": {"a": "Secure, anxious-ambivalent, disorganized and avoidant.", "b": "Secure, insecure, anxious-avoidant and disorganized.", "c": "Secure, anxious-ambivalent, anxious-avoidant and disorganized.", "d": "Secure, insecure, approachable and avoidant."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the four attachment styles are secure, anxious-ambivalent, disorganised and avoidant. John Bowlby (1969), a British psychiatrist, gave the theory in Attachment: infants are born with an evolved need to stay close to a caregiver, who becomes a secure base for exploring and a safe haven in danger, and from early care the child builds an internal working model of self and others. The styles themselves come from Mary Ainsworth (1978), Bowlby's colleague, who measured them with the Strange Situation, reported with Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall in Patterns of Attachment. A 1-year-old goes through brief separations from and reunions with the mother, with a stranger present at times. Securely attached babies are upset by separation and comforted quickly at reunion. Avoidant (anxious-avoidant) babies show little distress and ignore the mother when she returns. Anxious-ambivalent (resistant) babies are very distressed and, at reunion, both seek and angrily resist contact. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) later added disorganised attachment: contradictory, confused behaviour such as freezing or approaching with the face turned away, often linked to frightening care. For example, when Tara's mother returns, Tara runs to her, settles within a minute and goes back to her blocks, which is secure attachment. Option a is right because it lists the four patterns, one secure and three insecure, without adding an invented type. Exam trap: \"insecure\" is the umbrella for the three non-secure patterns, not a fourth type, so any list that counts it beside the others is wrong. Key note: options a and c name the same four patterns, and c simply uses Ainsworth's formal label \"anxious-avoidant\"; a is the wording this question is normally answered with, so a stands.", "topic": "Four attachment styles", "fig": "John Bowlby"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a specific interpersonal problem area in IPT?", "options": {"a": "Interpersonal effectiveness.", "b": "Interpersonal deficits.", "c": "Interpersonal role disputes.", "d": "Role transition."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: interpersonal effectiveness is not an IPT problem area; it is a skills module of dialectical behaviour therapy. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984) in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression. Drawing on the interpersonal ideas of Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) and on attachment theory, it treats depression as an illness that both affects and is affected by current relationships, instead of digging into childhood conflict. It usually runs 12 to 16 weekly sessions. Early on, therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas to work on. Grief: complicated mourning after a death. Interpersonal role disputes: conflict with a spouse, parent, boss or friend whose expectations do not match the patient's. Role transitions: difficulty adapting to a life change such as marriage, retirement, migration or parenthood. Interpersonal deficits: social isolation and a lack of lasting relationships. For example, Sunita became depressed after moving from Patna to Bengaluru for her husband's job and losing her friends and her own work; the IPT focus would be role transition. Interpersonal effectiveness is one of the four skills modules of dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), which Marsha Linehan (1993) designed for borderline personality disorder: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, which teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect. Option a is right because interpersonal effectiveness is a DBT skill set, not one of IPT's four areas. Exam trap: the word \"interpersonal\" appears in both therapies, so the distractor sounds like IPT; learn the IPT four as grief, disputes, transitions and deficits.", "topic": "IPT problem areas", "fig": "Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman"}, {"q": "Parameter is characteristic of:", "options": {"a": "The sample.", "b": "The population.", "c": "An individual.", "d": "A test."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a parameter is a numerical characteristic of a population. The population is the complete set of people or observations a researcher is interested in, for example every Class 10 student in Kerala. A value that describes that whole set, such as their true mean mathematics score, is a parameter. Because populations are usually too large to measure, researchers draw a sample and calculate the same value in it; a value computed from a sample is a statistic. A simple memory aid: Population goes with Parameter, Sample goes with Statistic. Parameters are written with Greek letters: mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation and rho for the population correlation. Statistics use Roman letters: x-bar for the sample mean, s for the standard deviation and r for the correlation. Inferential statistics uses statistics to estimate parameters and to test hypotheses about them. For example, Rahul tests 400 of those students and finds a mean of 62; that 62 is a statistic, his estimate of the unknown parameter mu. Option b is right because a parameter, by definition, describes the population. Exam trap: the two words are often reversed; a sample value is a statistic, never a parameter, and a parameter is usually unknown and fixed, while a statistic varies from sample to sample.", "topic": "Parameter versus statistic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "For which psychiatric illness is ERP best suggested for?", "options": {"a": "Paranoid Schizophrenia.", "b": "OCD.", "c": "Substance Dependence.", "d": "Depression."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, obsessions (intrusive thoughts, images or urges, such as a fear of contamination) create anxiety, and compulsions (washing, checking, counting, mental rituals) bring brief relief. That relief negatively reinforces the ritual and stops the person from learning that the feared outcome would not happen, or that the anxiety would fade on its own. Victor Meyer (1966), working in London, treated hospitalised patients with OCD by exposing them to what they feared while preventing their rituals, and showed that the rituals could be reduced. Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986) later explained how it works through emotional processing theory, and Foa's group tested it in clinical trials. ERP has two parts. Exposure: the person faces the feared cue, in real life or in imagination, usually climbing a graded hierarchy from mildly to highly distressing items. Response prevention: the person then refrains from the compulsion. Anxiety rises, peaks and declines through habituation and extinction, and the person learns that the feared disaster does not follow. For example, Farhan, who washes his hands 50 times a day, touches a door handle and then waits without washing; across sessions his urge weakens. ERP is often combined with SSRIs such as fluoxetine, which have their own evidence in OCD. Option b is right because ERP was built for OCD and has its strongest evidence there. Exam trap: exposure alone is used for phobias, PTSD and panic, but the response prevention half, blocking the compulsion, is what ties ERP specifically to OCD.", "topic": "ERP for OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Id, according to Freud, is driven by aggressive and sexual instincts. This corresponds to Jung's archetype called:", "options": {"a": "Shadow.", "b": "Persona.", "c": "Evil force.", "d": "Sun."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Jung's shadow archetype is the closest counterpart to Freud's id. Carl Jung broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and built his own analytical psychology. He kept a personal unconscious of forgotten and repressed experience but added beneath it a collective unconscious, an inherited layer shared by all humans, whose contents are archetypes: universal patterns that recur in myths, dreams and art. The main archetypes are the persona, the shadow, the anima and animus, and the Self. The shadow is the dark, primitive, animal side of the personality: the selfish, aggressive and sexual impulses a person does not want to own. Jung traced it to our pre-human, animal past, which is why it best matches the id, which Freud described in The Ego and the Id (1923) as the reservoir of sexual and aggressive drives working on the pleasure principle. Unlike the id, though, the shadow also holds vitality, spontaneity and creativity, and Jung wanted it accepted and integrated, not merely controlled. We often meet our own shadow by projecting it: Vikram, who cannot admit his own jealousy, sees jealousy in all his colleagues and despises them for it. In stories the shadow appears as the villain, the devil or the monster. Option a is right because the shadow, the instinctual dark side, is the Jungian match for the id's sexual and aggressive instincts. Exam trap: the persona sounds important but is the opposite, the polished social mask we show the world; and \"evil force\" describes how the shadow may feel, but it is not the name of an archetype.", "topic": "Jung's shadow archetype", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "According to Beck's cognitive triad, one has negative views about:", "options": {"a": "Past, present and future.", "b": "Self, world and future.", "c": "Self, past and future.", "d": "Self, world and present."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a negative view of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Beck (1967), an American psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that the thoughts and dreams of his depressed patients kept returning to themes of defeat and loss. He built a cognitive model of depression and later a treatment manual, Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). The triad is the content of depressive thinking. The self is seen as worthless, inadequate or unlovable (\"I am a failure\"). The world, meaning other people and present experience, is seen as unfair, demanding or full of obstacles (\"Nobody cares; everything goes wrong\"). The future is seen as hopeless (\"It will never get better\"). These views surface as automatic negative thoughts, are rooted in dysfunctional schemas formed in childhood, and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction and magnification. The hopeless view of the future is the strongest link to suicide risk, which is why Beck also built a hopelessness scale. For example, Ishaan fails one interview and thinks, \"I am useless (self), companies only hire people with contacts (world), and I will be jobless forever (future)\". In therapy he learns to catch, test and replace these thoughts with more balanced ones. Option b is right because it names exactly the three targets of the triad. Exam trap: the distractors swap in time words; the triad has only one time element, the future, and neither the past nor the present is a corner of it.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "Kurt Lewin's approach to personality was a:", "options": {"a": "Gestalt approach.", "b": "Behavioural approach.", "c": "Social learning approach.", "d": "Deterministic approach."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Kurt Lewin's approach to personality was a Gestalt approach. Kurt Lewin (1935) trained and worked at the Psychological Institute of the University of Berlin, the centre of the Gestalt school, and carried its central idea, that the whole field determines each part, from perception into personality, motivation and social behaviour. His field theory, set out in A Dynamic Theory of Personality (1935) and Principles of Topological Psychology (1936), says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment together: B = f(P, E). Person and environment make up the life space, the total of all the facts that influence a person at a given moment, as that person sees them. Lewin drew it as a map, with regions (goals, activities), boundaries and barriers between them, and vectors (forces) pulling toward goals of positive valence and away from those of negative valence. Tension in a region of the person persists until the need is met; his student Bluma Zeigarnik (1927) found that unfinished tasks are remembered better than finished ones. As in Gestalt perception, behaviour is understood from the whole present situation, not from past habits or single elements. For example, whether Pooja studies tonight depends on the whole field at that moment: the pull of the coming exam, the barrier of tiredness and the counter-pull of a friend's wedding. Option a is right because Lewin's field theory applies Gestalt principles of wholes and dynamic fields to personality. Exam trap: Lewin is also called the father of modern social psychology (group dynamics, leadership styles, action research), so students pick social learning; but social learning theory belongs to John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941) and Albert Bandura (1977), and Lewin's stress on the present field sets him against stimulus-response behaviourism.", "topic": "Lewin's field theory", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "The phenomenon of development of Super-Ego, according to Freud is a consequence of:", "options": {"a": "Identification with the opposite sex parent.", "b": "Resolution of Oedipus/Electra complex.", "c": "Anatomical superiority.", "d": "Age progression."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: according to Sigmund Freud (1923), the superego develops as a consequence of resolving the Oedipus complex (the Electra complex in girls). In The Ego and the Id (1923) Freud divided the mind into three parts: the id (inborn drives, run by the pleasure principle), the ego (the realistic executive, run by the reality principle) and the superego (the moral arm). The superego is not present at birth. It forms in the phallic stage, at about 5 to 6, out of a family drama. The boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival; fearing punishment (castration anxiety), he gives up the wish and instead identifies with his father, taking the father's standards into himself. The girl, in Freud's account, turns to her father and resolves the conflict, less completely, by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) named the girl's version the Electra complex, a term Freud himself did not adopt. Through this identification, control that once came from outside becomes inner self-control, which is why Freud called the superego the heir of the Oedipus complex. It has two parts: the conscience, which punishes with guilt, and the ego ideal, which rewards with pride. Example: six-year-old Arjun no longer needs his father in the room to stop himself from taking extra sweets; a voice inside him now says it is wrong. Option b is right because Freud tied the birth of the superego directly to the resolution of the Oedipal conflict. Exam trap: option a sounds Freudian but reverses the theory; the child identifies with the same-sex parent, while the opposite-sex parent is the object of desire that has to be given up.", "topic": "Freud: origin of the superego", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "options": {"a": "Interference.", "b": "Decay.", "c": "Selective attention.", "d": "Retrieval inhibition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting, because information that was never attended was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Forgetting means losing access to information that was once encoded into memory. The main explanations are these. Decay (trace decay) theory says memory traces fade with time and disuse, as in Edward Thorndike's (1914) law of disuse. Interference theory says other learning disrupts recall; John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not the mere passing of time, causes most forgetting. In proactive interference old learning blocks new learning; in retroactive interference new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, or cue-dependent forgetting (Endel Tulving, 1974), means the memory is stored but the right cue is missing, as in the tip-of-the-tongue state. Retrieval inhibition means recalling some items actively suppresses related ones; Michael Anderson (1994), with Robert and Elizabeth Bjork, called this retrieval-induced forgetting. Motivated forgetting (repression) and failed consolidation complete the list. Selective attention, by contrast, works at the very start, at encoding: it decides what enters memory in the first place, as in Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model. Unattended material is never learned, so failing to recall it is a failure to encode, not forgetting. For example, if Riya was texting during a lecture and cannot recall its main point, she never encoded it; if she learned it well but cannot recall it a month later because of newer lectures on similar topics, that is interference. Option c is right because selective attention governs what gets encoded, not why stored information is later lost. Exam trap: retrieval inhibition sounds like an attention process, but it is a genuine forgetting mechanism that acts on memories already stored.", "topic": "Theories of forgetting", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "options": {"a": "Purpose.", "b": "Will.", "c": "Competence.", "d": "Wisdom."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1950), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson described eight psychosocial stages across the whole life span, each built around a crisis between two poles. In Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a favourable resolution leaves behind. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 12, the same period Sigmund Freud (1905) called latency. The child now meets tools, tasks, teachers and classmates to compare with. A child who finishes tasks, builds skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work. Repeated failure, harsh comparison or being labelled slow brings inferiority. The healthy outcome, competence, is the free use of skill and intelligence to complete tasks, not weighed down by a sense of inferiority. Learn the eight virtues in order: hope (trust versus mistrust), will (autonomy versus shame and doubt), purpose (initiative versus guilt), competence (industry versus inferiority), fidelity (identity versus role confusion), love (intimacy versus isolation), care (generativity versus stagnation) and wisdom (integrity versus despair). Example: nine-year-old Meera keeps failing at fractions until her teacher breaks them into small steps and praises each success; by the end of term she volunteers to solve problems at the board, showing the competence Erikson described. Option c is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: every option is a real Erikson virtue, so count the stages carefully; purpose belongs to stage 3, will to stage 2 and wisdom to stage 8.", "topic": "Erikson's stage 4 virtue", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Which view supports the contention that we are normally driven to eat by the anticipated pleasure of eating?", "options": {"a": "Set point theory.", "b": "Negative feedback system.", "c": "Positive incentive theory.", "d": "Homeostasis."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: positive-incentive theory holds that we are normally drawn to eat by the anticipated pleasure of eating. For most of the twentieth century, hunger was explained like a thermostat. In this set-point view, eating begins when energy stores fall below a fixed level and stops when they are restored, through negative feedback that keeps the body in homeostasis. Jean Mayer (1953) proposed the glucostatic version, with blood glucose as the regulated quantity, and Gordon Kennedy (1953) the lipostatic version, with body fat as the regulated quantity. The set-point view fits poorly with everyday eating: people usually eat long before any energy deficit exists, they keep eating when food is tasty or company is good, and it cannot explain why obesity has risen so fast where food is plentiful. Positive-incentive theory, argued by John Pinel and colleagues (2000) and taught in Pinel's textbook Biopsychology, reverses the direction. We are not pushed by an internal deficit but pulled by the expected pleasure (the positive-incentive value) of food, which depends on its taste, what we have learned about it, the time since we last ate, how much is already in the stomach and the social setting. Body weight then drifts to a settling point rather than being defended at a fixed set point. Example: Kabir has just finished lunch, yet when a colleague brings in hot jalebis he eats three, because the expected pleasure, not hunger, drives him. Option c is right because only positive-incentive theory makes anticipated pleasure the main cause of eating. Exam trap: the other three options are all parts of one deficit model; set point, negative feedback and homeostasis describe being pushed to eat, not pulled.", "topic": "Positive-incentive theory of hunger", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following theories predicts that work motivation will be low if an employee perceives that an increased effort will have little, or no effect on his/her performance?", "options": {"a": "Just-world theory.", "b": "Need-drive theory.", "c": "Expectancy theory.", "d": "Normative theory."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: expectancy theory predicts low work motivation when an employee believes that extra effort will make little or no difference to performance. Victor Vroom (1964), in Work and Motivation, proposed that people decide how much effort to put in by weighing three beliefs, which is why it is also called VIE theory. Expectancy is the belief that effort leads to performance (\"If I try harder, will I actually do better?\"). Instrumentality is the belief that performance leads to outcomes (\"If I do better, will I be rewarded?\"). Valence is how much the person values those outcomes. Vroom combined them by multiplication: motivational force equals expectancy times instrumentality times valence. Because they multiply, if any one of the three is close to zero, motivation collapses, however high the other two are. The stem describes near-zero expectancy: effort seems unrelated to performance. Lyman Porter and Edward Lawler (1968) later extended the model by adding ability, role clarity and satisfaction. Example: Arjun works in a call centre where a computer routes calls and largely decides his sales figures; since working harder does not change his numbers, his expectancy is near zero and he stops trying, even though the bonus is attractive. Managers raise expectancy through training, resources and clear, achievable goals. Option c is right because the effort-to-performance link is exactly what Vroom called expectancy. Exam trap: option d, normative theory, also carries Vroom's name, because the normative decision model of Victor Vroom and Philip Yetton (1973) tells leaders how far to involve subordinates in decisions; it is a leadership model, not a theory of work motivation.", "topic": "Vroom's expectancy theory", "fig": "Victor Vroom"}, {"q": "The defence of unconscious shifting of emotions from the real source of threat to a less threatening external object is usually known as:", "options": {"a": "Dissociation.", "b": "Isolation.", "c": "Conversion.", "d": "Displacement."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: displacement is the unconscious shifting of an emotion from its real, threatening source to a safer, less threatening target. Sigmund Freud (1894) described defence mechanisms as unconscious ways the ego protects itself from anxiety, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, set them out systematically. In displacement the feeling itself (often anger, sometimes desire or fear) stays the same; only its object changes. The real target is too dangerous to face, perhaps because it is more powerful, loved or needed, so the impulse is discharged on someone or something that cannot hit back. Freud also saw displacement at work in dreams, where emotional weight moves from important to trivial details, and in phobias, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of his father was displaced onto horses. The classic everyday illustration is the man who is shouted at by his boss and goes home to kick the dog. Example: Riya's manager humiliates her in a meeting; she says nothing, but at home she snaps at her younger brother over the television remote. Option d is right because the stem defines displacement precisely: the emotion moves from the real source of threat to a less threatening external object. Exam trap: projection is the near miss examiners like, but projection attributes your own unacceptable impulse to another person (\"he hates me\"), whereas displacement keeps the impulse as yours and redirects it (\"I shout at him instead\").", "topic": "Defence mechanism: displacement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "options": {"a": "Kernberg.", "b": "Kohut.", "c": "Malan.", "d": "Horowitz."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "topic": "Kohut's self psychology", "fig": "Heinz Kohut"}, {"q": "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy is a type of __________ therapy.", "options": {"a": "Reconstructive.", "b": "Re-educative.", "c": "Supportive.", "d": "Meta-cognitive."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the classic three-way grouping of psychotherapies by their aim, cognitive behaviour therapy is a re-educative therapy. Lewis Wolberg (1954), in The Technique of Psychotherapy, sorted therapies by how deep a change they seek. Supportive therapy aims to shore up the person's existing defences and restore everyday functioning, using reassurance, guidance, advice, persuasion and changes to the environment; it does not try to change personality. Re-educative therapy aims at deliberate change in attitudes, beliefs and behaviour, working mostly on conscious, present-day material through learning, practice and insight into current patterns. Behaviour therapy, cognitive therapy, counselling and many goal-focused approaches belong here. Reconstructive therapy aims to restructure the personality itself by bringing unconscious conflicts into awareness, as in psychoanalysis and long-term psychoanalytic psychotherapy. CBT fits the middle class neatly. Aaron Beck (1979) taught clients to spot and test their automatic thoughts, and behavioural tasks such as activity scheduling and graded exposure retrain habits. The aim is to learn new ways of thinking and acting, not to uncover childhood conflicts or merely prop up defences. Example: Kabir, 22, believes \"everyone will laugh if I speak in class\". His therapist helps him test the belief with small, planned speaking tasks; as the prediction fails, he relearns both the thought and the behaviour, which is re-education. Option b is right because CBT changes conscious thoughts and behaviour through structured learning, the defining feature of Wolberg's re-educative class. Exam trap: reconstructive sounds like cognitive restructuring, a core CBT technique, but in Wolberg's scheme reconstructive means deep personality change through psychoanalytic work.", "topic": "Wolberg's classes of psychotherapy", "fig": "Lewis Wolberg"}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on _________:", "options": {"a": "Perception.", "b": "Psychophysics.", "c": "Serial Position Effect.", "d": "Memory."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists to one perfect recitation and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer.", "topic": "Ebbinghaus and memory", "fig": "Hermann Ebbinghaus"}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "options": {"a": "Existential Therapy.", "b": "Person Centered Therapy.", "c": "Logo therapy.", "d": "Gestalt Therapy."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1946). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist and neurologist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, set out his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from the Greek logos, meaning) holds that the deepest human motive is the will to meaning, rather than pleasure or power. When the search for meaning is blocked, a person falls into an existential vacuum and may develop what Frankl called a noogenic neurosis. He gave logotherapy two signature techniques. Dereflection treats hyper-reflection, excessive self-observation that makes a problem worse, as in insomnia, sexual difficulties or anxious self-monitoring. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or another person, so the symptom fades for want of attention. Paradoxical intention asks the client to wish for, or try to produce, the feared symptom, which breaks the cycle of anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or unavoidable suffering. Example: Meera lies awake every night checking whether sleep is coming; her therapist suggests she use that time to plan the dance class she teaches, and sleep arrives once she stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: dereflection and paradoxical intention are both Frankl's, so neither should be credited to Gestalt or person-centred therapy, even though all three are humanistic or existential in flavour. Key note: logotherapy is itself one of the existential therapies, so option a is the near miss; but dereflection is a named technique of Frankl's logotherapy specifically, and when the specific school is offered it is the better answer, so c stands.", "topic": "Frankl's logotherapy: dereflection", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Consider a scenario where a salesperson knocks on someone's door and requests them to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his organisation. When he is turned down, he requests them to buy a portable ice box, and the customer feels obliged to comply. The technique that this salesperson has used is:", "options": {"a": "Low ball technique.", "b": "Foot-in-the-door technique.", "c": "Door- in- the- face technique.", "d": "Reciprocity."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the salesperson used the door-in-the-face technique, a large request that is refused, followed by the smaller request he really wanted. Robert Cialdini (1975) and colleagues demonstrated it with students approached on campus. When first asked to work as unpaid counsellors to juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years, almost all refused; when then asked to chaperone the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about half agreed, roughly three times the rate among students who heard only the small request. Two explanations are offered. Reciprocal concessions: the requester seems to have given ground, so the target feels obliged to give ground too. Perceptual contrast: after the big request, the small one looks modest. It works best when the same person makes both requests close together. Compare the other sequential techniques. Foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) runs the other way: a small request first, then a larger one, working through self-perception and consistency. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins a commitment first and then worsens the terms. In the stem the refrigerator is the big request, the portable ice box is the real target, and the customer \"feels obliged\", the mark of a reciprocal concession. Option c is right because the order is a refused large request followed by a smaller one, which defines door-in-the-face. Exam trap: students mix up the two door techniques; the foot goes in small and grows, while the door is slammed on the big request first. Key note: door-in-the-face works through reciprocal concessions, so reciprocity (d) is the principle behind it; but the named technique the scenario shows is door-in-the-face, and c stands.", "topic": "Compliance: door-in-the-face", "fig": "Robert Cialdini"}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "options": {"a": "Analogies.", "b": "Algorithm.", "c": "Metacognition.", "d": "Constructive process."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn better by catching and correcting errors, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John Flavell (1976) introduced the term, often glossed as \"thinking about thinking\", and his 1979 paper made it central to the study of cognitive development. It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what a person knows about their own mind, about tasks and about strategies, such as knowing that one remembers diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation (monitoring and control) is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, checking whether one has understood, and changing strategy when one has not. Its memory branch, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judgements of how well something has been learned. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success, which is why teaching students to self-test and explain material to themselves works. Example: Arjun notices he keeps missing psychophysics questions, realises that rereading his notes is not working, and switches to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition at work. Option c is right because the stem describes monitoring and correcting one's own thinking, which is the definition of metacognition. Exam trap: \"meta\" means one level above, so metacognition is cognition about cognition; it is not general intelligence, and it is not a problem-solving method such as an algorithm or an analogy, which are tools used by thought rather than awareness of thought.", "topic": "Metacognition", "fig": "John Flavell"}, {"q": "The famous textbook \"The Principles of Psychology\" was authored by:", "options": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt.", "b": "William James.", "c": "Edward B. Titchener.", "d": "Alan Carr."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). James, who trained in medicine at Harvard before turning to philosophy and psychology, worked on the book for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). The Principles described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness, called habit the \"enormous fly-wheel of society\", separated the self as knower (the \"I\") from the self as known (the \"me\"), and set out James's theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885): we feel afraid because we tremble, not the other way round. The book made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration for functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option b is right because James is the author of The Principles of Psychology. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (1873 to 1874) wrote the similar-sounding Principles of Physiological Psychology, the founding text of experimental psychology, so option a is the near miss built on a look-alike title.", "topic": "William James: The Principles", "fig": "William James"}, {"q": "Ivan Petrovich Pavlov received the Nobel Prize in 1904 for:", "options": {"a": "Contribution to Psychology.", "b": "Conditioned reflexes.", "c": "Physiology of digestion.", "d": "Experiment with animals."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Ivan Pavlov (1904) received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1904 for his work on the physiology of digestion. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, spent the 1880s and 1890s studying how the digestive glands (the salivary glands, stomach and pancreas) are controlled by the nervous system. He developed surgical preparations, such as fistulas and a small isolated pouch of the stomach, that let him collect secretions from healthy, conscious dogs over long periods instead of from anaesthetised animals. He was the first Russian to win a Nobel Prize. During this work he noticed that dogs began to salivate before food reached the mouth, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant's footsteps. He called these \"psychic secretions\", and his study of them grew into the theory of conditioned reflexes, the foundation of classical conditioning, published in English as Conditioned Reflexes (1927). Option c is right because the Nobel award honoured his digestion research, not his conditioning work. Exam trap: option b is the famous trap; conditioned reflexes made Pavlov famous in psychology, but that work came after the prize, which was given for digestion.", "topic": "Pavlov's Nobel Prize", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "options": {"a": "A.T Beck.", "b": "E.R Guthrie.", "c": "B.F Skinner.", "d": "C.L Hull."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "topic": "Behaviourists versus Beck", "fig": "Aaron T. Beck"}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "options": {"a": "Dollard-Miller.", "b": "Max Wertheimer.", "c": "Horney.", "d": "Alfred Adler."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are the three neurotic trends of Karen Horney (1945). Horney, a German-born psychoanalyst who moved to the United States, argued in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time (1937) that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships and culture rather than by sexual instincts. A child whose parents are cold, inconsistent, dominating or rejecting feels basic hostility, represses it out of fear or dependence, and is left with basic anxiety, the feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child develops strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, listed in Self-Analysis (1942), such as needs for affection and approval, a partner to take over one's life, power, prestige, admiration, achievement, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval. The aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and control. The detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and privacy. A healthy person uses all three flexibly as situations demand; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among the trends is the core of neurosis. Example: Riya agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), her cousin Kabir turns every chat into a contest (against), and his brother Arjun avoids company altogether (away). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Alfred Adler (1912) also built a social theory of neurosis around inferiority feelings and the striving to overcome them, and later sorted people into ruling, getting, avoiding and socially useful types, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "topic": "Horney's three neurotic trends", "fig": "Karen Horney"}, {"q": "The need to submit passively to others, to seek and accept injury, blame and criticism is also known as the need for:", "options": {"a": "Abasement.", "b": "Succorance.", "c": "Order.", "d": "Defence."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the need to submit passively to others and to seek or accept injury, blame and criticism is n Abasement in the list of Henry Murray (1938). In Explorations in Personality (1938), written with colleagues at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, Murray proposed that behaviour is driven by needs interacting with press, the forces in the environment that help or block them. He separated viscerogenic (bodily) needs, such as food and water, from psychogenic (psychological) needs (textbooks usually list about twenty), each written with an \"n\", such as n Achievement, n Affiliation, n Dominance, n Autonomy, n Nurturance and n Exhibition. Each need comes with a short behavioural definition, and the stem paraphrases Murray's definition of abasement: to submit passively to external force, to accept injury, blame, criticism and punishment, to surrender, to admit inferiority and error, and to blame or belittle oneself. Murray's needs are measured by the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), in which stories told about ambiguous pictures reveal needs and press, and by the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (Allen Edwards, 1959), a forced-choice inventory of fifteen of Murray's needs. Example: Meera apologises even when a colleague made the mistake and accepts unfair blame without protest, a pattern of high n Abasement. Option a is right because the stem is the textbook definition of abasement. Exam trap: succorance sounds as if it involves suffering, but it means seeking help, sympathy and care, not seeking blame.", "topic": "Murray's needs: abasement", "fig": "Henry Murray"}, {"q": "'Animistic thinking', or the tendency to ascribe life to non-living things, is found in the ______ stage of Piaget's development:", "options": {"a": "Preoperational stage.", "b": "Concrete operational stage.", "c": "Formal operational stage.", "d": "Sensorimotor stage."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: animistic thinking, the belief that non-living things are alive and have feelings and intentions, belongs to the preoperational stage of Jean Piaget (1929). Piaget described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (about 11 or 12 onwards). In The Child's Conception of the World (1929) he reported interviews in which young children said that the sun follows them, that the wind knows it is blowing and that a table feels hurt when bumped. He traced animism to egocentrism: the child assumes everything works the way he or she does, so things that move or seem active must be alive and have purposes. Animism shrinks in steps, from crediting life to anything active or useful, to anything that moves, to things that move by themselves, and finally to plants and animals only. It sits alongside other preoperational features: artificialism (the belief that natural things such as lakes and mountains were made by people), egocentrism, centration and failure of conservation. Example: four-year-old Kabir scolds the chair he tripped over, saying \"Bad chair, you hurt me!\", and tells his mother that the moon is following the car. Option a is right because animism is a hallmark of preoperational thought. Exam trap: the sensorimotor stage (d) comes just before, but infants in it do not yet have symbolic thought, so they cannot hold beliefs about whether objects are alive; animism needs the mental representation that only begins at about 2.", "topic": "Piaget: animism, preoperational stage", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is not considered as a form of third wave therapies?", "options": {"a": "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy.", "b": "Mindfulness Based Interventions.", "c": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy.", "d": "Behaviour Therapy."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: classic behaviour therapy is the first wave, not a third-wave therapy. Steven Hayes (2004) described the history of behavioural and cognitive treatment as three waves. The first wave, behaviour therapy, took off in the 1950s and 1960s and applied learning principles directly to observable behaviour: systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958), exposure, and operant methods such as token economies built on the work of B. F. Skinner (1953). The second wave, cognitive therapy, added thoughts as targets in the 1960s and 1970s: Albert Ellis (1962) and Aaron Beck (1979) taught clients to identify and dispute irrational or distorted beliefs, and the merger of the two waves became CBT. The third wave, from the 1990s, changes the person's relationship to thoughts and feelings rather than their content, using mindfulness, acceptance, values and dialectics. Its members include acceptance and commitment therapy (Steven Hayes, 1999), dialectical behaviour therapy (Marsha Linehan, 1993), mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002) and other mindfulness-based interventions such as mindfulness-based stress reduction (Jon Kabat-Zinn, 1979). Example: when Riya thinks \"I am a failure\", a second-wave therapist asks her to examine the evidence, while an ACT therapist asks her to notice it as just a thought and act on her values anyway. Option d is right because behaviour therapy is the original first wave, the base from which the later waves grew. Exam trap: DBT keeps \"behaviour therapy\" in its name, so students sometimes pick it, but it is a core third-wave therapy, while plain behaviour therapy is the one that does not belong.", "topic": "Third-wave therapies", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The famous book \"Feminine Psychology\" is credited to the author:", "options": {"a": "Erik Erikson.", "b": "Karen Horney.", "c": "Anna Freud.", "d": "Melanie Klein."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Feminine Psychology is a book by Karen Horney (1967), published after her death. Harold Kelman (1967) edited it from the papers on women that Horney wrote between 1922 and 1937, while she was a psychoanalyst first in Berlin and then in the United States. In those papers she challenged the account of female development given by Sigmund Freud (1925). She argued that penis envy was a theory seen through male eyes and drawn from neurotic patients, and that women's feelings of inferiority came from culture, such as economic dependence and narrow social roles, not from anatomy. She turned the argument round with womb envy: men may envy women's power to bear children and make up for it through achievement. Essays such as \"The Flight from Womanhood\" (1926) and \"The Overvaluation of Love\" (1934) are collected in the book. Option b is right because Feminine Psychology gathers Horney's essays on the psychology of women. Exam trap: all four options are famous psychoanalytic authors with famous books, so match each to its own title: Anna Freud's classic is on defences, Melanie Klein's on child analysis and Erik Erikson's on the life cycle.", "topic": "Horney's Feminine Psychology", "fig": "Karen Horney"}, {"q": "A therapist suggests to a patient that each time he begins to feel anxious he should breathe deeply and imagine himself in a relaxing place. What principle could underlie this suggestion?", "options": {"a": "Extinction.", "b": "Flooding.", "c": "Reciprocal Inhibition.", "d": "Differential reinforcement."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: pairing deep breathing and a relaxing image with the first signs of anxiety rests on reciprocal inhibition. Joseph Wolpe (1958), a South African psychiatrist, set out the principle in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition: if a response incompatible with anxiety is made to occur in the presence of anxiety-provoking cues, it weakens the bond between those cues and anxiety. A person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment, because calm (parasympathetic) and fear (sympathetic) states work against each other. Wolpe borrowed the term from the physiologist Charles Sherrington (1906), who showed that when one muscle in a reflex contracts, its opposing muscle is inhibited. His main competing response was relaxation, drawn from the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1929); assertive and sexual responses could also serve. The principle underlies systematic desensitisation: the client learns relaxation, builds an anxiety hierarchy from the least to the most feared situation, and meets each step while relaxed until it no longer brings anxiety. Mary Cover Jones (1924) had used a similar idea when she fed little Peter while bringing a rabbit gradually closer. Example: Arjun panics before presentations; each time his heart starts to race, he breathes slowly and pictures a quiet beach in Goa, and over weeks the presentation loses its hold on him. Option c is right because the therapist is pairing a calm response that is incompatible with anxiety, so that one inhibits the other. Exam trap: flooding also targets anxiety, but it uses prolonged, full-intensity exposure with no relaxation, letting fear die out through extinction; reciprocal inhibition needs a competing response.", "topic": "Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "If a child is brought to you for assessment and his/her social quotient or SQ happens to be 43 on Vineland Social Maturity Scale, then according to The Gazette of India, what will be the disability calculation?", "options": {"a": "25%.", "b": "50%.", "c": "75%.", "d": "90%."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a social quotient of 43 falls in the moderate range of intellectual disability, which the Gazette of India guidelines rate as 75% disability. The Vineland Social Maturity Scale was devised by Edgar Doll (1935) at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey to measure social competence, the everyday ability to look after oneself and take part in community life. It was adapted for India by A. J. Malin at Nagpur as the VSMS: 89 items for ages 0 to 15 in eight areas (self-help general, self-help eating, self-help dressing, self-direction, occupation, communication, locomotion and socialisation). An informant, usually a parent, reports what the child habitually does. The items passed give a social age, and the social quotient is SQ = social age divided by chronological age, times 100, read like an IQ. Example: seven-year-old Kabir has a social age of 3 years; 3 divided by 7, times 100, gives an SQ of about 43. In India a medical board that includes a clinical psychologist certifies intellectual disability, using IQ from a standard test and SQ from the VSMS; the severity band is then converted to a percentage. Moderate intellectual disability carries 75%, well above the 40% that makes a benchmark disability under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016. Option c is right because an SQ of 43 sits in the moderate band, rated 75%. Exam trap: do not turn the SQ itself into the percentage; 43 does not mean 43%; the SQ first places the child in a band, and the band carries the percentage. Key note: the Gazette guidelines were revised in January 2018 under the RPWD Act 2016 to use SQ bands, with moderate at SQ 36 to 54, while the older 2001 bands put moderate at 35 to 49; an SQ of 43 is moderate under both and is given 75%, so c stands.", "topic": "VSMS SQ and disability percentage", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one is an immature defence?", "options": {"a": "Distortion.", "b": "Rationalization.", "c": "Altruism.", "d": "Acting out."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: acting out is an immature defence. A defence mechanism is an unconscious process by which the ego shields itself from anxiety, guilt or painful feelings; Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, catalogued the defences. George Vaillant (1977), who followed the Harvard men of the Grant Study in Adaptation to Life, ranked defences by maturity in four levels. Psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Neurotic: intellectualisation, isolation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Mature: altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation, suppression. The grouping in Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry is close to this; it calls the lowest level narcissistic and places rationalisation among the neurotic defences. George Vaillant found that men who relied on mature defences fared better in health, work and marriage. In acting out, an unconscious wish or conflict is expressed directly in action, so the person never has to feel or think about it; Sigmund Freud (1914) used the term for patients who repeat an old conflict in behaviour instead of remembering it. Immature defences are normal in adolescence and common in adults with personality disorders. Example: Kabir, 16, is hurt by his father's criticism but cannot admit it, so he slams doors, skips school and rides his bike recklessly; the feeling is discharged in behaviour and never put into words. Option d is right because acting out sits on the immature level of George Vaillant's hierarchy. Exam trap: distortion sounds worse than acting out and may be picked as 'immature', but it is lower still, a psychotic (narcissistic) defence that reshapes reality; rationalisation sounds like lying, yet it is neurotic, and altruism is mature.", "topic": "Vaillant's hierarchy of defences", "fig": "George Vaillant"}, {"q": "Pica is:", "options": {"a": "Pathological over eating.", "b": "Unrealistic fear of eating.", "c": "Craving non-food substance.", "d": "Food refusal."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: pica is the persistent craving for, and eating of, non-food substances. The name is Latin for magpie, a bird said to eat almost anything. In DSM-5 (2013) pica is a feeding and eating disorder: the persistent eating of non-nutritive, non-food substances, such as chalk, soil, paper, hair or paint, for at least 1 month. The eating must be inappropriate to the person's developmental level (mouthing objects is normal in infants, so it is not diagnosed before about age 2) and not part of a culturally supported practice. It is linked with iron or zinc deficiency, pregnancy, intellectual disability and autism, and it can cause lead poisoning or bowel blockage. Example: Meera, 7, who has iron-deficiency anaemia, has been eating chalk and plaster scraped off the walls for months. Option c is right because pica is defined by craving and eating things that are not food. Exam trap: each distractor is a real eating problem with another name. Overeating points to binge-eating disorder or bulimia nervosa, fear of eating to a phobia or to avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID), and food refusal to anorexia nervosa or ARFID. Do not confuse pica with rumination disorder, in which swallowed food is brought back up and re-chewed.", "topic": "Pica: eating non-food substances", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Pseudocyesis is:", "options": {"a": "Malingering.", "b": "Pseudo pregnancy.", "c": "Memory loss.", "d": "Delusion of guilt."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: pseudocyesis is pseudo pregnancy, that is, false pregnancy. The word comes from the Greek pseudes, false, and kyesis, pregnancy. A woman firmly believes she is pregnant and her body shows real signs of pregnancy: missed or scanty periods, a swollen abdomen, breast changes, morning sickness, weight gain and even felt foetal movements, yet there is no foetus. Hormonal changes can accompany it, a striking case of mind acting on body. DSM-5 (2013) lists pseudocyesis as an example of other specified somatic symptom and related disorder; DSM-IV had placed it under somatoform disorder not otherwise specified. It is linked with an intense wish for, or fear of, pregnancy, with infertility, a recent miscarriage and depression. An ultrasound scan settles the diagnosis, and care combines a sensitive explanation with psychological support. Example: Priya, 32, has tried for a child for six years; her periods stop, her abdomen swells and she says she feels kicks, but the scan shows an empty uterus. Option b is right because pseudocyesis means a false pregnancy with bodily signs. Exam trap: 'pseudo' tempts students towards malingering, but nothing here is faked on purpose; the woman truly believes, and her bodily changes are real. Separate it from a delusion of pregnancy in psychosis, which is held without the bodily signs, and from couvade syndrome, in which an expectant father develops pregnancy-like symptoms.", "topic": "Pseudocyesis (false pregnancy)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Waxy flexibility is:", "options": {"a": "Disorder of memory.", "b": "Disorder of Emotion.", "c": "Disorder of Motor behaviour.", "d": "Disorder of Consciousness."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: waxy flexibility is a disorder of motor behaviour. It is the English name for the Latin cerea flexibilitas. When the examiner moves the patient's arm, there is a slight, even resistance, like bending a soft wax candle, and the limb then stays in whatever position it has been placed, sometimes for minutes or hours. It is a sign of catatonia, the syndrome of disturbed movement first described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874). DSM-5 (2013) lists 12 catatonic signs and needs 3 or more: stupor, catalepsy, waxy flexibility, mutism, negativism, posturing, mannerism, stereotypy, agitation, grimacing, echolalia and echopraxia. Catatonia is a specifier that can attach to schizophrenia, depression, bipolar disorder and medical conditions, and it usually responds to lorazepam or electroconvulsive therapy. Catalepsy is holding a passively induced posture against gravity; waxy flexibility adds the feel of that slight resistance as the limb is moved. Example: Arjun, 24, admitted with catatonic schizophrenia, sits motionless; when the doctor raises his arm above his head and lets go, it stays there for ten minutes. Option c is right because waxy flexibility concerns posture and movement, so descriptive psychopathology files it under disorders of motor behaviour. Exam trap: catalepsy and cataplexy look alike. Cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by laughter or strong emotion, in narcolepsy, with full consciousness; catalepsy, with its waxy flexibility, belongs to catatonia.", "topic": "Waxy flexibility in catatonia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Intermittent Explosive Disorder is a:", "options": {"a": "Personality disorder.", "b": "Impulse control disorder.", "c": "Mood disorder.", "d": "Thought disorder."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: intermittent explosive disorder (IED) is an impulse-control disorder. Impulse-control disorders are failures to resist an impulse or urge to do something harmful; tension or arousal often builds before the act and relief follows, sometimes with regret afterwards. In IED the impulse is aggressive. DSM-5 (2013) describes recurrent outbursts, verbal (tirades, arguments, tantrums) or physical (assaults on people, animals or property), grossly out of proportion to the provocation. They occur either about twice a week for 3 months without damage or injury, or three times within 12 months with damage or injury. The outbursts are unplanned, not aimed at a goal such as money or intimidation, cause distress or impairment, occur in a person at least 6 years old, and are not better explained by another disorder. DSM-IV (1994) placed IED among the 'impulse-control disorders not elsewhere classified', with kleptomania, pyromania, pathological gambling and trichotillomania. DSM-5 moved it into the new chapter 'disruptive, impulse-control and conduct disorders', beside oppositional defiant disorder, conduct disorder, pyromania and kleptomania, so it remains an impulse-control disorder. Example: Rohan, 28, punches a wall and smashes his phone when someone takes his parking spot, several times a month, and afterwards feels ashamed of how little it took. Option b is right because IED is defined by a recurrent failure to control aggressive impulses. Exam trap: anger outbursts also occur in borderline and antisocial personality disorder, in mania and in disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (which DSM-5 places among the depressive disorders); IED is diagnosed only when such a disorder does not explain the outbursts, so options a and c are look-alikes, not the class.", "topic": "Intermittent explosive disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Empathy is:", "options": {"a": "Feeling against.", "b": "Feeling for.", "c": "Feeling with.", "d": "Lack of feeling."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: empathy is feeling with another person. It means sensing another person's feelings and meanings from inside their own frame of reference, as though standing in their shoes, while knowing that the feelings are theirs and not yours. The word was coined by Edward Titchener (1909) to translate Einfuhlung, 'feeling into', a term popularised by the German philosopher Theodor Lipps (1903). Carl Rogers (1957) made accurate empathic understanding one of the core conditions of therapeutic change, beside unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). He described it as sensing the client's private world 'as if it were your own, but without ever losing the \"as if\" quality'. Modern research separates affective empathy (sharing the feeling) from cognitive empathy (understanding what the other thinks and feels, or perspective taking); Mark Davis (1980) measured both with his Interpersonal Reactivity Index. Example: Meera tells her counsellor that she feels guilty for studying in Delhi while her father is ill at home. A sympathetic reply is 'Poor you, that is so sad.' An empathic reply is 'You feel torn, wanting to build your future here and to be with him there, and guilty whichever you choose.' The first stands outside her experience; the second enters it. Option c is right because empathy is defined as feeling with, not feeling for. Exam trap: all four words share the Greek pathos, feeling, and the roots can mislead: sympathy literally means 'feeling together', yet psychology teaches it as feeling for (concern or pity from outside), while empathy is feeling with. Antipathy is feeling against, and apathy is lack of feeling.", "topic": "Empathy versus sympathy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Hawthorne effect is:", "options": {"a": "Modification of behaviour under observation.", "b": "Obedience.", "c": "Modification of behaviour for better attention.", "d": "Defiance towards given instructions."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Hawthorne effect is the modification of behaviour under observation. People change how they act simply because they know they are being watched or studied, whatever the intervention itself. The name comes from the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago, where studies of worker output ran from 1924 to 1932. In the illumination experiments output rose when the lighting was increased, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed. In the relay assembly test room, a small group of women raised their output across many changes in rest pauses and working hours. The studies are associated with Elton Mayo (1933) and were reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson in Management and the Worker (1939); Henry Landsberger (1958) coined the term. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend, but the lesson stuck: an improvement may come from being studied rather than from the treatment, a threat to internal validity. Researchers guard against it with control groups that receive equal attention, unobtrusive measures and time for participants to get used to observers. Related artefacts are demand characteristics (Martin Orne, 1962) and the John Henry effect, in which a control group works harder to compete. Example: on the day an inspector sits at the back of Kabir's classroom, students who usually chat sit up straight and answer questions; nothing has changed except that they are being watched. Option a is right because the Hawthorne effect is defined by change caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: option c also mentions modification of behaviour and attention, echoing the popular explanation that the workers responded to the researchers' interest; but the effect is not behaviour aimed at winning attention, which is attention-seeking.", "topic": "Hawthorne effect", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychiatric medicine can be prescribed by:", "options": {"a": "Social workers.", "b": "Psychiatric social workers.", "c": "Clinical psychologists.", "d": "Psychiatrists."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychiatric medicine can be prescribed by psychiatrists. Antidepressants, antipsychotics, mood stabilisers such as lithium and anti-anxiety drugs are prescription medicines, and in India only a registered medical practitioner may prescribe them. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor (MBBS) who has specialised in psychiatry (MD, DNB or DPM), so he or she can diagnose, order investigations, prescribe drugs and give electroconvulsive therapy. A clinical psychologist completes postgraduate clinical training recognised by the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) and must be registered with it under the RCI Act of 1992. The clinical psychologist carries out psychological assessment, psychodiagnosis and psychotherapy, but does not prescribe. A psychiatric social worker works with the family, social support, rehabilitation and community follow-up. The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 counts all of these as mental health professionals, yet prescribing stays with the medical members of the team. Example: at a hospital clinic, Arjun, 22, with severe depression, is started on an antidepressant by the psychiatrist, given cognitive behaviour therapy by the clinical psychologist, and helped back to college by the psychiatric social worker, who also meets his parents. Option d is right because psychiatrists are the only medically qualified professionals among the options. Exam trap: the word 'psychiatric' in 'psychiatric social worker' does not give prescribing rights. As a side note, a few American states (New Mexico from 2002, Louisiana from 2004) let psychologists with extra training in psychopharmacology prescribe, but this does not apply in India and is not the standard answer.", "topic": "Who can prescribe psychiatric drugs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Median is:", "options": {"a": "The representative score.", "b": "The middle score.", "c": "The 1st quartile score.", "d": "The most frequent score."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the median is the middle score. When all the scores are arranged in order, the median is the point that divides the distribution into two equal halves, with 50 percent of scores below it and 50 percent above. So it is also the 50th percentile, the second quartile (Q2) and the fifth decile. With an odd number of scores it is the middle one; with an even number it is the mean of the two middle scores. Example: Riya's five test marks are 4, 7, 8, 10 and 25. The median is 8, while the mean is 10.8, pulled up by the single extreme score of 25. Because the median ignores how far extreme scores lie, it is preferred for skewed data such as income and for ordinal data. In a positively skewed distribution the mean is larger than the median, which is larger than the mode. Option b is right because 'middle' is the defining word for the median. Exam trap: the 'representative score' usually describes the mean, and the 'most frequent score' is the mode; the first quartile is the 25th percentile, not the 50th.", "topic": "Median as the middle score", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following names is usually not connected with Existential therapy?", "options": {"a": "Viktor Frankl.", "b": "Rollo May.", "c": "Walter Mischel.", "d": "Irvin Yalom."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Walter Mischel is not connected with existential therapy. Walter Mischel (1968) was a social-cognitive personality psychologist. In Personality and Assessment he argued that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly, with correlations rarely above about .30, and so started the person-situation debate. He is also known for the delay of gratification ('marshmallow') studies with preschool children at Stanford and, with Yuichi Shoda (1995), for the cognitive-affective personality system, which explains personality as stable if-then patterns of response to situations. Existential therapy comes from philosophy: Soren Kierkegaard (1844), Martin Heidegger (1927) and Jean-Paul Sartre (1943). It treats anxiety as part of being human and helps a person face the givens of life and choose how to live. Its leading names include Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy rests on the will to meaning; Rollo May (1958), who co-edited Existence and brought European existential psychiatry to America; and Irvin Yalom (1980), whose Existential Psychotherapy named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Example: after his father's sudden death, Kabir feels that nothing matters. An existential therapist would help him face mortality and his freedom to choose what gives his life meaning; an assessment in Walter Mischel's style would instead map how his behaviour shifts from one specific situation to another. Option c is right because Walter Mischel's work sits in social-cognitive personality theory, while the other three are standard names in existential therapy. Exam trap: Walter Mischel's stress on 'the situation' can sound like the existential idea of being-in-the-world, but his situations are learning contexts measured as if-then patterns, a social-cognitive idea, not a philosophy of freedom and meaning.", "topic": "Existential therapists versus Mischel", "fig": "Walter Mischel"}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "options": {"a": "Melanie Klein.", "b": "Otto Kernberg.", "c": "Heinz Kohut.", "d": "Margaret Mahler."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, joined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations ideas to ego psychology, and in Borderline Conditions and Pathological Narcissism (1975) he described three levels of personality organisation. Each level is judged on three features: identity, the level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (a shifting, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting, with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation and denial, and reality testing that is largely kept but may slip under stress. Psychotic organisation adds a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder; it also covers narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and he later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Ananya calls her new roommate the kindest person she has ever met, then, after one forgotten favour, calls her selfish and fake, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option b is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Melanie Klein first described splitting, Heinz Kohut (1971) also wrote on narcissism in the 1970s, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is used to explain borderline problems, so all four names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's.", "topic": "Borderline personality organisation", "fig": "Otto Kernberg"}, {"q": "______ is not an example of a non-parametric test:", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U Test.", "b": "Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test.", "c": "Friedman test.", "d": "Independent T test."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the independent t test is not a non-parametric test; it is parametric. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as means and variances and assumes interval or ratio scores, a roughly normal population, similar variances in the groups and independent observations; t tests, ANOVA and Pearson's r are examples. A non-parametric (distribution-free) test makes few assumptions about the population's shape and works on ranks, signs or frequencies, so it suits ordinal data, small samples and badly skewed scores. The independent t test, from William Sealy Gosset (1908), who published as 'Student', compares the means of two separate groups. Each common parametric test has a non-parametric partner: the independent t test pairs with the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947); the paired t test with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945); one-way ANOVA with the Kruskal-Wallis test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); and repeated-measures ANOVA with the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937). When the parametric assumptions hold, the parametric test is preferred because it has more power. Example: Riya compares the anxiety scores of 40 hostel students and 40 day scholars; the scores are interval and roughly normal, so she uses an independent t test. If a teacher had only ranked 8 students in each group, she would use the Mann-Whitney U test. Option d is right because the t test assumes normally distributed interval data and compares means. Exam trap: Frank Wilcoxon's name is on two tests, the signed-rank test for paired data and the rank-sum test for two independent groups (equivalent to the Mann-Whitney U), so check the design, not just the name.", "topic": "Parametric versus non-parametric tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sundowning or Sundown Syndrome is a phenomenon commonly associated with:", "options": {"a": "Depression.", "b": "Alcoholism.", "c": "Dementia.", "d": "Schizophrenia."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: sundowning is a phenomenon commonly associated with dementia. Sundowning, or sundown syndrome, is a pattern in which confusion, restlessness, agitation, anxiety, pacing, wandering and sometimes shouting or aggression increase in the late afternoon, evening and night. It is seen most often in the middle and later stages of Alzheimer's disease, the commonest cause of dementia, and in other dementias; the same evening worsening is also typical of delirium, which often complicates dementia. Several causes probably combine. Damage to the brain's body clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, disrupts circadian rhythms and the sleep-wake cycle. Fading light and shadows make the surroundings harder to interpret, tiredness builds through the day, and there are fewer orienting cues such as visitors and routine. Hunger, pain, a full bladder, infections and some medicines can add to it. Management is mostly practical: bright light and activity in the daytime, a fixed routine, less caffeine and fewer daytime naps, well-lit rooms in the evening, a calm setting and checks for pain or infection. Example: Kabir's 80-year-old grandfather, who has Alzheimer's disease, is calm and chatty in the morning, but as dusk falls he insists he must leave for his office and tries to open the gate. Option c is right because sundowning is a recognised feature of Alzheimer's disease and the other dementias. Exam trap: depression also shows a daily rhythm, but in the opposite direction: melancholic depression is typically worst in the morning and lifts towards evening (diurnal variation of mood), so a disturbance that peaks in the evening points to dementia or delirium, not depression.", "topic": "Sundowning in dementia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not based on operant conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Contingency Management.", "b": "Implosion.", "c": "Token economy.", "d": "Premack principle."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: implosion is not based on operant conditioning; it rests on classical conditioning and extinction. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour through its consequences: reinforcement makes a response more likely, punishment less likely. Implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) instead targets a conditioned fear. The client imagines the most feared scenes at full, exaggerated intensity, often enriched with horrific or psychodynamic themes, for a long session with no escape. Its logic comes from O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory: fear is learned by classical conditioning, and avoidance keeps it alive. When the feared cue (the conditioned stimulus) is met again and again without the dreaded outcome (the unconditioned stimulus), the fear extinguishes. Flooding applies the same idea, usually in real life; implosion is flooding in imagination, and it is rarely used now. Example: Arjun, terrified of snakes, is asked to picture for an hour snakes coiling round him and biting, while the therapist keeps the scenes vivid and blocks every escape; his fear peaks and then falls. The other three are operant. Contingency management gives reinforcers, such as vouchers, for a verified target behaviour, such as drug-free urine samples. A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives tokens for target behaviours that are exchanged for back-up rewards. The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) says a more probable activity can reinforce a less probable one. Option b is right because implosion works by extinguishing a classically conditioned fear, not by arranging consequences. Exam trap: all four are behaviour therapy techniques, so 'behavioural' does not decide the answer; ask whether the method arranges consequences (operant) or pairs and unpairs stimuli (classical).", "topic": "Implosion versus operant techniques", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Regarding Ganser's syndrome, which of the following is true?", "options": {"a": "Repeated lying.", "b": "Approximate answers.", "c": "Unconscious episodes.", "d": "Feigning illness."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the hallmark of Ganser syndrome is approximate answers. Sigbert Ganser (1898) described the syndrome in prisoners awaiting trial. The patient gives answers that are wrong but close to the right one, which shows that the question has been understood: asked how many legs a cow has, he says 'five'; asked what 2 plus 2 makes, he says '5'. German psychiatry called this vorbeireden, 'talking past the point'. With it come clouding of consciousness, pseudohallucinations and conversion symptoms, and when the episode ends, often suddenly, the person has amnesia for it. ICD-10 lists Ganser syndrome among the other dissociative (conversion) disorders, and DSM-IV gave it as an example of dissociative disorder not otherwise specified. It is rare and appears mostly under severe stress, such as detention. It differs from dementia, where errors come from real and lasting cognitive loss, and from depressive pseudodementia, where the person answers 'I don't know' rather than giving near misses. Example: Ravi, held in custody before his trial, is asked the colour of grass and says 'blue', and asked how many fingers he has says 'eleven'; days later he remembers nothing of the interview. Option b is right because approximate answers are the defining sign named after Sigbert Ganser. Exam trap: because the patients were prisoners with a reason to seem unwell, a long debate asked whether the syndrome was simply malingering, which is why 'feigning illness' is offered; the classic view treats it as a dissociative state in which the person is not consciously faking.", "topic": "Ganser syndrome", "fig": "Sigbert Ganser"}, {"q": "A type II error occurs:", "options": {"a": "Whenever a true null hypothesis is accepted.", "b": "Whenever a false null hypothesis is rejected.", "c": "Whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted.", "d": "Whenever a true null hypothesis is rejected."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a Type II error occurs whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted. In significance testing the researcher decides between the null hypothesis (no effect, no difference) and the alternative hypothesis. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) set out the two ways this decision can go wrong. Laid out as a table, there are four outcomes. If the null is true and we reject it, that is a Type I error (alpha), a false positive; its probability is the significance level, usually .05. If the null is false and we accept it, or more strictly fail to reject it, that is a Type II error (beta), a false negative: a real effect is missed. Accepting a true null and rejecting a false null are both correct decisions. Power, 1 minus beta, is the probability of detecting an effect that is really there; Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested aiming for at least .80. Power rises with a larger sample, a larger effect, less measurement error and a more lenient alpha, so for a fixed sample, lowering alpha to cut Type I errors raises the risk of a Type II error. Example: Meera tests whether a new revision method raises marks. The method truly works, but with only 10 students her result is not significant and she concludes it has no effect: a Type II error. Option c is right because a Type II error is keeping a null hypothesis that is actually false. Exam trap: the definitions are easy to reverse. Type I is rejecting a true null (option d, alpha, the false alarm); Type II is accepting a false null (beta, the miss). Think of the boy who cried wolf: first the villagers believed in a wolf that was not there (Type I), later they ignored the wolf that was (Type II).", "topic": "Type I and Type II errors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which type of reinforcement is least resistant to Extinction?", "options": {"a": "Ratio Interval.", "b": "Variable interval.", "c": "Fixed Interval.", "d": "Fixed Ratio."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: fixed interval reinforcement is the least resistant to extinction of the options given. Resistance to extinction is how long a learned response continues once reinforcement stops. Continuous reinforcement, a reward after every response, extinguishes fastest. Partial (intermittent) reinforcement makes behaviour last longer, the partial reinforcement extinction effect (Lloyd Humphreys, 1939). Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped four basic partial schedules built from two choices: ratio (reward depends on the number of responses) or interval (on time passed), and fixed or variable. Fixed ratio gives a high rate with a pause after each reward, as in piece-rate pay. Variable ratio gives the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance, as in slot machines. Fixed interval gives a pause, then faster responding as the time approaches (the scallop). Variable interval gives a moderate, steady rate. Predictable schedules extinguish faster because the switch to no reward is easier to notice; on a variable schedule, long gaps without reward have always happened before. Example: Meera's college posts results every Monday, so she checks the notice board only on Mondays; when it stops being updated, she soon gives up. Her brother Kabir plays a game that pays coins after an unpredictable number of spins, and he keeps spinning for days after the coins stop. Option c is right because, among the four basic schedules, introductory texts give fixed interval as the easiest to extinguish. Exam trap: 'ratio interval' (a) is not a schedule at all; it joins the two dimensions. Key note: continuous reinforcement, which is not offered, extinguishes fastest of all; among the partial schedules both fixed ones extinguish faster than the variable ones, and introductory texts single out fixed interval as the easiest to extinguish, so fixed ratio (d) is the near miss and c stands.", "topic": "Schedules and resistance to extinction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "options": {"a": "Each involves a crisis or dilemma.", "b": "Each signals a new stage of cognitive development.", "c": "Each is an expression of biological programming.", "d": "Their failure to appear is evidence of psychopathology."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (1905) better; Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial crises", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "options": {"a": "Eating disorders.", "b": "Adolescent behaviour.", "c": "Coping with stress.", "d": "Death and dying."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of death and dying. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-born American psychiatrist (the paper spells her first name 'Elizabeth'), interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital and reported what she learned in On Death and Dying. She proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live until my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work pushed doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice and palliative care movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best seen as common reactions, not a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: told that his cancer has spread, Ramesh, 60, first insists the scans were mixed up (denial), then snaps at his nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up smoking if he can see his grandson born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Elisabeth Kübler-Ross.", "topic": "Kübler-Ross: death and dying", "fig": "Elisabeth Kübler-Ross"}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Adaptation level.", "b": "Intuition.", "c": "Frames of reference.", "d": "Context."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the internal standard used to judge stimuli is called the adaptation level. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) proposed adaptation-level theory to explain why the same stimulus can feel heavy or light, warm or cool, loud or soft. He argued that we never judge a stimulus in absolute terms. Instead, the perceiver keeps a running neutral point, the adaptation level, pooled from three sources: the focal stimulus being judged, the background stimuli around it, and residual stimuli, the traces of past experience. A stimulus above that point feels heavy, bright or pleasant; one below it feels light, dim or unpleasant; one at it feels neutral. The level keeps shifting as experience changes. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. The idea later spread to happiness: Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) argued that people adapt to good fortune, so pleasures fade into a new neutral point, the hedonic treadmill. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his adaptation level has moved up. Option a is right because adaptation level is Helson's technical term for this internal neutral point. Exam trap: 'frames of reference' sounds like the same idea and is the planted near miss. Key note: adaptation level is often described as an internal frame of reference, so c is the near miss; 'frame of reference' is the broader idea that includes outside context, while adaptation level is the technical name for the internal standard, and a stands.", "topic": "Helson's adaptation level", "fig": "Harry Helson"}, {"q": "__________ theory maximized and __________ theory minimized the role of the unconscious:", "options": {"a": "Trait; Humanistic.", "b": "Psychoanalytic; Behaviourist.", "c": "Psychoanalytic; Humanistic.", "d": "Trait; Behaviourist."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "topic": "Unconscious across personality approaches", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "options": {"a": "CBT and sertraline are not significantly different in terms of efficacy.", "b": "CBT improves outcome in around 60% of cases.", "c": "CBT and sertraline combined produce the best outcome.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "topic": "Treating childhood anxiety: CAMS", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "'Eccentric' behaviour marked by odd patterns of thinking and communication.", "b": "An enduring pattern of instability in personal relationships.", "c": "A lack of a well-defined and stable self-image.", "d": "Regular and unpredictable changes in mood."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder features", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "options": {"a": "Block design.", "b": "Mazes.", "c": "Non-verbal cues.", "d": "Drawing people."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "topic": "Porteus Maze Test", "fig": "Stanley David Porteus"}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "options": {"a": "Delinquency as a factor affecting the development of intelligence.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age.", "c": "A z-score.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "topic": "Deviation IQ as a z-score", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "_____ is a sub process of _____:", "options": {"a": "Psychological testing; Observation.", "b": "Psychological testing; Interviewing.", "c": "Interviewing; Psychological assessment.", "d": "Psychological assessment; Observation."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: interviewing is a sub-process of psychological assessment. Psychological assessment is the broad process of collecting and combining information from many sources to answer a referral question about a person: Is this a learning disability? How severe is the depression? Which treatment suits? Its sources include the clinical interview, behavioural observation, psychological tests, the case history, school or medical records and reports from family. Psychological testing is narrower. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour; it yields a score. Assessment, as Gary Groth-Marnat stresses in his Handbook of Psychological Assessment, is the clinician's work of integrating test scores with everything else into a reasoned conclusion and recommendations. So testing, interviewing and observation are all sub-processes, or tools, within assessment; none of them contains the others. Example: Kabir, 9, is referred for falling marks. The psychologist interviews his parents and teacher, talks with and observes Kabir, gives an intelligence test and a reading test, reads his school reports, and then writes a report that ties it all together. The whole sequence is assessment; the interview is one step in it. Option c is right because it is the only option that puts a component (interviewing) inside the umbrella process (psychological assessment). Exam trap: testing and assessment are often used loosely as synonyms, so options that put testing above interviewing or observation look plausible; assessment is always the umbrella, and the other three are its parts.", "topic": "Assessment, testing and interviewing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1?", "options": {"a": "50 Percent.", "b": "16 Percent.", "c": "25 Percent.", "d": "10 Percent."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "topic": "Normal curve areas above z", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "options": {"a": "Verbal fluency.", "b": "Visual perception.", "c": "Memory.", "d": "Attention."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "topic": "Stroop test and attention", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jemas-2024": [{"q": "Symbol search subtest of WAIS-IV assesses:", "concept": "The answer is d: Symbol Search is a core subtest of the Processing Speed Index of the WAIS-IV, so it measures processing speed. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, fourth edition (2008), descends from the Wechsler-Bellevue scale of David Wechsler (1939). It has 10 core subtests grouped into four indices. Verbal Comprehension: Similarities, Vocabulary and Information (Comprehension supplemental). Perceptual Reasoning: Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles (Figure Weights and Picture Completion supplemental). Working Memory: Digit Span and Arithmetic (Letter-Number Sequencing supplemental). Processing Speed: Symbol Search and Coding (Cancellation supplemental). In Symbol Search the person looks at one or two target symbols, scans a row of symbols and marks whether a target is present, working against a strict time limit. In Coding the person copies symbols paired with numbers from a key. Both tasks are easy in themselves, so the score mostly reflects how quickly and accurately someone can scan, compare and respond to simple visual information. Processing speed is sensitive to brain injury, ageing and depression. Example: Meera, aged 68, recovering from a stroke, answers every Symbol Search item she attempts correctly but completes far fewer items than others her age; her low Processing Speed score points to slowed mental speed, not poor reasoning. Option d is right because Symbol Search is one of the two core subtests of the Processing Speed Index. Exam trap: \"mental manipulation\" sounds like any demanding task, but holding and manipulating information in mind is working memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic, Letter-Number Sequencing); and concentration, though every timed task needs it, is not a WAIS-IV index.", "traps": {"a": "The WAIS-IV has no auditory processing index; auditory memory is an index of the separate Wechsler Memory Scale (WMS-IV), and Symbol Search is a purely visual task.", "b": "Concentration helps on every timed subtest and is part of what the Working Memory Index is said to reflect, but no WAIS-IV index is called concentration, and Symbol Search is scored for speed.", "c": "Mental manipulation (holding and reworking information in mind) is the job of the Working Memory Index, measured by Digit Span and Arithmetic, not by the Processing Speed subtests."}}, {"q": "In his observation of motor skills of young children, Arnold Gesell found infant motor skills developing in a consistent sequence. According to his observations, an infant typically:", "concept": "The answer is c: in Arnold Gesell's norms an infant typically sits without support at about five to six months, after rolling over and well before standing or walking. Arnold Gesell (1880 to 1961), at the Yale Clinic of Child Development, filmed thousands of infants and turned the ages at which behaviours appeared into norms (1925, 1940), later used in the Gesell Developmental Schedules. He found that motor skills unfold in the same order in almost every child: head control, rolling over, sitting, crawling, standing and then walking. This follows the cephalocaudal principle, in which control spreads from head to foot. Gesell explained the order by maturation: a genetically programmed timetable in nerves and muscles, so practice adds little until the child is ready. His co-twin study with Helen Thompson (1929), in which one twin was trained in stair climbing and soon matched by the untrained twin, made this point. Rough norms: steady head control at about 6 weeks to 3 months, rolling over at about 3 to 6 months, sitting without support at about 6 months, crawling at about 7 to 9 months, standing alone at about 11 to 12 months and walking alone at about 12 months (normal range roughly 9 to 17). Example: baby Kabir rolls over at 4 months, sits on the floor without cushions at 6 months and takes his first steps just after his first birthday, an ordinary Gesell sequence. Option c is right because it is the only option whose age matches the sitting norm. Exam trap: the distractors keep a believable order but move each skill months too early. Key note: modern norms (WHO Multicentre Growth Reference Study, 2006) put unsupported sitting at about 6 months with a wide normal range, so \"five to six months\" is at the early end; it is still the only option in the right range, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Walking alone is the last of the gross motor milestones, at about 12 months on average (roughly 9 to 17); nine months is the very early edge, not the typical age in Gesell's norms.", "b": "At one month an infant can barely lift the head when lying on the tummy; rolling over comes at about 3 to 6 months, once head and trunk control have developed in cephalocaudal order.", "d": "At seven months most infants are just sitting steadily and starting to crawl; standing alone comes at about 11 to 12 months, after pulling to stand at about 8 to 9 months."}}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that:", "concept": "The answer is b: Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty depended heavily on the situation, so the same child could cheat in one setting and stay honest in another that tested the same moral principle. In the Character Education Inquiry at Teachers College, Columbia University, reported in Studies in Deceit, they asked a simple question: is honesty a general trait that a child carries everywhere? They gave thousands of American schoolchildren disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in class tests, party games, athletic contests and home tasks: for instance, marking their own test papers when they could secretly change answers, or keeping coins from a box. Almost every child cheated somewhere, and few cheated everywhere. Honesty in one situation predicted honesty in another only weakly, and knowing the moral rule, or attending character education or Sunday school, barely predicted conduct. What mattered was the situation: the risk of being caught, the effort needed and what classmates were doing. They called this the doctrine of specificity. Walter Mischel (1968) later built on it in his critique of trait consistency, which started the person-situation debate; Seymour Epstein (1979) replied that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more stable. Example: Riya peeks at a classmate's answer sheet when the teacher steps out, yet on the way home returns the extra change a shopkeeper gave her by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situation-specific (situational) morality. Exam trap: the absolute words \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c describe exactly the consistent moral character that the study failed to find.", "traps": {"a": "This is the opposite of the finding: no group of \"moral\" children kept to recognised standards in every situation, and honesty in one test predicted honesty in the next only weakly.", "c": "Nearly every child cheated somewhere when the risk of being caught was low, so a group of children who never cheat did not exist; the doctrine of specificity says conduct shifts with the situation.", "d": "The inquiry's conclusion was about situations, not sex differences; the claim that one sex has a weaker conscience (superego) echoes Sigmund Freud (1925), not Hartshorne and May."}}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "traps": {"a": "Voluntary (intentional) movement and object permanence are sensorimotor achievements of the first two years, while intuitive thought belongs to ages 4 to 7, so putting object permanence last reverses the order.", "b": "Concrete operations (7 to 11) come long after mental representation (about 18 to 24 months), and information processing is a separate approach modelled on the computer, not a step in Piaget's sequence.", "d": "Conservation is the hallmark of the concrete operational stage (7 to 11), so it cannot come before object permanence (infancy) or intuitive thought (4 to 7)."}}, {"q": "The play pattern most prevalent among three-year old children is:", "concept": "The answer is d: parallel play, playing beside other children with similar toys but not with them, is the play pattern most typical of three-year-olds. Mildred Parten (1932) watched nursery-school children aged two to five and sorted their free play by how socially involved it was. Her six categories, from least to most social, are: unoccupied behaviour (not playing, watching whatever catches the eye); solitary play (playing alone, absorbed in one's own toys); onlooker behaviour (watching others play, perhaps talking to them, without joining in); parallel play (playing alongside others with the same kind of toys, without trying to influence them); associative play (playing together and sharing materials, but with no common goal or division of roles); and cooperative play (organised play with a shared goal and roles, such as building a house together or playing a team game). The more social forms rise with age, but the earlier forms do not vanish: solitary and parallel play remain common in older preschoolers. Example: three-year-olds Arjun and Meera sit side by side in the sandpit, each filling a bucket and now and then glancing at the other, but neither joins the other's game. Option d is right because parallel play is the pattern most characteristic of the third year. Exam trap: associative play looks similar because the children are close together, but in associative play they share toys and talk about the activity, and it rises mainly from about 3 to 4. Key note: parallel play peaks around 2 to 3 and associative play rises through 3 to 4, so age 3 sits near the boundary; child development texts give parallel play as the typical pattern of the three-year-old, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Solitary play is the infant and early toddler pattern in Mildred Parten's scheme, when a child plays alone without regard to others; by three most children are playing near their peers.", "b": "Associative play (sharing and interacting, but with no common goal) rises from about 3 to 4, so it is the step after the three-year-old's typical parallel play.", "c": "Cooperative play, organised play with a shared goal and roles, is the most social of Parten's categories and appears from about 4 to 5 onward."}}, {"q": "The positive psychology movement:", "concept": "The answer is d: positive psychology shares its central themes of growth, strengths, meaning and well-being with the humanistic psychology of Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1961). Positive psychology is the scientific study of positive experiences, positive traits and the institutions that help people flourish. Martin Seligman (1998) launched it in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, and he and Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi set out its programme in a special issue of the American Psychologist (2000). Seligman argued that psychology had spent decades on disease and repair and should also study what makes life worth living. Its topics include happiness, character strengths, hope, gratitude, resilience, flow (Csikszentmihalyi's absorbing state when challenge matches skill) and the PERMA model of well-being (positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment). These themes echo humanistic psychology, the \"third force\" of the 1950s and 1960s, which stressed self-actualisation and the fully functioning person; Maslow even used the phrase \"positive psychology\" in Motivation and Personality. The main difference is method: positive psychology insists on measurement and controlled research, which humanists were often accused of neglecting. Example: instead of only asking why Kabir's anxiety keeps returning, a positive psychologist also measures his strengths, such as kindness and curiosity, and helps him use them every day. Option d is right because the two movements share clear thematic parallels, even though positive psychology claims a more empirical style. Exam trap: c reverses the movement, which emphasises adaptation, resilience and the strengths that help people cope, rather than playing them down.", "traps": {"a": "Psychodynamic theory (Sigmund Freud and his successors) centres on unconscious conflict and pathology; positive psychology arose as a reaction against a deficit focus, not from within psychoanalysis.", "b": "Social behaviourism is George Herbert Mead's (1934) account of how the self grows out of social interaction; positive psychology began with Martin Seligman's 1998 address, not in that tradition.", "c": "This reverses the movement: positive psychology emphasises adaptation, resilience and strengths that help people cope and flourish."}}, {"q": "Language comprehension is traditionally associated with which area of the brain:", "concept": "The answer is d: language comprehension is traditionally linked to Wernicke's area, in the back part of the left superior temporal gyrus. The classic map of language in the brain came from patients with strokes. Paul Broca (1861) studied a man nicknamed \"Tan\", who understood speech but could say little beyond that one syllable; at autopsy the damage lay in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area and linked with speech production and grammar. Carl Wernicke (1874) then described patients with damage further back, in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant (usually left) hemisphere. They spoke fluently, with normal rhythm, but their speech was full of wrong or invented words and they understood little of what was said to them. This is Wernicke's (fluent, receptive) aphasia, and the region is Wernicke's area. A bundle of fibres, the arcuate fasciculus, joins the two areas; damage to it causes conduction aphasia, with very poor repetition. Example: after a stroke, Arjun talks easily and quickly, but when his daughter asks him to close his eyes he smiles and describes his breakfast; he has lost comprehension, which points to Wernicke's area. Option d is right because Wernicke's area is the classic centre for understanding spoken and written language. Exam trap: Broca's area is the famous near miss, since it is also a left-hemisphere language area, but it serves production, so Broca's aphasia is halting, effortful speech with fairly good understanding.", "traps": {"a": "There is no \"Recorla's\" area; the name looks like a garbled Robert Rescorla, the conditioning theorist of the Rescorla-Wagner model (1972), not a brain region.", "b": "Broca's area (Paul Broca, 1861), in the left inferior frontal gyrus, is linked to speech production; damage there leaves comprehension relatively spared.", "c": "\"Gage's\" points to Phineas Gage, whose 1848 railway accident drove an iron rod through his frontal lobe and changed his personality (reported by his physician John Harlow); it is a famous case, not a language area."}}, {"q": "In the Jungian view of personality, key functions include:", "concept": "The answer is b: sensing, feeling and intuiting are three of the four psychological functions in Carl Jung's analytical psychology. In Psychological Types, Carl Jung (1921) asked why people meet the same world so differently. His first answer was two attitudes, which describe the direction of psychic energy: extraversion, in which energy flows outward to people and things, and introversion, in which it flows inward to one's own thoughts and feelings. His second answer was four functions, the ways the mind takes in and judges experience, arranged in two opposed pairs. Thinking judges whether something is true or false, and feeling judges whether it is pleasant or valuable; because both evaluate, Jung called them the rational functions. Sensing registers what is actually there through the senses, like a camera, and intuiting grasps possibilities and hunches beyond the evidence; because both perceive without evaluating, he called them the irrational functions. One function usually dominates while its opposite stays undeveloped in the unconscious. Combining the 2 attitudes with the 4 functions gives his 8 psychological types, such as the introverted thinking type or the extraverted feeling type. Example: on a holiday, Meera notices every colour, smell and price in the market (sensing), while her brother Kabir keeps imagining what the old town could become (intuiting). Option b is right because it lists three genuine Jungian functions, with only thinking left out. Exam trap: d uses Jung's real words extraversion and introversion, but these are attitudes, not functions, and \"interposition\" is a depth cue from perception.", "traps": {"a": "The will to power is Alfred Adler's term (borrowed from Friedrich Nietzsche), and the will to pleasure is how Viktor Frankl summed up Sigmund Freud, set against his own will to meaning; neither is a Jungian function.", "c": "Prototaxic, parataxic and syntaxic are Harry Stack Sullivan's (1953) modes of experience in his interpersonal theory of development, not Jung's functions.", "d": "Extraversion and introversion are Jung's attitudes (directions of psychic energy), not functions, and interposition is a monocular depth cue in perception, unrelated to Jung."}}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "traps": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working is the everyday sense of 'loafing' (idling or slacking); the social psychology term is specifically about reduced effort when efforts are pooled in a group.", "b": "Working harder in groups than alone is the reverse of social loafing; it resembles social compensation (Kipling Williams and Steven Karau, 1991), where people work harder to make up for weak teammates.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition is social facilitation, better performance on simple tasks in the presence of others (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), not loafing."}}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "traps": {"a": "Taste is processed in the gustatory cortex of the insula and frontal operculum, reached through cranial nerves VII, IX and X and the thalamus, not in the postcentral gyrus.", "b": "Sight is processed in the primary visual (striate) cortex around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe.", "c": "Hearing is processed in the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus of the temporal lobe."}}, {"q": "REM sleep behavior disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterized as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "traps": {"b": "Rapid eye movements continue in RBD; they are how the stage is recognised on the sleep study, so they are not the missing feature.", "c": "The desynchronised, high-frequency, low-amplitude EEG of REM is still present in RBD; the brain is in genuine REM, and only the muscle paralysis fails.", "d": "Penile erections are a normal feature of REM sleep in males and are not what is lost in RBD; their presence during sleep is used instead in nocturnal penile tumescence testing for erectile dysfunction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is a monoamine neurotransmitter released by neurons of the raphe nuclei; it regulates mood, sleep and appetite and is the target of SSRIs, so it is a true neurotransmitter.", "b": "Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter of the nigrostriatal (movement) and mesolimbic (reward) pathways, linked to Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but some brainstem neurons release it at synapses, so it is both a hormone and a neurotransmitter; that dual role is why it tempts, yet insulin is the purer exception."}}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is not classified as an opioid?", "concept": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "traps": {"b": "Codeine is a natural opiate alkaloid of the opium poppy, a weaker relative of morphine used in cough syrups and painkillers; the liver converts part of it to morphine, so it is a true opioid.", "c": "Heroin (diacetylmorphine) is a semi-synthetic opioid made by adding two acetyl groups to morphine; it acts at mu opioid receptors and is the classic opioid of dependence.", "d": "Morphine is the main natural alkaloid of opium and the reference opioid against which other painkillers are measured, so it cannot be the exception."}}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "concept": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A hit is a correct 'yes' when the signal really is present, the opposite cell of the table from a false alarm, which happens on a trial with no signal.", "b": "A miss is saying 'no' when a signal was present, a failure to detect; the stem describes the reverse error, a 'yes' when nothing was there.", "d": "False positive is the medical and statistical name (as in a Type I error) for the same kind of event, so it is a near synonym; but the stem asks for the signal-detection term, which is false alarm."}}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "traps": {"a": "'Anomalous conditioning' is not a recognised type of conditioning; it is an invented distractor that borrows the word 'anomalous' (irregular) to sound technical.", "b": "'Distinctive conditioning' is not a standard term; the nearest real idea is discrimination, where a subject learns to respond to one stimulus and not to a similar one, which is not what happens with the light.", "c": "Counterconditioning, shown by Mary Cover Jones (1924) with Peter and the rabbit, replaces an existing CR with an incompatible one (fear with relaxation or pleasure); it removes a response rather than spreading it to a new stimulus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an accurate learning acquisition sequence?", "concept": "The answer is a: in the learning theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941, 1950), learning runs from drive to cue to response to reinforcement. At Yale, in Social Learning and Imitation and later Personality and Psychotherapy, they restated Sigmund Freud's clinical ideas in the language of the drive-reduction theory of Clark Hull (1943). They said learning needs four things, often summed up as \"want something, notice something, do something, get something\". A drive is a strong stimulus that pushes the person to act; it can be primary (hunger, pain) or learned (fear, the need for approval). A cue is a weaker stimulus that tells the person when, where and how to respond. A response is the behaviour itself. Reinforcement (reward) is the drive reduction that follows, which strengthens the link between that cue and that response so it is more likely next time. Their key idea was the learned drive: Miller's rats, once shocked in a white box, learned to turn a wheel to escape it even with no shock present, reinforced only by fear reduction. They used the same scheme to explain repression (not-thinking reinforced by relief from fear) and approach-avoidance conflict. Example: Riya is hungry (drive), sees the college canteen sign (cue), walks in and orders idli (response), and her hunger falls (reinforcement), so next time the sign draws her in faster. Option a is right because it lists the four elements in their causal order. Exam trap: the distractors reuse the same four words in a shuffled order; because a cue only guides behaviour that a drive has already set going, any order that puts the cue first, or the response before the cue, is wrong.", "traps": {"b": "Putting the cue first reverses the theory's logic: in Dollard and Miller's model a cue only guides behaviour once a drive has set the person into action, so drive must come first.", "c": "This places the drive after the response, but it is the drive that impels the response in the first place, and its reduction is what counts as reinforcement.", "d": "The cue tells the person when, where and how to respond, so it must come before the response, not after it."}}, {"q": "An animal receives a shock but learns that pressing a bar will turn off the shock. However, if there is no response that will turn off the shock, the situation is described by the term:", "concept": "The answer is c: when no response can end an aversive event, an animal learns that it has no control and stops trying, a state called learned helplessness. The stem contrasts two situations. In the first, pressing a bar turns off a shock that is already on; this is escape learning, a form of negative reinforcement, and the animal learns it quickly. In the second, nothing works. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs shocks they could switch off by pressing a panel, a second, yoked group identical shocks they could not control, and a third group no shock. Later all the dogs were placed in a shuttle box, where jumping a low barrier ended the shock. Most dogs in the escapable and no-shock groups learned to jump, but most yoked dogs lay down and whined, passively taking shocks they could easily have escaped. Lack of control, not the shock itself, produced the deficit. Seligman offered learned helplessness as a model of depression; Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later recast it in attributional terms, and Seligman turned it around into learned optimism. Example: after failing three maths tests despite studying hard, Kabir stops revising for the fourth, convinced nothing he does matters, even though a good tutor is now available. Option c is right because it names the state produced by uncontrollable aversive events. Exam trap: avoidance learning sounds close, but it means responding to a warning signal so the shock never arrives; it needs a response that works, which is exactly what is missing here.", "traps": {"a": "Avoidance learning means responding to a warning signal so that the shock never comes (O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory, 1947); it requires an effective response, which this stem says does not exist.", "b": "Second-order (higher-order) conditioning is Ivan Pavlov's finding that a well-established conditioned stimulus can itself condition a new neutral stimulus; it has nothing to do with uncontrollable shock.", "d": "Latent learning (Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik, 1930) is learning that happens without reinforcement and shows only when a reward appears, as in rats that learned a maze while simply exploring it."}}, {"q": "One is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, four is a door. This is part of improving memory and the method is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: \"one is a bun, two is a shoe\" is the pegword (peg-word) method, a mnemonic that hangs each item to be remembered on a rhyming number peg. A mnemonic is any device that adds organisation, meaning or imagery so that material is easier to recall. In the pegword method you first overlearn a fixed rhyme: one is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, four is a door, and so on up to ten. These words are the pegs. To remember a list, you form a vivid, interacting image of each item with its peg. Because the pegs are numbered and never change, you can recall the list in order, start in the middle, or say at once which item was seventh. The method works mainly through visual imagery: the dual coding theory of Allan Paivio (1971) holds that concrete words stored both as an image and as a verbal label are remembered better than words stored only one way. Example: to remember milk, onions and soap, Meera pictures a bun soaked in milk, a shoe stuffed with onions and a tree hung with bars of soap; in the shop she runs through the rhyme and each image comes back. Option a is right because a rhyme of numbered pegs is the defining feature of the pegword method. Exam trap: the method of loci also uses vivid images, but it places them at locations along a familiar route, not on number rhymes.", "traps": {"b": "The method of loci, credited to Simonides of Ceos, places vivid images at places along a familiar route, such as the rooms of your house; it uses locations, not rhyming number pegs.", "c": "Visual imagery is the general ingredient that both pegword and loci rely on (Allan Paivio's dual coding, 1971), not the name of this specific rhyming technique.", "d": "Flashbulb memory (Roger Brown and James Kulik, 1977) is a vivid, confident memory of where you were when you heard shocking news; it is a kind of memory, not a method for improving memory."}}, {"q": "Which of these is a stage in the Stages of Change Model?", "concept": "The answer is b: contemplation is the second stage of the Stages of Change (transtheoretical) model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983). They built the model from studies of how smokers quit, with and without therapy, and found that people change through a predictable series of stages rather than in one leap. In precontemplation there is no intention to change within the next six months, and the person may not see a problem at all. In contemplation the person knows there is a problem and is seriously thinking about change, but is ambivalent, weighing pros and cons, and can stay stuck here for a long time. In preparation the person intends to act within the next month and takes small steps. In action the behaviour has been changed within the past six months. In maintenance the change has lasted more than six months and the work is preventing relapse. Termination, added later, means there is no temptation left. Relapse is common, and people often cycle back through earlier stages. The model guides treatment: a person in precontemplation needs awareness raising, not a quit plan, and motivational interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, 1991) is often paired with it to resolve ambivalence. Example: Arjun says, \"I know my drinking is hurting my family and I should cut down, but not yet; it helps me relax.\" He is in contemplation. Option b is right because contemplation is a named stage of the model. Exam trap: deliberation sounds like a synonym for contemplation, but it is not the model's term; the stage is called contemplation.", "traps": {"a": "Relaxation is a technique used in behaviour therapy, for example Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1929), not a stage in Prochaska and DiClemente's model.", "c": "Deliberation is an everyday word for thinking things over, but the model calls this stage contemplation; deliberation is not one of its stages.", "d": "Meditation is a contemplative practice used in mindfulness-based therapies, not a stage of change in the transtheoretical model."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client-centered therapist most likely to use?", "concept": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) named the core conditions that let clients heal themselves: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "traps": {"a": "Analysis, the interpretation of free associations, dreams, resistance and transference, is the core of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (1900 onwards), in which the expert explains hidden meanings; Carl Rogers rejected the therapist as interpreter.", "b": "Giving advice is directive counselling, as in Edmund Williamson's trait-and-factor approach (1939), which puts the counsellor's judgement in charge; Carl Rogers held that advice undermines the client's own capacity to find answers.", "d": "Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus (for example, disulfiram for drinking) and belongs to behaviour therapy; its controlling, technique-led stance is the opposite of Carl Rogers's accepting relationship."}}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "concept": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "traps": {"a": "Judgement is the ability to weigh a situation and act sensibly, tested by asking what the person would do on finding a stamped, addressed letter in the street; a hallucinating person may judge well or badly, but the symptom itself is perceptual.", "c": "Intelligence is general reasoning ability measured by tests such as the Wechsler scales; hallucinations occur in people of any IQ and are not a fault of intellect.", "d": "Disorders of thinking are delusions (thought content) and formal thought disorder such as loosening of associations (thought form); hallucinations often accompany them in psychosis but are classed under perception."}}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "concept": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "traps": {"b": "Bizarre behaviour (odd clothing, agitated or aggressive acts, strange repetitive rituals) is one of the main domains of Nancy Andreasen's SAPS (1984); it is a positive symptom, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Circumstantiality is speech that wanders through needless detail before finally reaching its point (tangential speech never gets there); Andreasen's SAPS rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, so it belongs in the list.", "d": "Delusions are fixed false beliefs held despite clear contrary evidence, such as ideas of persecution or reference; they are the most typical positive symptom of all."}}, {"q": "When same group is studied over a period of time; the study method is known as?", "concept": "The answer is a: a longitudinal study follows the same group of people and measures them repeatedly over a period of time. Developmental psychologists have three main designs for studying how behaviour changes with age. In a longitudinal design the same individuals are re-tested over months or years, so the researcher sees real change within each person and can test stability, such as whether a shy toddler becomes a shy teenager. The gifted children study of Lewis Terman (1921) followed about 1,500 children into old age. Its drawbacks are cost, time, loss of participants (attrition), practice effects from repeated testing, and results that may be tied to one generation. In a cross-sectional design, different age groups are tested once, at the same time; it is quick and cheap, but it confounds age with cohort (generation) effects and shows only group differences, not individual change. A sequential design (K. Warner Schaie, 1965) combines the two by following several cohorts over time; his Seattle Longitudinal Study, begun in 1956, found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood. Example: a researcher tests Riya and 200 other children on vocabulary at ages 5, 10 and 15 to see how each child's language grows; because the same children are tested each time, the study is longitudinal. Option a is right because studying the same group repeatedly over time is the definition of a longitudinal design. Exam trap: sequential designs also follow people over time, but they track several cohorts at once, so \"same group studied over time\" points to longitudinal.", "traps": {"b": "A sequential (cohort-sequential) design, set out by K. Warner Schaie (1965), combines cross-sectional and longitudinal methods by following several age cohorts over time, so it studies more than one group.", "c": "A cross-sectional design compares different age groups at a single point in time, so different people, not the same group, supply each age.", "d": "Recapitulation is G. Stanley Hall's (1904) theory, borrowed from Ernst Haeckel, that a child's development replays the evolution of the species; it is a theory, not a research method."}}, {"q": "Which psychologist thought that our behaviours are learnt through interaction with, and observation of, the environment?", "concept": "The answer is d: Albert Bandura (1977) held that behaviour is learned through interaction with, and observation of, the social environment. Bandura argued that strict behaviourism, in which a person must respond and be reinforced, could not explain how quickly people pick up complex behaviour. Much learning is observational: we watch a model, notice what happens to that model (vicarious reinforcement or punishment) and later copy the behaviour. In the Bobo doll studies of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961, 1963), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later imitated the same acts, often word for word, while children who saw a calm model rarely did. Bandura described four processes needed for modelling: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. In Social Learning Theory (1977) and later social cognitive theory (1986) he added reciprocal determinism: behaviour, personal factors (thoughts, expectations, self-efficacy) and the environment all influence one another, so people both shape and are shaped by their surroundings. Example: Kabir watches his older sister earn praise for greeting guests politely and starts greeting them himself, though nobody has ever rewarded him directly. Option d is right because learning through observation of, and interaction with, the environment is the core of Bandura's theory. Exam trap: Clark Hull is also a learning theorist, but his drive-reduction theory explains learning through reinforcement of one's own responses, not through watching others.", "traps": {"a": "Raymond Cattell (1949) is a trait theorist who used factor analysis to find 16 source traits, measured by his 16PF; he explained personality through traits, not through observation of the environment.", "b": "Clark Hull (1943) built drive-reduction theory, in which responses are learned when they reduce a biological drive; learning comes from one's own reinforced responses, not from watching others.", "c": "Sigmund Freud (1900) traced behaviour to unconscious drives and early childhood conflict in psychoanalysis, not to learning by observing the environment."}}, {"q": "The procedure in which people learn to modify internal responses such as heart rate and body temperature is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: biofeedback is the procedure in which people learn to change internal bodily responses, such as heart rate or skin temperature, by watching a live signal of them. Normally we cannot sense our blood pressure or muscle tension precisely, so we cannot learn to control them. In biofeedback, sensors measure a bodily process and an instrument turns it into a tone, graph or light that changes moment by moment. When the signal moves in the desired direction, that change is reinforced, so the person gradually learns which thoughts, breathing patterns or ways of relaxing produce it. Common types include electromyographic (EMG) feedback of muscle tension for tension headache, skin temperature (thermal) feedback for migraine, heart-rate feedback for anxiety, and EEG feedback (neurofeedback) for attention problems. The idea rests on the claim of Neal Miller (1969) that autonomic responses, once thought purely involuntary, can be operantly conditioned; his early animal results proved hard to replicate, but human biofeedback is useful, especially when combined with relaxation training. Example: Meera has tension headaches; with EMG sensors on her forehead she hears a tone that falls as her muscles relax, and over several sessions she learns to lower the tone without the machine. Option a is right because learning to modify internal responses using feedback about them is the definition of biofeedback. Exam trap: hypnosis can also change bodily experience, but through suggestion, with no feedback signal to learn from, so it is not this procedure.", "traps": {"b": "Hypnosis (the term was coined by James Braid, 1843) is a state of focused attention and heightened suggestibility induced by a hypnotist; it gives no ongoing measurement of the body for the person to learn from.", "c": "Sublimation is a Freudian defence mechanism in which unacceptable impulses are channelled into socially valued activities, such as aggression into sport; it has nothing to do with controlling heart rate.", "d": "There is a correct option, since biofeedback is exactly the procedure described, so \"none of the above\" does not apply."}}, {"q": "\"First Rank Symptoms\" of schizophrenia given by Schneider include all except:", "concept": "The answer is a: depersonalisation is not one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia. Kurt Schneider (1939), a German psychiatrist, wanted symptoms that clinicians could rate reliably and that, in the absence of brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia; his Clinical Psychopathology (English edition 1959) made them famous as first-rank symptoms (FRS). Their common theme is a breakdown of the boundary between self and the outside world. Three kinds of voices: audible thoughts (thought echo), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity (made) experiences: made feelings, made impulses and made volitional acts, felt as controlled by an outside force, plus somatic passivity, bodily sensations imposed from outside. Finally, delusional perception: a normal perception suddenly given an unrelated delusional meaning. Depersonalisation, the feeling that one's self is unreal or detached, is common in anxiety, depersonalisation-derealisation disorder and after drug use, and insight is kept. Example: Arjun says his neighbour's thoughts are being pushed into his head (thought insertion, an FRS), while Kabir says that during panic attacks he feels as if he is watching himself from outside (depersonalisation, not an FRS). Option a is right because depersonalisation is the only option missing from Schneider's list. Exam trap: depersonalisation sounds like passivity, but in passivity the person believes an outside agency controls them, while in depersonalisation they know the strangeness is a feeling. Key note: the booklet's wording is loose: the symptom is voices giving a running commentary on one's actions, and the delusion on the list is delusional perception, which is one kind of primary delusion (Karl Jaspers, 1913); neither changes the answer, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions are one of Schneider's three kinds of first-rank auditory hallucinations; the option's wording is loose, but it still points to a first-rank symptom.", "c": "Delusional perception, a primary delusion (in Karl Jaspers's sense) that arises suddenly from a normal perception, is on Schneider's list, so it is not the exception.", "d": "Somatic passivity, bodily sensations felt as imposed by an outside agency, is one of Schneider's passivity (made) experiences, so it is a first-rank symptom."}}, {"q": "The Indian Psychoanalytic Society was founded by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Girindrasekhar Bose (1922) founded the Indian Psychoanalytic Society in Calcutta. Bose (1887 to 1953) trained first in medicine and then in psychology at Calcutta University, and worked out psychoanalytic ideas largely on his own; his doctoral thesis, Concept of Repression (1921), began his long correspondence with Sigmund Freud. The letters show real disagreement. Bose proposed his theory of opposite wishes, in which every wish is paired with its opposite, one expressed and the other repressed, and he did not give the Oedipus complex the central place that Freud did. He is called the father of psychoanalysis in India. Modern Indian psychology itself began at Calcutta University. The philosopher Brajendra Nath Seal drew up the syllabus of India's first psychology department there (1915 to 1916). Narendra Nath Sengupta (1889 to 1944), who had trained under Hugo Munsterberg at Harvard, set up its experimental laboratory and later led the founding of the Indian Psychological Association in the mid-1920s. Example: if Riya is asked who brought Freud's method to India and argued about it with Freud himself, the answer is Bose; if she is asked who brought the experimental laboratory, it is Sengupta. Option a is right because Bose founded the society and led it as its first president. Exam trap: all four names belong to the same early Calcutta story, so examiners count on you mixing up the founder of psychoanalysis in India (Bose) with the founder of experimental psychology in India (Sengupta), or assuming Freud founded the society himself.", "traps": {"b": "Brajendra Nath Seal, a philosopher, drew up the syllabus of India's first psychology department at Calcutta University (1915 to 1916); he did not found the psychoanalytic society.", "c": "N. N. Sengupta, trained under Hugo Munsterberg at Harvard, set up the experimental psychology laboratory at Calcutta and led the founding of the Indian Psychological Association in the mid-1920s; he is a pioneer of experimental, not psychoanalytic, psychology in India.", "d": "Sigmund Freud corresponded with Girindrasekhar Bose and welcomed the society, but he never came to India and did not found any Indian society."}}, {"q": "\"Kluver-Bucy Syndrome\" is characterised by all except:", "concept": "The answer is d: delusions are not part of Kluver-Bucy syndrome, the cluster of behavioural changes that follows damage to both temporal lobes, especially both amygdalae. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both anterior temporal lobes, amygdalae included, in rhesus monkeys. Wild, aggressive monkeys became tame and placid, and approached snakes and people without fear. The monkeys showed a set of signs now named after the two researchers: psychic blindness, a visual agnosia in which objects are seen but their meaning is not recognised; hyperorality, examining everything by mouth (licking, biting, mouthing objects); hypersexuality, sexual behaviour directed at unusual partners and objects; placidity and loss of fear; hypermetamorphosis, a compulsion to attend to and touch every visual stimulus; and dietary change, eating things they normally refuse, such as meat. Most of these follow from losing the amygdala, which attaches emotional meaning and fear to what we perceive. In humans, partial forms follow herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia, often with aphasia and amnesia from wider damage. Delusions, false fixed beliefs, belong to psychosis and are not part of the syndrome. Example: after herpes encephalitis, Kabir puts coins and keys in his mouth, cannot tell what a comb is just by looking at it, and shows no fear of a barking dog, a partial Kluver-Bucy picture. Option d is right because delusions are the only option missing from the syndrome. Exam trap: \"compulsive licking and biting\" is simply hyperorality in plain words, so do not choose it as the exception just because the technical term is absent.", "traps": {"a": "Visual agnosia (psychic blindness), seeing an object without recognising what it is, was one of the first signs Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy described, so it is part of the syndrome.", "b": "Hypersexuality, sexual behaviour towards unusual partners or objects, is a core Kluver-Bucy sign linked to loss of the amygdala, so it is not the exception.", "c": "Compulsive licking and biting describes hyperorality, the urge to examine objects by mouth, which is a defining feature of the syndrome."}}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "concept": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. His cerebellum and basal ganglia were intact. The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum is the whole of both cerebral hemispheres, including the cortex, hippocampus and basal ganglia; it is far too broad to name the structure tied to procedural memory.", "c": "The motor cortex sends commands for voluntary movement and does change as skills are practised, so it is involved; but it executes movement rather than being the classic site of skill memory, which is the cerebellum.", "d": "The limbic system, especially the hippocampus and amygdala, supports declarative and emotional memory; H.M. lost most of his hippocampi yet still learned mirror drawing, which shows that skills do not depend on it."}}, {"q": "According to social-exchange theory we will help when:", "concept": "The answer is b: social exchange theory predicts that we help when the benefits of helping are greater than the costs. Social exchange theory, developed by George Homans (1958) and by John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959), treats social life as a kind of trade in which people try, often without noticing, to maximise rewards and minimise costs. Applied to helping, a bystander weighs what helping would bring against what it would cost. Rewards can be external, such as praise, payment or a good reputation, or internal, such as relief from one's own distress at seeing someone suffer, or feeling good about oneself. Costs include time, effort, danger and embarrassment. On this view even helping that looks unselfish is partly self-interested, so the theory sides with psychological egoism in the debate about true altruism. The arousal: cost-reward model of Jane Piliavin and colleagues (1981) uses the same logic: seeing someone in distress causes unpleasant arousal, which bystanders reduce by the cheapest route, so they help when the costs of helping are low and the costs of not helping (guilt, blame) are high. In their New York subway studies (1969), a collapsed man with a cane was helped far more often than one who seemed drunk. Example: Meera stops to help an elderly man pick up his spilt fruit because it takes a minute and she feels good, but drives past a stranded car on a dark highway because the risk feels too high. Option b is right because helping occurs when perceived rewards outweigh perceived costs. Exam trap: a borrows the theory's real external and internal language but garbles it; benefits and costs can each be external or internal, and what matters is which side is larger.", "traps": {"a": "This garbles the theory's terms: rewards and costs can each be external (praise, danger) or internal (good feeling, guilt); social exchange theory predicts helping from which side is larger, not from where they come from.", "c": "Proportional benefits and costs gives no clear prediction about helping; proportion between inputs and outcomes is the language of equity theory (J. Stacy Adams, 1965; applied to relationships by Elaine Walster, G. William Walster and Ellen Berscheid, 1978), which concerns fairness in relationships.", "d": "When costs outweigh benefits, social exchange theory predicts that people will not help, so this is the reverse of its prediction."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was introduced by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test in Paris, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's result as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child could pass. Mental age alone is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. Stern's solution was to divide mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove the decimals in his Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100, and made the abbreviation IQ popular. So if Arjun is 8 years old with a mental age of 10, his IQ is 10/8 x 100 = 125. The ratio IQ later ran into trouble with adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and its name. Exam trap: Terman is the most common wrong answer because the Stanford-Binet made IQ famous, but he popularised the term rather than introducing it. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient, mental age divided by chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and made the abbreviation IQ popular, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "traps": {"a": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied William Stern's ratio by 100 in the Stanford-Binet and popularised the abbreviation IQ, but he did not introduce the term.", "b": "Gordon Allport (1937) is a trait theorist known for cardinal, central and secondary traits and for his definition of personality; he had nothing to do with IQ.", "d": "John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism with his manifesto 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' and ran the Little Albert study (1920); he did not coin IQ."}}, {"q": "What does MMSE test help to assess?", "concept": "The answer is d: the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) is a brief screening test for cognitive impairment, used chiefly to detect and track dementia. Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh (1975) designed it as a ten-minute bedside test. It is scored out of 30: orientation to time and place (10), registration of three words (3), attention and calculation, such as serial sevens (5), recall of the three words (3), and language and copying (9), which covers naming, repeating a phrase, following a three-step command, reading, writing a sentence and copying intersecting pentagons. A score of 23 or below (cut-off 23/24) suggests cognitive impairment, though education and age affect scores; the Hindi Mental State Examination (Mary Ganguli, 1995) adapted it for rural Indian elders with little schooling. It is a screen, not a diagnosis: a low score calls for a full assessment. Example: Riya's 74-year-old grandmother cannot name the year, recalls none of the three words and cannot copy the pentagons, scoring 18, so her doctor refers her for a dementia work-up. Option d is right because the MMSE is designed to screen for dementia. Exam trap: depression can lower MMSE scores (depressive pseudodementia), but the MMSE does not measure mood; depression needs its own scales, such as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961).", "traps": {"a": "Phobias are assessed by clinical interview and fear measures such as the Fear Survey Schedule (Joseph Wolpe and Peter Lang, 1964); the MMSE contains no items on fear or avoidance.", "b": "Depression is measured with mood scales such as the Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (Max Hamilton, 1960) or the Beck Depression Inventory; low mood can lower MMSE scores, but the test does not assess it.", "c": "ADHD is assessed through developmental history and parent and teacher rating scales, such as the Conners scales; the MMSE is a cognitive screen for dementia, not a measure of attention-deficit disorder."}}, {"q": "What is the full form of the test ISAA?", "concept": "The answer is c: ISAA stands for the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism. It is a rating scale built in India to identify autism spectrum disorder and to grade how severe it is. The National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped (NIMH, Secunderabad, now called NIEPID, the National Institute for the Empowerment of Persons with Intellectual Disabilities) developed it in 2009, partly drawing on items of the Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS) of Eric Schopler and colleagues (1980). ISAA has 40 items spread over six domains: social relationship and reciprocity, emotional responsiveness, speech-language and communication, behaviour patterns, sensory aspects and the cognitive component. The clinician observes the child and interviews the parents, then rates each item from 1 (rarely) to 5 (always). Totals therefore run from 40 to 200; a total below 70 means no autism, and higher totals grade mild, moderate or severe autism with a disability percentage. This is why the scale matters in practice: ISAA is the instrument prescribed for certifying autism as a disability in India. For example, when Kabir, aged 6, is brought to a government hospital for a disability certificate, the psychologist scores ISAA, and his total decides whether he is certified and at what percentage. Option c is right because every word of the name fits: Indian (developed and standardised in India), Scale, for Assessment (it measures and grades), of Autism (the condition as a whole, not only in adults). Exam trap: each distractor changes one word, swapping \"Indian\" for \"International\" or \"Assessment\" for \"adults\"; ISAA is not an adult-only tool (it is mostly used with children), and no international scale goes by this name.", "traps": {"a": "\"Indian scale for autism in adults\" swaps \"Assessment\" for \"adults\"; no Indian test has this title, and ISAA is mostly used with children, not restricted to adults.", "b": "\"International scale\" is wrong because ISAA was built and standardised in India by NIMH, Secunderabad (2009); the well-known foreign-origin scales are CARS (USA) and the ADOS.", "d": "\"Indian scale for adult autism\" is an invented title; the two A's in ISAA stand for Assessment and Autism, and the scale is not limited to adults."}}, {"q": "Which of these is not a defense mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is b: catastrophising is not a defence mechanism; it is a cognitive distortion. A defence mechanism, an idea Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced and Anna Freud systematised in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), is an unconscious strategy of the ego that keeps anxiety out of awareness by distorting or disguising a threatening impulse or fact. Displacement moves a feeling from a dangerous target to a safer one, as when Arjun, scolded by his boss, shouts at his younger brother. Reaction formation hides an unacceptable impulse behind its exaggerated opposite, such as gushing over a rival one secretly resents. Humour counts as a defence too: George Vaillant (1977), in his hierarchy of defences, placed it among the mature defences, alongside sublimation, suppression, altruism and anticipation, because it lets a person face something painful while easing the tension. Catastrophising works the other way. It means expecting the worst possible outcome and treating it as unbearable: Meera gets one low mark and concludes she will fail the course, lose her career and ruin her life. Albert Ellis (1962) called this \"awfulising\" in rational emotive therapy, Aaron Beck (1967) described related thinking errors such as magnification, and David Burns (1980) listed it in Feeling Good. It is a habitual, largely conscious thinking error that raises anxiety rather than lowering it, and therapists target it with cognitive restructuring. Option b is right because catastrophising belongs to the cognitive therapy list of distortions, not to the psychoanalytic list of ego defences. Exam trap: both lists describe distorted thinking, so examiners mix them; ask whether the process unconsciously protects the ego from anxiety (a defence) or creates extra anxiety through an exaggerated appraisal (a distortion).", "traps": {"a": "Displacement is a classic Freudian defence (described by Anna Freud, 1936): an impulse is redirected from a threatening target to a safer substitute, like kicking the dog after a row with the boss, so it is a defence.", "c": "Reaction formation is a Freudian defence in which an unacceptable impulse is held down by showing its exaggerated opposite, such as excessive sweetness covering hostility, so it is a defence.", "d": "Humour is a defence, a mature one in George Vaillant's (1977) hierarchy: it lets a person face a painful truth while softening its sting, so it is a defence."}}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "concept": "The answer is b: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness marked by profound retrograde amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian neuropsychiatrist, described it in long-term heavy drinkers. The cause is thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency: heavy drinkers often eat poorly, and alcohol also interferes with the absorption and use of thiamine. The acute stage is often Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881), with confusion, ataxia (an unsteady gait) and eye-movement palsy; if thiamine is not given quickly, the chronic Korsakoff stage follows, so the two are named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the medial (mediodorsal) thalamus. The memory picture has three parts. There is dense anterograde amnesia, so new events are lost within minutes. There is retrograde amnesia reaching back years, with a temporal gradient: recent years are lost more than childhood. And there is confabulation, sincere invented stories that fill the gaps. For example, Rajesh, aged 55, tells his doctor he drove to the office this morning, though he has been in hospital for a month and left his job years ago. Immediate memory span and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option b is right because it is the only option caused by chronic alcohol use, and its retrograde loss is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is an amnestic syndrome caused by a vitamin deficiency, not a primary degenerative dementia, so early thiamine can prevent it; and confabulation is not lying or malingering, because the patient believes the story.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is a progressive degenerative dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles; memory loss is prominent, but it is not caused by alcohol abuse.", "c": "Parkinson's disease (James Parkinson, 1817) comes from loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, giving tremor, rigidity and slowed movement; it is not an alcohol-caused amnesia.", "d": "Huntington's disease (George Huntington, 1872) is an inherited autosomal dominant disorder (an expanded CAG repeat on chromosome 4) with chorea, psychiatric change and dementia; alcohol plays no part in it."}}, {"q": "After recent catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: interviewing flood survivors after the event is ex post facto field research. \"Ex post facto\" is Latin for \"after the fact\". Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, defined it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred, or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher starts from the effect and looks back at the presumed cause. It is also called causal-comparative research. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, assign some people to it and spare others, so manipulation and random assignment are impossible. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards, into people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, and measure outcomes such as fear, sleep problems or grief, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. That combination, an independent variable that happened on its own plus data gathered in the natural setting, is ex post facto field research. Imagine Ananya, a clinical psychologist, interviewing 120 families in Assam two months after the floods to see how many show post-traumatic stress symptoms. Option c is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the study took place in the field. The design has known weaknesses: people were not randomly exposed, other differences between groups may explain the results, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused what followed (the post hoc fallacy). Exam trap: the word \"field\" pulls students toward field experiment, but every experiment, in the laboratory or in the field, requires the researcher to manipulate the independent variable; without manipulation it is ex post facto research, not an experiment.", "traps": {"a": "A controlled (laboratory) experiment needs the researcher to manipulate the independent variable and hold other conditions constant, usually in a laboratory; nobody can manipulate a flood.", "b": "A field experiment still manipulates an independent variable, only in a natural setting (for example, randomly giving one village a counselling programme); here the flood was not manipulated, so this is not an experiment.", "d": "Psychometric research builds and evaluates tests and scales (reliability, validity, norms); the aim here is to study the consequences of an event, not to construct a measure."}}, {"q": "Memory can be distorted by being influenced by the individual's expectation rather than what has actually happened. The term is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: memory distorted by what a person expected, rather than by what actually happened, is described here as confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice, interpret and remember information that fits what we already believe or expect, and to overlook what does not. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task: people guessed a rule such as \"numbers rising by two\" and tested only sequences that fitted their guess, so they never found the true rule (any increasing numbers). The same bias acts on memory. Expectations work like a filter when we store an event and like a template when we recall it, so details that fit are kept and sharpened while details that clash are dropped or bent. Suppose Kabir expects his new colleague Riya to be careless. After a month he clearly recalls the one report she submitted late but has no memory of the five she submitted early. The broader mechanism is reconstructive memory: Frederic Bartlett (1932), in Remembering, found that English students retelling the Native American story \"The War of the Ghosts\" reshaped it to fit their own cultural expectations, for instance turning canoes into boats. Option a is right because it is the only option that names a distortion driven by expectation; the other three concern mood or vivid emotional events. Exam trap: mood-dependent memory and the mood-congruence effect also bend recall, but their driver is the person's emotional state, not a belief about what should have happened. Key note: the stricter textbook name for memory reshaped by expectations is schema-driven, reconstructive memory (Bartlett, 1932); confirmation bias is the broader tendency to seek, notice and recall what fits our expectations. It is the only option about expectations, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Mood-dependent memory (Gordon Bower, 1981) means recall is better when the mood at recall matches the mood at learning; it is driven by emotional state, not by expectations.", "c": "The mood-congruence effect means we more easily notice and recall material whose emotional tone matches our current mood (a sad person recalls sad events); again mood, not expectation, is the cause.", "d": "Flashbulb memory (Roger Brown and James Kulik, 1977) is a vivid, confident memory of the circumstances in which one heard shocking news; it is a kind of memory, not a distortion produced by expectation."}}, {"q": "According to Elisabeth Kubler-Ross, most people experience predictable stages when they face impending death. Which is the correct sequence of the stages?", "concept": "The answer is b: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) gave the sequence denial, anger, bargaining, depression (here called sadness) and acceptance. Kubler-Ross, a Swiss-American psychiatrist, interviewed terminally ill hospital patients in Chicago and described, in On Death and Dying, how people often react on learning that they are dying. First comes denial (\"This cannot be happening; the reports must be wrong\"), which cushions the shock. As denial fails, anger appears (\"Why me?\"), often aimed at doctors, family or God. Next is bargaining, an attempt to postpone death by striking a deal (\"If I live to see my daughter's wedding, I will ask for nothing more\"). When the illness can no longer be denied, depression or sadness sets in, over losses already suffered and losses still to come. Finally, many reach acceptance, a calm, sometimes tired readiness. A memory aid is DABDA. For example, Meera's father, told he has advanced cancer, first insists the scan was mixed up, then shouts at the nurses, then promises to give up smoking if he can attend his grandson's naming ceremony, then withdraws and weeps, and at last calmly starts settling his affairs. Option b is right because it is the only option that lists the five stages in Kubler-Ross's order. Her work helped launch the hospice and palliative care movement and changed how doctors speak to dying patients. Exam trap: the stages are not a fixed ladder; Kubler-Ross herself said people can skip stages, move back and forth or feel two at once, and later research has not confirmed a set sequence. That caution does not change the textbook order, which is always DABDA, so any option that starts with anger, bargaining or acceptance is wrong.", "traps": {"a": "Anger, sadness, bargaining, denial, acceptance moves denial, the first reaction that cushions the shock, to fourth place; in Kubler-Ross's order denial comes before anger.", "c": "Bargaining, anger, denial, sadness, acceptance puts bargaining first, but bargaining is the third stage, an attempt to buy time that follows denial and anger.", "d": "Acceptance, anger, bargaining, denial, sadness begins with the final stage; in Kubler-Ross's model acceptance is where the process ends, not where it starts."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big Five personality factors?", "concept": "The answer is c: submissiveness is not one of the Big Five personality factors. The Big Five, or Five-Factor Model, is the most widely accepted map of broad personality traits. It grew from the lexical hypothesis: the important ways people differ become words in everyday language, so factor-analysing trait words should reveal the main dimensions. Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) found five factors in US Air Force ratings, Warren Norman (1963) confirmed them, and Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five. Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) reached the same five with questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory (revised as the NEO-PI-R, 1992). Remember them as OCEAN: Openness to experience (curious, imaginative), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable), Extraversion (sociable, energetic), Agreeableness (kind, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious and moody, as against emotionally stable). Each is a continuum, and most people score near the middle. For example, Arjun may score high on conscientiousness, never missing a deadline, but low on extraversion, preferring a quiet evening at home. Submissiveness, a tendency to yield to others and avoid confrontation, is a narrower quality. In the NEO-PI-R, assertiveness is a facet of extraversion and compliance is a facet of agreeableness, so submissiveness overlaps with pieces of two factors but is not a factor itself. Option c is right because extraversion, agreeableness and openness to experience are three of the five named factors, while submissiveness is not on the list. Exam trap: dominance versus submissiveness does appear in other trait systems, for example as factor E (dominance) in Raymond Cattell's 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949), so it sounds like a standard trait; it is still not one of the Big Five.", "traps": {"a": "Extraversion (sociable, talkative, energetic versus reserved) is the E in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five.", "b": "Agreeableness (trusting, kind, cooperative versus antagonistic) is the A in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five.", "d": "Openness to experience (curious, imaginative, open to new ideas versus conventional) is the O in OCEAN, so it is one of the Big Five."}}, {"q": "The tendency to organize things to make a balanced or symmetrical figure that includes all parts is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the tendency to organise what we see into the most balanced, symmetrical and complete figure is the law of good figure, also called the law of Pragnanz. It is the master principle of Gestalt psychology, the German school which held that we perceive organised wholes rather than collections of separate elements. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual grouping, and Kurt Koffka (1935), in Principles of Gestalt Psychology, stated Pragnanz as the rule behind them: every pattern is seen in the simplest, most regular, stable and symmetrical organisation that the stimulus allows. \"Pragnanz\" is German for conciseness or good form. Two overlapping rings, for instance, are seen as two complete circles, not as three odd-shaped pieces. When Neha glances at the Olympic rings she sees five neat circles linked together, not a tangle of curved fragments. The other grouping laws can be read as special cases of this pull toward good form: proximity (near items group), similarity (alike items group), continuation (items on a smooth line or curve group) and closure (gaps are filled). Option c is right because balance, symmetry and the simplest complete form are exactly what good figure describes. Exam trap: the phrase \"includes all parts\" pulls students toward closure, but closure is narrower, the filling-in of gaps in an incomplete outline. Key note: closure is the near miss because of \"includes all parts\"; closure is filling gaps so a broken figure looks whole, while the drive toward the balanced, symmetrical, simplest form is the definition of good figure, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The principle of continuation (good continuation) says elements lying on a smooth, continuous line or curve are seen as belonging together; it concerns smooth paths, not the balance and symmetry of a whole figure.", "b": "The principle of similarity says elements alike in colour, shape or size are grouped together, such as rows of same-coloured dots; it is not about forming a balanced whole.", "d": "The law of closure says we fill in gaps so an incomplete outline, such as a broken circle, is seen as complete; it is the near miss, but it is about filling gaps, not about the most balanced and symmetrical organisation."}}, {"q": "As per ICD-10 Classification of mental and behavioural disorders what is the diagnostic code for Schizophrenia:", "concept": "The answer is a: in ICD-10, schizophrenia is coded F20. The International Classification of Diseases, tenth revision, published by the World Health Organization as The ICD-10 Classification of Mental and Behavioural Disorders (1992), puts mental disorders in chapter V, with codes beginning with F. The first digit after F marks the block: F0 organic disorders (including the dementias), F1 substance use, F2 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders, F3 mood disorders, F4 neurotic and stress-related disorders, F5 behavioural syndromes with physical factors (such as eating and sleep disorders), F6 personality disorders, F7 mental retardation, F8 developmental disorders and F9 behavioural and emotional disorders of childhood. Inside the F2 block, F20 is schizophrenia (with subtypes such as F20.0 paranoid), F21 schizotypal disorder, F22 persistent delusional disorders, F23 acute and transient psychotic disorders and F25 schizoaffective disorders. So if Rohan's case file reads \"F20.0\", Rohan has a diagnosis of paranoid schizophrenia. Option a is right because F20 is the code that opens the psychotic block and stands for schizophrenia itself. Exam trap: examiners offer look-alike codes, such as F22 (delusional disorder) from the same block and F00 (dementia in Alzheimer's disease) from the organic block; also remember that ICD-11, in use from 2022, has a new coding system in which schizophrenia is 6A20.", "traps": {"b": "F90 is hyperkinetic disorders, the ICD-10 name for ADHD, in the F9 block of behavioural and emotional disorders with onset in childhood.", "c": "F00 is dementia in Alzheimer's disease, the first code of the F0 block of organic mental disorders.", "d": "F22 is persistent delusional disorders; it sits in the same F2 psychotic block as schizophrenia, which makes it the closest distractor."}}, {"q": "In research the following is not a type of sampling method:", "concept": "The answer is d: closure is not a sampling method; it is a Gestalt law of perception, the tendency to fill gaps so that an incomplete figure looks whole. Sampling is the way a researcher picks a smaller group, the sample, from the larger population so that results can be generalised. Methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen. Simple random sampling gives everyone an equal chance, like drawing names from a hat. Systematic sampling picks every kth person from a list after a random start: from a list of 1,000 students, choose a random number from 1 to 10, then take every 10th name after it. Stratified sampling splits the population into subgroups (strata) such as gender or school type, then samples randomly within each, so every subgroup is properly represented. In non-probability sampling, selection is not random. Snowball sampling, or chain referral, asks each participant to name others like them, which suits hidden groups such as drug users. For example, Priya, studying homeless people who inject drugs in Kolkata, finds three participants through a clinic, and each introduces her to more. Option d is right because snowball, systematic and stratified are all recognised sampling methods, while closure belongs to the psychology of perception. Exam trap: examiners slip in a familiar term from another chapter; do not confuse closure with cluster sampling, in which whole groups such as schools are selected at random.", "traps": {"a": "Snowball sampling is a non-probability method in which participants refer others (chain referral), used for hard-to-reach groups; it is a genuine sampling method.", "b": "Systematic sampling is a probability method that takes every kth case from a list after a random start; it is a genuine sampling method.", "c": "Stratified sampling divides the population into subgroups (strata) and samples randomly within each so that every subgroup is represented; it is a genuine sampling method."}}, {"q": "In WAIS-IV which of the following subtest belongs to the Perceptual Reasoning Index scale?", "concept": "The answer is b: Visual Puzzles is a core subtest of the WAIS-IV Perceptual Reasoning Index. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, fourth edition (WAIS-IV, 2008), descends from David Wechsler's Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939) and gives a Full Scale IQ plus four index scores. Verbal Comprehension measures verbal reasoning and knowledge (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information; Comprehension supplemental). Perceptual Reasoning measures non-verbal, visual-spatial reasoning: its core subtests are Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles, with Figure Weights and Picture Completion supplemental. Working Memory measures holding and manipulating information in mind (Digit Span, Arithmetic; Letter-Number Sequencing supplemental). Processing Speed measures quick visual scanning and copying (Symbol Search, Coding; Cancellation supplemental). In Visual Puzzles the person looks at a completed design and must choose, within a time limit, the three pieces that together would make it, which means mentally rotating and fitting shapes without touching any blocks. For example, Aditi sees a square design and picks three of six fragments that would rebuild it; she is using spatial reasoning, not words or memory span. Visual Puzzles, Figure Weights and Cancellation were new subtests in the WAIS-IV. Option b is right because Visual Puzzles is one of the three core Perceptual Reasoning subtests. Exam trap: Digit Span and Letter-Number Sequencing feel like puzzles, but they belong to Working Memory, and Symbol Search, though visual, belongs to Processing Speed because it is scored for speed.", "traps": {"a": "Digit Span (repeating digits forwards, backwards and in ascending order) is a core subtest of the Working Memory Index, not Perceptual Reasoning.", "c": "Symbol Search (scanning groups of symbols for a match under time pressure) is a core subtest of the Processing Speed Index, together with Coding.", "d": "Letter-Number Sequencing (reordering a mixed string of letters and numbers) is a supplemental subtest of the Working Memory Index."}}, {"q": "According to John Bowlby what are the 4 types of attachment styles?", "concept": "The answer is a: the four attachment styles are secure, anxious-ambivalent, disorganised and avoidant. John Bowlby (1969), a British psychiatrist, gave the theory in Attachment: infants are born with an evolved need to stay close to a caregiver, who becomes a secure base for exploring and a safe haven in danger, and from early care the child builds an internal working model of self and others. The styles themselves come from Mary Ainsworth (1978), Bowlby's colleague, who measured them with the Strange Situation, reported with Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall in Patterns of Attachment. A 1-year-old goes through brief separations from and reunions with the mother, with a stranger present at times. Securely attached babies are upset by separation and comforted quickly at reunion. Avoidant (anxious-avoidant) babies show little distress and ignore the mother when she returns. Anxious-ambivalent (resistant) babies are very distressed and, at reunion, both seek and angrily resist contact. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) later added disorganised attachment: contradictory, confused behaviour such as freezing or approaching with the face turned away, often linked to frightening care. For example, when Tara's mother returns, Tara runs to her, settles within a minute and goes back to her blocks, which is secure attachment. Option a is right because it lists the four patterns, one secure and three insecure, without adding an invented type. Exam trap: \"insecure\" is the umbrella for the three non-secure patterns, not a fourth type, so any list that counts it beside the others is wrong. Key note: options a and c name the same four patterns, and c simply uses Ainsworth's formal label \"anxious-avoidant\"; a is the wording this question is normally answered with, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Secure, insecure, anxious-avoidant and disorganised wrongly counts \"insecure\" as a separate style; insecure is the umbrella for the avoidant, ambivalent and disorganised patterns, and this list leaves out anxious-ambivalent.", "c": "Secure, anxious-ambivalent, anxious-avoidant and disorganised names the same four patterns using Ainsworth's formal label \"anxious-avoidant\"; it is the near miss, but a is the standard wording for this question.", "d": "Secure, insecure, approachable and avoidant lists \"insecure\" as a type and invents \"approachable\", which is not an attachment category in Bowlby's or Ainsworth's work."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a specific interpersonal problem area in IPT?", "concept": "The answer is a: interpersonal effectiveness is not an IPT problem area; it is a skills module of dialectical behaviour therapy. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984) in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression. Drawing on the interpersonal ideas of Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) and on attachment theory, it treats depression as an illness that both affects and is affected by current relationships, instead of digging into childhood conflict. It usually runs 12 to 16 weekly sessions. Early on, therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas to work on. Grief: complicated mourning after a death. Interpersonal role disputes: conflict with a spouse, parent, boss or friend whose expectations do not match the patient's. Role transitions: difficulty adapting to a life change such as marriage, retirement, migration or parenthood. Interpersonal deficits: social isolation and a lack of lasting relationships. For example, Sunita became depressed after moving from Patna to Bengaluru for her husband's job and losing her friends and her own work; the IPT focus would be role transition. Interpersonal effectiveness is one of the four skills modules of dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), which Marsha Linehan (1993) designed for borderline personality disorder: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, which teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect. Option a is right because interpersonal effectiveness is a DBT skill set, not one of IPT's four areas. Exam trap: the word \"interpersonal\" appears in both therapies, so the distractor sounds like IPT; learn the IPT four as grief, disputes, transitions and deficits.", "traps": {"b": "Interpersonal deficits is one of IPT's four problem areas, chosen when the patient is socially isolated or has few lasting relationships.", "c": "Interpersonal role disputes is one of IPT's four problem areas: a conflict with a significant other over mismatched expectations.", "d": "Role transition is one of IPT's four problem areas: trouble adjusting to a life change such as a new job, marriage, retirement or migration."}}, {"q": "Parameter is characteristic of:", "concept": "The answer is b: a parameter is a numerical characteristic of a population. The population is the complete set of people or observations a researcher is interested in, for example every Class 10 student in Kerala. A value that describes that whole set, such as their true mean mathematics score, is a parameter. Because populations are usually too large to measure, researchers draw a sample and calculate the same value in it; a value computed from a sample is a statistic. A simple memory aid: Population goes with Parameter, Sample goes with Statistic. Parameters are written with Greek letters: mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation and rho for the population correlation. Statistics use Roman letters: x-bar for the sample mean, s for the standard deviation and r for the correlation. Inferential statistics uses statistics to estimate parameters and to test hypotheses about them. For example, Rahul tests 400 of those students and finds a mean of 62; that 62 is a statistic, his estimate of the unknown parameter mu. Option b is right because a parameter, by definition, describes the population. Exam trap: the two words are often reversed; a sample value is a statistic, never a parameter, and a parameter is usually unknown and fixed, while a statistic varies from sample to sample.", "traps": {"a": "A value describing a sample is a statistic (x-bar, s, r), not a parameter; this option reverses the two terms.", "c": "A characteristic of one individual is just a score or observation; parameters summarise a whole population, not a single person.", "d": "A test is a measuring instrument; item response theory does speak of item parameters (difficulty, discrimination), but in the basic population versus sample distinction a parameter is a population value."}}, {"q": "For which psychiatric illness is ERP best suggested for?", "concept": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, obsessions (intrusive thoughts, images or urges, such as a fear of contamination) create anxiety, and compulsions (washing, checking, counting, mental rituals) bring brief relief. That relief negatively reinforces the ritual and stops the person from learning that the feared outcome would not happen, or that the anxiety would fade on its own. Victor Meyer (1966), working in London, treated hospitalised patients with OCD by exposing them to what they feared while preventing their rituals, and showed that the rituals could be reduced. Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986) later explained how it works through emotional processing theory, and Foa's group tested it in clinical trials. ERP has two parts. Exposure: the person faces the feared cue, in real life or in imagination, usually climbing a graded hierarchy from mildly to highly distressing items. Response prevention: the person then refrains from the compulsion. Anxiety rises, peaks and declines through habituation and extinction, and the person learns that the feared disaster does not follow. For example, Farhan, who washes his hands 50 times a day, touches a door handle and then waits without washing; across sessions his urge weakens. ERP is often combined with SSRIs such as fluoxetine, which have their own evidence in OCD. Option b is right because ERP was built for OCD and has its strongest evidence there. Exam trap: exposure alone is used for phobias, PTSD and panic, but the response prevention half, blocking the compulsion, is what ties ERP specifically to OCD.", "traps": {"a": "Paranoid schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotic medication plus psychosocial work such as CBT for psychosis, family intervention and social skills training; ERP is not its standard therapy.", "c": "Substance dependence is treated with motivational interviewing, relapse prevention, contingency management and cue exposure; ERP as a named package is the OCD treatment.", "d": "Depression is treated with cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck), behavioural activation, interpersonal psychotherapy and antidepressants; it has no compulsion to block, so ERP is not the suggested therapy."}}, {"q": "The Id, according to Freud, is driven by aggressive and sexual instincts. This corresponds to Jung's archetype called:", "concept": "The answer is a: Jung's shadow archetype is the closest counterpart to Freud's id. Carl Jung broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and built his own analytical psychology. He kept a personal unconscious of forgotten and repressed experience but added beneath it a collective unconscious, an inherited layer shared by all humans, whose contents are archetypes: universal patterns that recur in myths, dreams and art. The main archetypes are the persona, the shadow, the anima and animus, and the Self. The shadow is the dark, primitive, animal side of the personality: the selfish, aggressive and sexual impulses a person does not want to own. Jung traced it to our pre-human, animal past, which is why it best matches the id, which Freud described in The Ego and the Id (1923) as the reservoir of sexual and aggressive drives working on the pleasure principle. Unlike the id, though, the shadow also holds vitality, spontaneity and creativity, and Jung wanted it accepted and integrated, not merely controlled. We often meet our own shadow by projecting it: Vikram, who cannot admit his own jealousy, sees jealousy in all his colleagues and despises them for it. In stories the shadow appears as the villain, the devil or the monster. Option a is right because the shadow, the instinctual dark side, is the Jungian match for the id's sexual and aggressive instincts. Exam trap: the persona sounds important but is the opposite, the polished social mask we show the world; and \"evil force\" describes how the shadow may feel, but it is not the name of an archetype.", "traps": {"b": "The persona is Jung's social mask, the public face we put on to meet society's expectations; it hides the instinctual side rather than corresponding to the id.", "c": "\"Evil force\" is not a Jungian archetype; the shadow may be pictured as the devil or a monster, but Jung's term for the id-like side is the shadow.", "d": "\"Sun\" is not one of Jung's named archetypes (persona, shadow, anima, animus, Self, wise old man, great mother, hero); the sun can appear as a symbol in myths and dreams, but it is not the counterpart of the id."}}, {"q": "According to Beck's cognitive triad, one has negative views about:", "concept": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a negative view of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Beck (1967), an American psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that the thoughts and dreams of his depressed patients kept returning to themes of defeat and loss. He built a cognitive model of depression and later a treatment manual, Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). The triad is the content of depressive thinking. The self is seen as worthless, inadequate or unlovable (\"I am a failure\"). The world, meaning other people and present experience, is seen as unfair, demanding or full of obstacles (\"Nobody cares; everything goes wrong\"). The future is seen as hopeless (\"It will never get better\"). These views surface as automatic negative thoughts, are rooted in dysfunctional schemas formed in childhood, and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction and magnification. The hopeless view of the future is the strongest link to suicide risk, which is why Beck also built a hopelessness scale. For example, Ishaan fails one interview and thinks, \"I am useless (self), companies only hire people with contacts (world), and I will be jobless forever (future)\". In therapy he learns to catch, test and replace these thoughts with more balanced ones. Option b is right because it names exactly the three targets of the triad. Exam trap: the distractors swap in time words; the triad has only one time element, the future, and neither the past nor the present is a corner of it.", "traps": {"a": "Past, present and future is a time-based distractor; Beck's triad is about the self, the world and the future, and only the future is a time element.", "c": "Self, past and future keeps two correct corners but replaces the world with the past; rumination about the past is common in depression, but it is not part of the triad.", "d": "Self, world and present swaps the future for the present; hopelessness about the future is the corner most linked to suicide risk."}}, {"q": "Kurt Lewin's approach to personality was a:", "concept": "The answer is a: Kurt Lewin's approach to personality was a Gestalt approach. Kurt Lewin (1935) trained and worked at the Psychological Institute of the University of Berlin, the centre of the Gestalt school, and carried its central idea, that the whole field determines each part, from perception into personality, motivation and social behaviour. His field theory, set out in A Dynamic Theory of Personality (1935) and Principles of Topological Psychology (1936), says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment together: B = f(P, E). Person and environment make up the life space, the total of all the facts that influence a person at a given moment, as that person sees them. Lewin drew it as a map, with regions (goals, activities), boundaries and barriers between them, and vectors (forces) pulling toward goals of positive valence and away from those of negative valence. Tension in a region of the person persists until the need is met; his student Bluma Zeigarnik (1927) found that unfinished tasks are remembered better than finished ones. As in Gestalt perception, behaviour is understood from the whole present situation, not from past habits or single elements. For example, whether Pooja studies tonight depends on the whole field at that moment: the pull of the coming exam, the barrier of tiredness and the counter-pull of a friend's wedding. Option a is right because Lewin's field theory applies Gestalt principles of wholes and dynamic fields to personality. Exam trap: Lewin is also called the father of modern social psychology (group dynamics, leadership styles, action research), so students pick social learning; but social learning theory belongs to John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941) and Albert Bandura (1977), and Lewin's stress on the present field sets him against stimulus-response behaviourism.", "traps": {"b": "A behavioural approach (John B. Watson, 1913; B. F. Skinner, 1938) explains behaviour by learned stimulus-response links and reinforcement history; Lewin explained it by the whole present psychological field.", "c": "The social learning approach belongs to John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941) and Albert Bandura (observational learning); Lewin helped found social psychology, but his personality theory is Gestalt field theory, not social learning.", "d": "\"Deterministic approach\" is not a school of personality; nearly every scientific theory assumes behaviour has causes, and the label usually points to Freud's psychic determinism, so it does not name Lewin's approach."}}, {"q": "The phenomenon of development of Super-Ego, according to Freud is a consequence of:", "concept": "The answer is b: according to Sigmund Freud (1923), the superego develops as a consequence of resolving the Oedipus complex (the Electra complex in girls). In The Ego and the Id (1923) Freud divided the mind into three parts: the id (inborn drives, run by the pleasure principle), the ego (the realistic executive, run by the reality principle) and the superego (the moral arm). The superego is not present at birth. It forms in the phallic stage, at about 5 to 6, out of a family drama. The boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival; fearing punishment (castration anxiety), he gives up the wish and instead identifies with his father, taking the father's standards into himself. The girl, in Freud's account, turns to her father and resolves the conflict, less completely, by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) named the girl's version the Electra complex, a term Freud himself did not adopt. Through this identification, control that once came from outside becomes inner self-control, which is why Freud called the superego the heir of the Oedipus complex. It has two parts: the conscience, which punishes with guilt, and the ego ideal, which rewards with pride. Example: six-year-old Arjun no longer needs his father in the room to stop himself from taking extra sweets; a voice inside him now says it is wrong. Option b is right because Freud tied the birth of the superego directly to the resolution of the Oedipal conflict. Exam trap: option a sounds Freudian but reverses the theory; the child identifies with the same-sex parent, while the opposite-sex parent is the object of desire that has to be given up.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses Freud: the opposite-sex parent is the object of the child's desire, and the superego comes from identification with the same-sex parent (the boy with his father, the girl with her mother).", "c": "Anatomical superiority is not a Freudian cause of the superego; it garbles ideas about anatomy such as penis envy or Alfred Adler's organ inferiority (1907), neither of which explains how conscience forms.", "d": "The superego does appear at about 5 to 6, but Freud tied it to resolving the Oedipal conflict, not to simply growing older; age alone does not produce a conscience in his theory."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "concept": "The answer is c: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting, because information that was never attended was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Forgetting means losing access to information that was once encoded into memory. The main explanations are these. Decay (trace decay) theory says memory traces fade with time and disuse, as in Edward Thorndike's (1914) law of disuse. Interference theory says other learning disrupts recall; John McGeoch (1932) argued that interference, not the mere passing of time, causes most forgetting. In proactive interference old learning blocks new learning; in retroactive interference new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, or cue-dependent forgetting (Endel Tulving, 1974), means the memory is stored but the right cue is missing, as in the tip-of-the-tongue state. Retrieval inhibition means recalling some items actively suppresses related ones; Michael Anderson (1994), with Robert and Elizabeth Bjork, called this retrieval-induced forgetting. Motivated forgetting (repression) and failed consolidation complete the list. Selective attention, by contrast, works at the very start, at encoding: it decides what enters memory in the first place, as in Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter model. Unattended material is never learned, so failing to recall it is a failure to encode, not forgetting. For example, if Riya was texting during a lecture and cannot recall its main point, she never encoded it; if she learned it well but cannot recall it a month later because of newer lectures on similar topics, that is interference. Option c is right because selective attention governs what gets encoded, not why stored information is later lost. Exam trap: retrieval inhibition sounds like an attention process, but it is a genuine forgetting mechanism that acts on memories already stored.", "traps": {"a": "Interference (John McGeoch, 1932) is a major cause of forgetting: proactive and retroactive interference make stored memories harder to recall.", "b": "Decay theory holds that unused memory traces fade over time (Edward Thorndike's law of disuse), so it is a classic explanation of forgetting.", "d": "Retrieval inhibition is a real cause of forgetting: recalling some items suppresses related ones, shown by Michael Anderson (1994) as retrieval-induced forgetting."}}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "concept": "The answer is c: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1950), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson described eight psychosocial stages across the whole life span, each built around a crisis between two poles. In Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a favourable resolution leaves behind. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 12, the same period Sigmund Freud (1905) called latency. The child now meets tools, tasks, teachers and classmates to compare with. A child who finishes tasks, builds skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work. Repeated failure, harsh comparison or being labelled slow brings inferiority. The healthy outcome, competence, is the free use of skill and intelligence to complete tasks, not weighed down by a sense of inferiority. Learn the eight virtues in order: hope (trust versus mistrust), will (autonomy versus shame and doubt), purpose (initiative versus guilt), competence (industry versus inferiority), fidelity (identity versus role confusion), love (intimacy versus isolation), care (generativity versus stagnation) and wisdom (integrity versus despair). Example: nine-year-old Meera keeps failing at fractions until her teacher breaks them into small steps and praises each success; by the end of term she volunteers to solve problems at the board, showing the competence Erikson described. Option c is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: every option is a real Erikson virtue, so count the stages carefully; purpose belongs to stage 3, will to stage 2 and wisdom to stage 8.", "traps": {"a": "Purpose is the virtue of Erikson's third stage, initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6, the play age): the courage to pursue valued goals.", "b": "Will is the virtue of Erikson's second stage, autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3), built through toilet training and early self-control.", "d": "Wisdom is the virtue of Erikson's eighth and last stage, integrity versus despair (late adulthood): accepting the life one has lived in the face of death."}}, {"q": "Which view supports the contention that we are normally driven to eat by the anticipated pleasure of eating?", "concept": "The answer is c: positive-incentive theory holds that we are normally drawn to eat by the anticipated pleasure of eating. For most of the twentieth century, hunger was explained like a thermostat. In this set-point view, eating begins when energy stores fall below a fixed level and stops when they are restored, through negative feedback that keeps the body in homeostasis. Jean Mayer (1953) proposed the glucostatic version, with blood glucose as the regulated quantity, and Gordon Kennedy (1953) the lipostatic version, with body fat as the regulated quantity. The set-point view fits poorly with everyday eating: people usually eat long before any energy deficit exists, they keep eating when food is tasty or company is good, and it cannot explain why obesity has risen so fast where food is plentiful. Positive-incentive theory, argued by John Pinel and colleagues (2000) and taught in Pinel's textbook Biopsychology, reverses the direction. We are not pushed by an internal deficit but pulled by the expected pleasure (the positive-incentive value) of food, which depends on its taste, what we have learned about it, the time since we last ate, how much is already in the stomach and the social setting. Body weight then drifts to a settling point rather than being defended at a fixed set point. Example: Kabir has just finished lunch, yet when a colleague brings in hot jalebis he eats three, because the expected pleasure, not hunger, drives him. Option c is right because only positive-incentive theory makes anticipated pleasure the main cause of eating. Exam trap: the other three options are all parts of one deficit model; set point, negative feedback and homeostasis describe being pushed to eat, not pulled.", "traps": {"a": "Set-point theory (glucostatic, Jean Mayer, 1953; lipostatic, Gordon Kennedy, 1953) says we eat to correct an energy deficit below a fixed level; it is the push model that positive-incentive theory replaced.", "b": "A negative feedback system is the mechanism set-point theory assumes: falling energy switches eating on and restored energy switches it off, so eating is driven by deficit, not by anticipated pleasure.", "d": "Homeostasis (Walter Cannon, The Wisdom of the Body, 1932) is the general tendency to keep the internal environment stable; it frames eating as correcting an imbalance, not as pursuing pleasure."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following theories predicts that work motivation will be low if an employee perceives that an increased effort will have little, or no effect on his/her performance?", "concept": "The answer is c: expectancy theory predicts low work motivation when an employee believes that extra effort will make little or no difference to performance. Victor Vroom (1964), in Work and Motivation, proposed that people decide how much effort to put in by weighing three beliefs, which is why it is also called VIE theory. Expectancy is the belief that effort leads to performance (\"If I try harder, will I actually do better?\"). Instrumentality is the belief that performance leads to outcomes (\"If I do better, will I be rewarded?\"). Valence is how much the person values those outcomes. Vroom combined them by multiplication: motivational force equals expectancy times instrumentality times valence. Because they multiply, if any one of the three is close to zero, motivation collapses, however high the other two are. The stem describes near-zero expectancy: effort seems unrelated to performance. Lyman Porter and Edward Lawler (1968) later extended the model by adding ability, role clarity and satisfaction. Example: Arjun works in a call centre where a computer routes calls and largely decides his sales figures; since working harder does not change his numbers, his expectancy is near zero and he stops trying, even though the bonus is attractive. Managers raise expectancy through training, resources and clear, achievable goals. Option c is right because the effort-to-performance link is exactly what Vroom called expectancy. Exam trap: option d, normative theory, also carries Vroom's name, because the normative decision model of Victor Vroom and Philip Yetton (1973) tells leaders how far to involve subordinates in decisions; it is a leadership model, not a theory of work motivation.", "traps": {"a": "Just-world theory (Melvin Lerner, 1980) is the belief that people get what they deserve, used to explain victim blaming; it says nothing about whether effort raises performance.", "b": "Need-drive theory (Clark Hull, 1943, drive reduction) says behaviour is pushed by tension from unmet biological needs; it involves no beliefs about the link between effort and performance.", "d": "Normative theory here is the Vroom-Yetton normative decision model (Victor Vroom and Philip Yetton, 1973), which advises leaders how participative a decision should be; it is about leadership style, not work motivation."}}, {"q": "The defence of unconscious shifting of emotions from the real source of threat to a less threatening external object is usually known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: displacement is the unconscious shifting of an emotion from its real, threatening source to a safer, less threatening target. Sigmund Freud (1894) described defence mechanisms as unconscious ways the ego protects itself from anxiety, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, set them out systematically. In displacement the feeling itself (often anger, sometimes desire or fear) stays the same; only its object changes. The real target is too dangerous to face, perhaps because it is more powerful, loved or needed, so the impulse is discharged on someone or something that cannot hit back. Freud also saw displacement at work in dreams, where emotional weight moves from important to trivial details, and in phobias, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of his father was displaced onto horses. The classic everyday illustration is the man who is shouted at by his boss and goes home to kick the dog. Example: Riya's manager humiliates her in a meeting; she says nothing, but at home she snaps at her younger brother over the television remote. Option d is right because the stem defines displacement precisely: the emotion moves from the real source of threat to a less threatening external object. Exam trap: projection is the near miss examiners like, but projection attributes your own unacceptable impulse to another person (\"he hates me\"), whereas displacement keeps the impulse as yours and redirects it (\"I shout at him instead\").", "traps": {"a": "Dissociation (Pierre Janet, 1889) splits memories, identity or awareness off from consciousness, as in dissociative amnesia; it disconnects experience rather than moving an emotion to a new target.", "b": "Isolation (isolation of affect) strips the feeling away from a thought, so a painful event is recalled calmly, as in obsessional patients; the emotion is removed, not redirected to another object.", "c": "Conversion (Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud, Studies on Hysteria, 1895) turns psychological conflict into a bodily symptom such as paralysis or blindness; the outlet is one's own body, not an external object."}}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "concept": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "traps": {"a": "Otto Kernberg (1975) described borderline personality organisation and pathological narcissism built on splitting; his transference-focused psychotherapy aims to integrate split good and bad images through confrontation and interpretation, not Kohut's empathic building of a cohesive self.", "c": "David Malan, at the Tavistock Clinic, developed brief dynamic psychotherapy around the triangle of conflict (defence, anxiety, hidden feeling) and the triangle of person (others, therapist, parents); his focus is one circumscribed conflict, not self cohesion.", "d": "Mardi Horowitz described stress response syndromes (alternating intrusion and avoidance after trauma) and co-developed the Impact of Event Scale (1979); his work concerns processing traumatic events, not the fragmented self."}}, {"q": "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy is a type of __________ therapy.", "concept": "The answer is b: in the classic three-way grouping of psychotherapies by their aim, cognitive behaviour therapy is a re-educative therapy. Lewis Wolberg (1954), in The Technique of Psychotherapy, sorted therapies by how deep a change they seek. Supportive therapy aims to shore up the person's existing defences and restore everyday functioning, using reassurance, guidance, advice, persuasion and changes to the environment; it does not try to change personality. Re-educative therapy aims at deliberate change in attitudes, beliefs and behaviour, working mostly on conscious, present-day material through learning, practice and insight into current patterns. Behaviour therapy, cognitive therapy, counselling and many goal-focused approaches belong here. Reconstructive therapy aims to restructure the personality itself by bringing unconscious conflicts into awareness, as in psychoanalysis and long-term psychoanalytic psychotherapy. CBT fits the middle class neatly. Aaron Beck (1979) taught clients to spot and test their automatic thoughts, and behavioural tasks such as activity scheduling and graded exposure retrain habits. The aim is to learn new ways of thinking and acting, not to uncover childhood conflicts or merely prop up defences. Example: Kabir, 22, believes \"everyone will laugh if I speak in class\". His therapist helps him test the belief with small, planned speaking tasks; as the prediction fails, he relearns both the thought and the behaviour, which is re-education. Option b is right because CBT changes conscious thoughts and behaviour through structured learning, the defining feature of Wolberg's re-educative class. Exam trap: reconstructive sounds like cognitive restructuring, a core CBT technique, but in Wolberg's scheme reconstructive means deep personality change through psychoanalytic work.", "traps": {"a": "Reconstructive therapy, in Lewis Wolberg's scheme, seeks deep personality change by making unconscious conflict conscious, as in psychoanalysis; CBT works on present, conscious thoughts and behaviour.", "c": "Supportive therapy shores up existing defences with reassurance, advice and guidance without aiming at lasting change; CBT actively teaches new skills and ways of thinking, so it goes beyond support.", "d": "Metacognitive therapy (Adrian Wells, 2009) is a separate therapy that targets beliefs about thinking, such as worry about worry; it is a named treatment, not one of Wolberg's three classes."}}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on _________:", "concept": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists to one perfect recitation and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer.", "traps": {"a": "Perception was the territory of Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) and others; the Ebbinghaus illusion of size contrast carries his name, but his pioneering experimental programme was on memory.", "b": "Psychophysics was founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860); Ebbinghaus borrowed Fechner's quantitative spirit for memory but did not pioneer psychophysics.", "c": "The serial position effect (better recall of first and last items) was noticed by Ebbinghaus but studied systematically later (for example by Bennet Murdock, 1962); it is one finding inside his memory work, not the field he pioneered."}}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "concept": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1946). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist and neurologist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, set out his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from the Greek logos, meaning) holds that the deepest human motive is the will to meaning, rather than pleasure or power. When the search for meaning is blocked, a person falls into an existential vacuum and may develop what Frankl called a noogenic neurosis. He gave logotherapy two signature techniques. Dereflection treats hyper-reflection, excessive self-observation that makes a problem worse, as in insomnia, sexual difficulties or anxious self-monitoring. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or another person, so the symptom fades for want of attention. Paradoxical intention asks the client to wish for, or try to produce, the feared symptom, which breaks the cycle of anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or unavoidable suffering. Example: Meera lies awake every night checking whether sleep is coming; her therapist suggests she use that time to plan the dance class she teaches, and sleep arrives once she stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: dereflection and paradoxical intention are both Frankl's, so neither should be credited to Gestalt or person-centred therapy, even though all three are humanistic or existential in flavour. Key note: logotherapy is itself one of the existential therapies, so option a is the near miss; but dereflection is a named technique of Frankl's logotherapy specifically, and when the specific school is offered it is the better answer, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, 1958; Irvin Yalom, 1980) is the broad family that works with death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; logotherapy belongs to it, but dereflection is Frankl's own technique, so the specific school is the better answer.", "b": "Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, and avoids directive techniques such as dereflection.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, 1951) heightens here-and-now awareness through the empty chair and exaggeration; it turns attention towards experience, the opposite of dereflection."}}, {"q": "Consider a scenario where a salesperson knocks on someone's door and requests them to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his organisation. When he is turned down, he requests them to buy a portable ice box, and the customer feels obliged to comply. The technique that this salesperson has used is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the salesperson used the door-in-the-face technique, a large request that is refused, followed by the smaller request he really wanted. Robert Cialdini (1975) and colleagues demonstrated it with students approached on campus. When first asked to work as unpaid counsellors to juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years, almost all refused; when then asked to chaperone the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about half agreed, roughly three times the rate among students who heard only the small request. Two explanations are offered. Reciprocal concessions: the requester seems to have given ground, so the target feels obliged to give ground too. Perceptual contrast: after the big request, the small one looks modest. It works best when the same person makes both requests close together. Compare the other sequential techniques. Foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) runs the other way: a small request first, then a larger one, working through self-perception and consistency. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins a commitment first and then worsens the terms. In the stem the refrigerator is the big request, the portable ice box is the real target, and the customer \"feels obliged\", the mark of a reciprocal concession. Option c is right because the order is a refused large request followed by a smaller one, which defines door-in-the-face. Exam trap: students mix up the two door techniques; the foot goes in small and grows, while the door is slammed on the big request first. Key note: door-in-the-face works through reciprocal concessions, so reciprocity (d) is the principle behind it; but the named technique the scenario shows is door-in-the-face, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The low-ball technique (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) gets agreement to a deal and then worsens the terms; here the first request was refused, not accepted and then changed.", "b": "Foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) starts with a small request and moves to a larger one; the salesperson did the reverse, big first and small second.", "d": "Reciprocity is Robert Cialdini's general principle that we feel obliged to repay what others give us; it explains why door-in-the-face works, but the specific named technique in the scenario is door-in-the-face."}}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn better by catching and correcting errors, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John Flavell (1976) introduced the term, often glossed as \"thinking about thinking\", and his 1979 paper made it central to the study of cognitive development. It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what a person knows about their own mind, about tasks and about strategies, such as knowing that one remembers diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation (monitoring and control) is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, checking whether one has understood, and changing strategy when one has not. Its memory branch, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judgements of how well something has been learned. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success, which is why teaching students to self-test and explain material to themselves works. Example: Arjun notices he keeps missing psychophysics questions, realises that rereading his notes is not working, and switches to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition at work. Option c is right because the stem describes monitoring and correcting one's own thinking, which is the definition of metacognition. Exam trap: \"meta\" means one level above, so metacognition is cognition about cognition; it is not general intelligence, and it is not a problem-solving method such as an algorithm or an analogy, which are tools used by thought rather than awareness of thought.", "traps": {"a": "Analogies solve a new problem by mapping it onto a familiar one with a similar structure (as in Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak's 1980 radiation problem studies); this is a problem-solving strategy, not awareness of one's own thinking.", "b": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution if followed correctly, unlike a heuristic shortcut; it is a method of solving, not self-monitoring of thought.", "d": "Constructive process refers to memory and perception actively building and filling in information, as in Frederic Bartlett's (1932) reconstructive memory; it is not awareness of one's own thought."}}, {"q": "The famous textbook \"The Principles of Psychology\" was authored by:", "concept": "The answer is b: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). James, who trained in medicine at Harvard before turning to philosophy and psychology, worked on the book for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). The Principles described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness, called habit the \"enormous fly-wheel of society\", separated the self as knower (the \"I\") from the self as known (the \"me\"), and set out James's theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885): we feel afraid because we tremble, not the other way round. The book made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration for functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option b is right because James is the author of The Principles of Psychology. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (1873 to 1874) wrote the similar-sounding Principles of Physiological Psychology, the founding text of experimental psychology, so option a is the near miss built on a look-alike title.", "traps": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt wrote Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) and founded the Leipzig laboratory in 1879; the title looks similar, but The Principles of Psychology is James's.", "c": "Edward Titchener, Wundt's student and founder of structuralism at Cornell, wrote the laboratory manuals Experimental Psychology (1901 to 1905), not The Principles of Psychology.", "d": "Alan Carr is a modern Irish clinical psychologist known for textbooks on positive psychology and child and adolescent clinical psychology, written a century after James."}}, {"q": "Ivan Petrovich Pavlov received the Nobel Prize in 1904 for:", "concept": "The answer is c: Ivan Pavlov (1904) received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1904 for his work on the physiology of digestion. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, spent the 1880s and 1890s studying how the digestive glands (the salivary glands, stomach and pancreas) are controlled by the nervous system. He developed surgical preparations, such as fistulas and a small isolated pouch of the stomach, that let him collect secretions from healthy, conscious dogs over long periods instead of from anaesthetised animals. He was the first Russian to win a Nobel Prize. During this work he noticed that dogs began to salivate before food reached the mouth, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant's footsteps. He called these \"psychic secretions\", and his study of them grew into the theory of conditioned reflexes, the foundation of classical conditioning, published in English as Conditioned Reflexes (1927). Option c is right because the Nobel award honoured his digestion research, not his conditioning work. Exam trap: option b is the famous trap; conditioned reflexes made Pavlov famous in psychology, but that work came after the prize, which was given for digestion.", "traps": {"a": "There is no Nobel Prize for psychology; Pavlov's prize was in Physiology or Medicine, and he regarded himself as a physiologist, not a psychologist.", "b": "Conditioned reflexes grew out of his digestion research and made him famous in psychology, but the Nobel award of 1904 honoured the digestive physiology, not conditioning.", "d": "Pavlov did experiment on animals, chiefly dogs, but the prize honoured a specific discovery, how the digestive glands work, not animal experimentation as such."}}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "concept": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "traps": {"b": "Edwin Ray Guthrie was a neobehaviourist known for contiguity theory and one-trial learning (The Psychology of Learning, 1935), so he belongs to behaviourism.", "c": "Burrhus Frederic Skinner was the leading radical behaviourist: operant conditioning, the Skinner box and schedules of reinforcement.", "d": "Clark Leonard Hull was a neobehaviourist whose drive-reduction theory (Principles of Behavior, 1943) defined reaction potential as habit strength times drive."}}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "concept": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are the three neurotic trends of Karen Horney (1945). Horney, a German-born psychoanalyst who moved to the United States, argued in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time (1937) that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships and culture rather than by sexual instincts. A child whose parents are cold, inconsistent, dominating or rejecting feels basic hostility, represses it out of fear or dependence, and is left with basic anxiety, the feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child develops strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, listed in Self-Analysis (1942), such as needs for affection and approval, a partner to take over one's life, power, prestige, admiration, achievement, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval. The aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and control. The detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and privacy. A healthy person uses all three flexibly as situations demand; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among the trends is the core of neurosis. Example: Riya agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), her cousin Kabir turns every chat into a contest (against), and his brother Arjun avoids company altogether (away). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Alfred Adler (1912) also built a social theory of neurosis around inferiority feelings and the striving to overcome them, and later sorted people into ruling, getting, avoiding and socially useful types, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "traps": {"a": "John Dollard and Neal Miller (Personality and Psychotherapy, 1950) translated Freud into learning theory (drive, cue, response, reward, approach-avoidance conflict); they did not classify needs as movements towards, against or away from people.", "b": "Max Wertheimer (1912) founded Gestalt psychology with the phi phenomenon and the laws of perceptual grouping; he wrote about perception and productive thinking, not neurotic needs.", "d": "Alfred Adler's individual psychology centres on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, social interest and style of life; his types (ruling, getting, avoiding, socially useful) carry different names from Horney's three movements."}}, {"q": "The need to submit passively to others, to seek and accept injury, blame and criticism is also known as the need for:", "concept": "The answer is a: the need to submit passively to others and to seek or accept injury, blame and criticism is n Abasement in the list of Henry Murray (1938). In Explorations in Personality (1938), written with colleagues at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, Murray proposed that behaviour is driven by needs interacting with press, the forces in the environment that help or block them. He separated viscerogenic (bodily) needs, such as food and water, from psychogenic (psychological) needs (textbooks usually list about twenty), each written with an \"n\", such as n Achievement, n Affiliation, n Dominance, n Autonomy, n Nurturance and n Exhibition. Each need comes with a short behavioural definition, and the stem paraphrases Murray's definition of abasement: to submit passively to external force, to accept injury, blame, criticism and punishment, to surrender, to admit inferiority and error, and to blame or belittle oneself. Murray's needs are measured by the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), in which stories told about ambiguous pictures reveal needs and press, and by the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (Allen Edwards, 1959), a forced-choice inventory of fifteen of Murray's needs. Example: Meera apologises even when a colleague made the mistake and accepts unfair blame without protest, a pattern of high n Abasement. Option a is right because the stem is the textbook definition of abasement. Exam trap: succorance sounds as if it involves suffering, but it means seeking help, sympathy and care, not seeking blame.", "traps": {"b": "Succorance is Murray's need to have one's needs met by a sympathetic other: to be nursed, supported, protected and consoled; it seeks care, not blame or injury.", "c": "Order is Murray's need to put things in order and to achieve cleanliness, arrangement, balance and precision; it has nothing to do with submission.", "d": "Defence (Murray's n Defendance) is the need to defend oneself against attack, criticism and blame and to justify one's actions; it is the opposite of accepting blame."}}, {"q": "'Animistic thinking', or the tendency to ascribe life to non-living things, is found in the ______ stage of Piaget's development:", "concept": "The answer is a: animistic thinking, the belief that non-living things are alive and have feelings and intentions, belongs to the preoperational stage of Jean Piaget (1929). Piaget described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (about 11 or 12 onwards). In The Child's Conception of the World (1929) he reported interviews in which young children said that the sun follows them, that the wind knows it is blowing and that a table feels hurt when bumped. He traced animism to egocentrism: the child assumes everything works the way he or she does, so things that move or seem active must be alive and have purposes. Animism shrinks in steps, from crediting life to anything active or useful, to anything that moves, to things that move by themselves, and finally to plants and animals only. It sits alongside other preoperational features: artificialism (the belief that natural things such as lakes and mountains were made by people), egocentrism, centration and failure of conservation. Example: four-year-old Kabir scolds the chair he tripped over, saying \"Bad chair, you hurt me!\", and tells his mother that the moon is following the car. Option a is right because animism is a hallmark of preoperational thought. Exam trap: the sensorimotor stage (d) comes just before, but infants in it do not yet have symbolic thought, so they cannot hold beliefs about whether objects are alive; animism needs the mental representation that only begins at about 2.", "traps": {"b": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) brings logical thought about concrete objects, conservation and classification; by now children mostly reserve life for plants and animals, so animism has faded.", "c": "The formal operational stage (about 11 or 12 onwards) brings abstract, hypothetical-deductive reasoning; animistic beliefs have long since gone.", "d": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is thinking through action and the senses, building object permanence; without symbolic representation the infant cannot yet reason that a chair has feelings."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is not considered as a form of third wave therapies?", "concept": "The answer is d: classic behaviour therapy is the first wave, not a third-wave therapy. Steven Hayes (2004) described the history of behavioural and cognitive treatment as three waves. The first wave, behaviour therapy, took off in the 1950s and 1960s and applied learning principles directly to observable behaviour: systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958), exposure, and operant methods such as token economies built on the work of B. F. Skinner (1953). The second wave, cognitive therapy, added thoughts as targets in the 1960s and 1970s: Albert Ellis (1962) and Aaron Beck (1979) taught clients to identify and dispute irrational or distorted beliefs, and the merger of the two waves became CBT. The third wave, from the 1990s, changes the person's relationship to thoughts and feelings rather than their content, using mindfulness, acceptance, values and dialectics. Its members include acceptance and commitment therapy (Steven Hayes, 1999), dialectical behaviour therapy (Marsha Linehan, 1993), mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002) and other mindfulness-based interventions such as mindfulness-based stress reduction (Jon Kabat-Zinn, 1979). Example: when Riya thinks \"I am a failure\", a second-wave therapist asks her to examine the evidence, while an ACT therapist asks her to notice it as just a thought and act on her values anyway. Option d is right because behaviour therapy is the original first wave, the base from which the later waves grew. Exam trap: DBT keeps \"behaviour therapy\" in its name, so students sometimes pick it, but it is a core third-wave therapy, while plain behaviour therapy is the one that does not belong.", "traps": {"a": "Dialectical behaviour therapy (Marsha Linehan, 1993), built for borderline personality disorder, balances acceptance and change through mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness; it is a core third-wave therapy despite its name.", "b": "Mindfulness-based interventions such as MBSR (Jon Kabat-Zinn, 1979) and MBCT (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002) train non-judgemental, present-moment awareness and are central to the third wave.", "c": "Acceptance and commitment therapy (Steven Hayes, 1999) uses acceptance, defusion and values-based action to build psychological flexibility; Hayes himself named the third wave, and ACT is its flagship."}}, {"q": "The famous book \"Feminine Psychology\" is credited to the author:", "concept": "The answer is b: Feminine Psychology is a book by Karen Horney (1967), published after her death. Harold Kelman (1967) edited it from the papers on women that Horney wrote between 1922 and 1937, while she was a psychoanalyst first in Berlin and then in the United States. In those papers she challenged the account of female development given by Sigmund Freud (1925). She argued that penis envy was a theory seen through male eyes and drawn from neurotic patients, and that women's feelings of inferiority came from culture, such as economic dependence and narrow social roles, not from anatomy. She turned the argument round with womb envy: men may envy women's power to bear children and make up for it through achievement. Essays such as \"The Flight from Womanhood\" (1926) and \"The Overvaluation of Love\" (1934) are collected in the book. Option b is right because Feminine Psychology gathers Horney's essays on the psychology of women. Exam trap: all four options are famous psychoanalytic authors with famous books, so match each to its own title: Anna Freud's classic is on defences, Melanie Klein's on child analysis and Erik Erikson's on the life cycle.", "traps": {"a": "Erik Erikson wrote Childhood and Society (1950), which set out his eight psychosocial stages; he did not write Feminine Psychology.", "c": "Anna Freud wrote The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936), the classic catalogue of defences, and pioneered child analysis; Feminine Psychology is Horney's.", "d": "Melanie Klein wrote The Psycho-Analysis of Children (1932), founding play technique and object relations theory; her work centres on the infant's inner objects, not a critique of penis envy."}}, {"q": "A therapist suggests to a patient that each time he begins to feel anxious he should breathe deeply and imagine himself in a relaxing place. What principle could underlie this suggestion?", "concept": "The answer is c: pairing deep breathing and a relaxing image with the first signs of anxiety rests on reciprocal inhibition. Joseph Wolpe (1958), a South African psychiatrist, set out the principle in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition: if a response incompatible with anxiety is made to occur in the presence of anxiety-provoking cues, it weakens the bond between those cues and anxiety. A person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment, because calm (parasympathetic) and fear (sympathetic) states work against each other. Wolpe borrowed the term from the physiologist Charles Sherrington (1906), who showed that when one muscle in a reflex contracts, its opposing muscle is inhibited. His main competing response was relaxation, drawn from the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1929); assertive and sexual responses could also serve. The principle underlies systematic desensitisation: the client learns relaxation, builds an anxiety hierarchy from the least to the most feared situation, and meets each step while relaxed until it no longer brings anxiety. Mary Cover Jones (1924) had used a similar idea when she fed little Peter while bringing a rabbit gradually closer. Example: Arjun panics before presentations; each time his heart starts to race, he breathes slowly and pictures a quiet beach in Goa, and over weeks the presentation loses its hold on him. Option c is right because the therapist is pairing a calm response that is incompatible with anxiety, so that one inhibits the other. Exam trap: flooding also targets anxiety, but it uses prolonged, full-intensity exposure with no relaxation, letting fear die out through extinction; reciprocal inhibition needs a competing response.", "traps": {"a": "Extinction (Ivan Pavlov) weakens a conditioned response by presenting the conditioned stimulus again and again without the unconditioned stimulus; it needs no competing response such as deep breathing.", "b": "Flooding exposes the person to the feared situation at full intensity for a long time without relaxation, so that anxiety extinguishes; here the therapist adds a calming response instead.", "d": "Differential reinforcement is an operant method (B. F. Skinner) that rewards a desired or incompatible behaviour while withholding reward for the unwanted one; the stem uses a classical counterconditioning idea, not consequences."}}, {"q": "If a child is brought to you for assessment and his/her social quotient or SQ happens to be 43 on Vineland Social Maturity Scale, then according to The Gazette of India, what will be the disability calculation?", "concept": "The answer is c: a social quotient of 43 falls in the moderate range of intellectual disability, which the Gazette of India guidelines rate as 75% disability. The Vineland Social Maturity Scale was devised by Edgar Doll (1935) at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey to measure social competence, the everyday ability to look after oneself and take part in community life. It was adapted for India by A. J. Malin at Nagpur as the VSMS: 89 items for ages 0 to 15 in eight areas (self-help general, self-help eating, self-help dressing, self-direction, occupation, communication, locomotion and socialisation). An informant, usually a parent, reports what the child habitually does. The items passed give a social age, and the social quotient is SQ = social age divided by chronological age, times 100, read like an IQ. Example: seven-year-old Kabir has a social age of 3 years; 3 divided by 7, times 100, gives an SQ of about 43. In India a medical board that includes a clinical psychologist certifies intellectual disability, using IQ from a standard test and SQ from the VSMS; the severity band is then converted to a percentage. Moderate intellectual disability carries 75%, well above the 40% that makes a benchmark disability under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016. Option c is right because an SQ of 43 sits in the moderate band, rated 75%. Exam trap: do not turn the SQ itself into the percentage; 43 does not mean 43%; the SQ first places the child in a band, and the band carries the percentage. Key note: the Gazette guidelines were revised in January 2018 under the RPWD Act 2016 to use SQ bands, with moderate at SQ 36 to 54, while the older 2001 bands put moderate at 35 to 49; an SQ of 43 is moderate under both and is given 75%, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "25% would describe a far milder impairment than an SQ of 43 shows; it does not match the moderate band into which 43 falls.", "b": "50% understates the case: an SQ of 43 is well below the mild range (about 55 to 69 in the 2018 bands, 50 to 69 in the older ones) and sits in the moderate band, which is rated 75%.", "d": "90% would point to a much more severe impairment; an SQ of 43 is above the severe range, which begins below 36 in the 2018 bands and below 35 in the older ones."}}, {"q": "Which one is an immature defence?", "concept": "The answer is d: acting out is an immature defence. A defence mechanism is an unconscious process by which the ego shields itself from anxiety, guilt or painful feelings; Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, catalogued the defences. George Vaillant (1977), who followed the Harvard men of the Grant Study in Adaptation to Life, ranked defences by maturity in four levels. Psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Neurotic: intellectualisation, isolation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Mature: altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation, suppression. The grouping in Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry is close to this; it calls the lowest level narcissistic and places rationalisation among the neurotic defences. George Vaillant found that men who relied on mature defences fared better in health, work and marriage. In acting out, an unconscious wish or conflict is expressed directly in action, so the person never has to feel or think about it; Sigmund Freud (1914) used the term for patients who repeat an old conflict in behaviour instead of remembering it. Immature defences are normal in adolescence and common in adults with personality disorders. Example: Kabir, 16, is hurt by his father's criticism but cannot admit it, so he slams doors, skips school and rides his bike recklessly; the feeling is discharged in behaviour and never put into words. Option d is right because acting out sits on the immature level of George Vaillant's hierarchy. Exam trap: distortion sounds worse than acting out and may be picked as 'immature', but it is lower still, a psychotic (narcissistic) defence that reshapes reality; rationalisation sounds like lying, yet it is neurotic, and altruism is mature.", "traps": {"a": "Distortion is a gross reshaping of outer reality to fit inner needs, such as grandiose delusional beliefs; George Vaillant puts it on the psychotic level and Kaplan and Sadock's grouping calls it narcissistic, which lies below the immature level.", "b": "Rationalisation, a term introduced by Ernest Jones (1908), is a logical-sounding excuse invented after the act for behaviour driven by other motives ('the job was boring anyway'); it is a neurotic (intermediate) defence, not an immature one.", "c": "Altruism is constructive, gratifying service to others that turns one's own distress into helping; it is one of George Vaillant's mature defences, with humour, sublimation, suppression and anticipation."}}, {"q": "Pica is:", "concept": "The answer is c: pica is the persistent craving for, and eating of, non-food substances. The name is Latin for magpie, a bird said to eat almost anything. In DSM-5 (2013) pica is a feeding and eating disorder: the persistent eating of non-nutritive, non-food substances, such as chalk, soil, paper, hair or paint, for at least 1 month. The eating must be inappropriate to the person's developmental level (mouthing objects is normal in infants, so it is not diagnosed before about age 2) and not part of a culturally supported practice. It is linked with iron or zinc deficiency, pregnancy, intellectual disability and autism, and it can cause lead poisoning or bowel blockage. Example: Meera, 7, who has iron-deficiency anaemia, has been eating chalk and plaster scraped off the walls for months. Option c is right because pica is defined by craving and eating things that are not food. Exam trap: each distractor is a real eating problem with another name. Overeating points to binge-eating disorder or bulimia nervosa, fear of eating to a phobia or to avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID), and food refusal to anorexia nervosa or ARFID. Do not confuse pica with rumination disorder, in which swallowed food is brought back up and re-chewed.", "traps": {"a": "Pathological overeating is binge eating, eating an unusually large amount of ordinary food with a sense of lost control; it is the core of binge-eating disorder and bulimia nervosa, not pica.", "b": "An unrealistic fear of eating is a phobic reaction (sometimes called cibophobia) or the fear of choking or vomiting seen in avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder; it reduces eating rather than adding non-food items.", "d": "Food refusal is seen in anorexia nervosa (driven by fear of weight gain), in ARFID, in severe depression and in hunger strikes; it is the opposite of eating extra, non-food substances."}}, {"q": "Pseudocyesis is:", "concept": "The answer is b: pseudocyesis is pseudo pregnancy, that is, false pregnancy. The word comes from the Greek pseudes, false, and kyesis, pregnancy. A woman firmly believes she is pregnant and her body shows real signs of pregnancy: missed or scanty periods, a swollen abdomen, breast changes, morning sickness, weight gain and even felt foetal movements, yet there is no foetus. Hormonal changes can accompany it, a striking case of mind acting on body. DSM-5 (2013) lists pseudocyesis as an example of other specified somatic symptom and related disorder; DSM-IV had placed it under somatoform disorder not otherwise specified. It is linked with an intense wish for, or fear of, pregnancy, with infertility, a recent miscarriage and depression. An ultrasound scan settles the diagnosis, and care combines a sensitive explanation with psychological support. Example: Priya, 32, has tried for a child for six years; her periods stop, her abdomen swells and she says she feels kicks, but the scan shows an empty uterus. Option b is right because pseudocyesis means a false pregnancy with bodily signs. Exam trap: 'pseudo' tempts students towards malingering, but nothing here is faked on purpose; the woman truly believes, and her bodily changes are real. Separate it from a delusion of pregnancy in psychosis, which is held without the bodily signs, and from couvade syndrome, in which an expectant father develops pregnancy-like symptoms.", "traps": {"a": "Malingering is the intentional production or exaggeration of symptoms for an external reward such as money, leave or escape from prosecution; in pseudocyesis the belief is sincere and nothing is produced on purpose.", "c": "Memory loss is amnesia, either organic (as in Korsakoff syndrome) or dissociative; it has nothing to do with a belief of being pregnant.", "d": "A delusion of guilt is a mood-congruent delusion of severe (psychotic) depression, a fixed false belief that one has committed a terrible sin or crime; it is not about pregnancy."}}, {"q": "Waxy flexibility is:", "concept": "The answer is c: waxy flexibility is a disorder of motor behaviour. It is the English name for the Latin cerea flexibilitas. When the examiner moves the patient's arm, there is a slight, even resistance, like bending a soft wax candle, and the limb then stays in whatever position it has been placed, sometimes for minutes or hours. It is a sign of catatonia, the syndrome of disturbed movement first described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874). DSM-5 (2013) lists 12 catatonic signs and needs 3 or more: stupor, catalepsy, waxy flexibility, mutism, negativism, posturing, mannerism, stereotypy, agitation, grimacing, echolalia and echopraxia. Catatonia is a specifier that can attach to schizophrenia, depression, bipolar disorder and medical conditions, and it usually responds to lorazepam or electroconvulsive therapy. Catalepsy is holding a passively induced posture against gravity; waxy flexibility adds the feel of that slight resistance as the limb is moved. Example: Arjun, 24, admitted with catatonic schizophrenia, sits motionless; when the doctor raises his arm above his head and lets go, it stays there for ten minutes. Option c is right because waxy flexibility concerns posture and movement, so descriptive psychopathology files it under disorders of motor behaviour. Exam trap: catalepsy and cataplexy look alike. Cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by laughter or strong emotion, in narcolepsy, with full consciousness; catalepsy, with its waxy flexibility, belongs to catatonia.", "traps": {"a": "Disorders of memory include amnesia, confabulation and paramnesias such as deja vu; a patient with waxy flexibility shows no change in memory.", "b": "Disorders of emotion include blunted, flat or inappropriate affect, lability and anhedonia; waxy flexibility describes how the limbs respond to being moved, not how the person feels.", "d": "Disorders of consciousness include clouding, delirium and coma; although stupor is one of the catatonic signs, a catatonic patient is usually alert, and many can later recall what went on around them."}}, {"q": "Intermittent Explosive Disorder is a:", "concept": "The answer is b: intermittent explosive disorder (IED) is an impulse-control disorder. Impulse-control disorders are failures to resist an impulse or urge to do something harmful; tension or arousal often builds before the act and relief follows, sometimes with regret afterwards. In IED the impulse is aggressive. DSM-5 (2013) describes recurrent outbursts, verbal (tirades, arguments, tantrums) or physical (assaults on people, animals or property), grossly out of proportion to the provocation. They occur either about twice a week for 3 months without damage or injury, or three times within 12 months with damage or injury. The outbursts are unplanned, not aimed at a goal such as money or intimidation, cause distress or impairment, occur in a person at least 6 years old, and are not better explained by another disorder. DSM-IV (1994) placed IED among the 'impulse-control disorders not elsewhere classified', with kleptomania, pyromania, pathological gambling and trichotillomania. DSM-5 moved it into the new chapter 'disruptive, impulse-control and conduct disorders', beside oppositional defiant disorder, conduct disorder, pyromania and kleptomania, so it remains an impulse-control disorder. Example: Rohan, 28, punches a wall and smashes his phone when someone takes his parking spot, several times a month, and afterwards feels ashamed of how little it took. Option b is right because IED is defined by a recurrent failure to control aggressive impulses. Exam trap: anger outbursts also occur in borderline and antisocial personality disorder, in mania and in disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (which DSM-5 places among the depressive disorders); IED is diagnosed only when such a disorder does not explain the outbursts, so options a and c are look-alikes, not the class.", "traps": {"a": "Personality disorders, such as borderline or antisocial personality disorder, are enduring, pervasive patterns of inner experience and behaviour from early adulthood; anger may be part of them, but IED is a separate, episodic impulse-control diagnosis.", "c": "Mood disorders (depressive and bipolar) are disturbances of sustained mood; irritability occurs in mania and in disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, but in IED the mood between the brief outbursts is normal.", "d": "Thought disorder means a disturbance in the form of thinking, such as loosening of associations or derailment, seen in psychosis; IED involves no psychotic or formal thought disturbance."}}, {"q": "Empathy is:", "concept": "The answer is c: empathy is feeling with another person. It means sensing another person's feelings and meanings from inside their own frame of reference, as though standing in their shoes, while knowing that the feelings are theirs and not yours. The word was coined by Edward Titchener (1909) to translate Einfuhlung, 'feeling into', a term popularised by the German philosopher Theodor Lipps (1903). Carl Rogers (1957) made accurate empathic understanding one of the core conditions of therapeutic change, beside unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). He described it as sensing the client's private world 'as if it were your own, but without ever losing the \"as if\" quality'. Modern research separates affective empathy (sharing the feeling) from cognitive empathy (understanding what the other thinks and feels, or perspective taking); Mark Davis (1980) measured both with his Interpersonal Reactivity Index. Example: Meera tells her counsellor that she feels guilty for studying in Delhi while her father is ill at home. A sympathetic reply is 'Poor you, that is so sad.' An empathic reply is 'You feel torn, wanting to build your future here and to be with him there, and guilty whichever you choose.' The first stands outside her experience; the second enters it. Option c is right because empathy is defined as feeling with, not feeling for. Exam trap: all four words share the Greek pathos, feeling, and the roots can mislead: sympathy literally means 'feeling together', yet psychology teaches it as feeling for (concern or pity from outside), while empathy is feeling with. Antipathy is feeling against, and apathy is lack of feeling.", "traps": {"a": "Feeling against is antipathy, a settled dislike or aversion towards a person or thing; it moves away from the other person rather than into their experience.", "b": "Feeling for is sympathy: concern, sorrow or pity for someone seen from outside, without entering their frame of reference; it is the classic near miss for empathy.", "d": "Lack of feeling is apathy, indifference with loss of emotion and drive, seen in severe depression, the negative symptoms of schizophrenia and frontal lobe damage."}}, {"q": "Hawthorne effect is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Hawthorne effect is the modification of behaviour under observation. People change how they act simply because they know they are being watched or studied, whatever the intervention itself. The name comes from the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago, where studies of worker output ran from 1924 to 1932. In the illumination experiments output rose when the lighting was increased, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed. In the relay assembly test room, a small group of women raised their output across many changes in rest pauses and working hours. The studies are associated with Elton Mayo (1933) and were reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson in Management and the Worker (1939); Henry Landsberger (1958) coined the term. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend, but the lesson stuck: an improvement may come from being studied rather than from the treatment, a threat to internal validity. Researchers guard against it with control groups that receive equal attention, unobtrusive measures and time for participants to get used to observers. Related artefacts are demand characteristics (Martin Orne, 1962) and the John Henry effect, in which a control group works harder to compete. Example: on the day an inspector sits at the back of Kabir's classroom, students who usually chat sit up straight and answer questions; nothing has changed except that they are being watched. Option a is right because the Hawthorne effect is defined by change caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: option c also mentions modification of behaviour and attention, echoing the popular explanation that the workers responded to the researchers' interest; but the effect is not behaviour aimed at winning attention, which is attention-seeking.", "traps": {"b": "Obedience is following the direct orders of an authority figure, as in Stanley Milgram's (1963) shock studies; it is compliance with commands, not change due to being observed.", "c": "Changing behaviour in order to get more attention is attention-seeking, as in a child who misbehaves because it brings notice; the Hawthorne effect is change caused by knowing one is observed, whatever the motive.", "d": "Defiance towards instructions resembles psychological reactance (Jack Brehm, 1966), pushing back when one's freedom feels threatened; participants showing the Hawthorne effect usually improve rather than resist."}}, {"q": "Psychiatric medicine can be prescribed by:", "concept": "The answer is d: psychiatric medicine can be prescribed by psychiatrists. Antidepressants, antipsychotics, mood stabilisers such as lithium and anti-anxiety drugs are prescription medicines, and in India only a registered medical practitioner may prescribe them. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor (MBBS) who has specialised in psychiatry (MD, DNB or DPM), so he or she can diagnose, order investigations, prescribe drugs and give electroconvulsive therapy. A clinical psychologist completes postgraduate clinical training recognised by the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) and must be registered with it under the RCI Act of 1992. The clinical psychologist carries out psychological assessment, psychodiagnosis and psychotherapy, but does not prescribe. A psychiatric social worker works with the family, social support, rehabilitation and community follow-up. The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 counts all of these as mental health professionals, yet prescribing stays with the medical members of the team. Example: at a hospital clinic, Arjun, 22, with severe depression, is started on an antidepressant by the psychiatrist, given cognitive behaviour therapy by the clinical psychologist, and helped back to college by the psychiatric social worker, who also meets his parents. Option d is right because psychiatrists are the only medically qualified professionals among the options. Exam trap: the word 'psychiatric' in 'psychiatric social worker' does not give prescribing rights. As a side note, a few American states (New Mexico from 2002, Louisiana from 2004) let psychologists with extra training in psychopharmacology prescribe, but this does not apply in India and is not the standard answer.", "traps": {"a": "Social workers help with social welfare, case work and community resources; they have no medical training and no right to prescribe.", "b": "Psychiatric social workers specialise in family work, psychoeducation, rehabilitation and community follow-up within mental health teams; despite the word 'psychiatric', they cannot prescribe.", "c": "Clinical psychologists assess, diagnose and give psychotherapy; in India they cannot prescribe, and only a few US states (New Mexico, Louisiana) grant prescribing rights to specially trained psychologists."}}, {"q": "Median is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the median is the middle score. When all the scores are arranged in order, the median is the point that divides the distribution into two equal halves, with 50 percent of scores below it and 50 percent above. So it is also the 50th percentile, the second quartile (Q2) and the fifth decile. With an odd number of scores it is the middle one; with an even number it is the mean of the two middle scores. Example: Riya's five test marks are 4, 7, 8, 10 and 25. The median is 8, while the mean is 10.8, pulled up by the single extreme score of 25. Because the median ignores how far extreme scores lie, it is preferred for skewed data such as income and for ordinal data. In a positively skewed distribution the mean is larger than the median, which is larger than the mode. Option b is right because 'middle' is the defining word for the median. Exam trap: the 'representative score' usually describes the mean, and the 'most frequent score' is the mode; the first quartile is the 25th percentile, not the 50th.", "traps": {"a": "'The representative score' usually describes the arithmetic mean (the sum of scores divided by their number), the most widely used and most stable average; every average represents the group, but the median's defining feature is its middle position.", "c": "The first quartile (Q1) is the 25th percentile, the score below which a quarter of the scores fall; the median is the second quartile (Q2).", "d": "The most frequent score is the mode, the only measure of central tendency that can be used with nominal data."}}, {"q": "Which of the following names is usually not connected with Existential therapy?", "concept": "The answer is c: Walter Mischel is not connected with existential therapy. Walter Mischel (1968) was a social-cognitive personality psychologist. In Personality and Assessment he argued that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly, with correlations rarely above about .30, and so started the person-situation debate. He is also known for the delay of gratification ('marshmallow') studies with preschool children at Stanford and, with Yuichi Shoda (1995), for the cognitive-affective personality system, which explains personality as stable if-then patterns of response to situations. Existential therapy comes from philosophy: Soren Kierkegaard (1844), Martin Heidegger (1927) and Jean-Paul Sartre (1943). It treats anxiety as part of being human and helps a person face the givens of life and choose how to live. Its leading names include Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy rests on the will to meaning; Rollo May (1958), who co-edited Existence and brought European existential psychiatry to America; and Irvin Yalom (1980), whose Existential Psychotherapy named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Example: after his father's sudden death, Kabir feels that nothing matters. An existential therapist would help him face mortality and his freedom to choose what gives his life meaning; an assessment in Walter Mischel's style would instead map how his behaviour shifts from one specific situation to another. Option c is right because Walter Mischel's work sits in social-cognitive personality theory, while the other three are standard names in existential therapy. Exam trap: Walter Mischel's stress on 'the situation' can sound like the existential idea of being-in-the-world, but his situations are learning contexts measured as if-then patterns, a social-cognitive idea, not a philosophy of freedom and meaning.", "traps": {"a": "Viktor Frankl, a survivor of the Nazi camps, founded logotherapy, the 'third Viennese school' of psychotherapy built on the will to meaning, with techniques such as paradoxical intention and dereflection; he is a central existential name.", "b": "Rollo May (The Meaning of Anxiety, 1950; Existence, co-edited 1958) is the leading American existential psychologist, so he clearly belongs to existential therapy.", "d": "Irvin Yalom (Existential Psychotherapy, 1980) defined the four ultimate concerns, death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness, and is the best-known modern existential therapist."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, joined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations ideas to ego psychology, and in Borderline Conditions and Pathological Narcissism (1975) he described three levels of personality organisation. Each level is judged on three features: identity, the level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (a shifting, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting, with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation and denial, and reality testing that is largely kept but may slip under stress. Psychotic organisation adds a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder; it also covers narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and he later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Ananya calls her new roommate the kindest person she has ever met, then, after one forgotten favour, calls her selfish and fake, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option b is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Melanie Klein first described splitting, Heinz Kohut (1971) also wrote on narcissism in the 1970s, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is used to explain borderline problems, so all four names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's.", "traps": {"a": "Melanie Klein (1946) described the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions and the defences of splitting and projective identification; Otto Kernberg built on these ideas, but she did not propose levels of personality organisation.", "c": "Heinz Kohut (1971) founded self psychology, which explains narcissism as a deficit left by failures of parental mirroring and idealisation (selfobject needs); he wrote on narcissistic personality, not borderline organisation.", "d": "Margaret Mahler (1975) described separation-individuation, the infant's psychological birth through subphases such as rapprochement; her work is used to explain borderline problems, but the term borderline personality organisation is not hers."}}, {"q": "______ is not an example of a non-parametric test:", "concept": "The answer is d: the independent t test is not a non-parametric test; it is parametric. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as means and variances and assumes interval or ratio scores, a roughly normal population, similar variances in the groups and independent observations; t tests, ANOVA and Pearson's r are examples. A non-parametric (distribution-free) test makes few assumptions about the population's shape and works on ranks, signs or frequencies, so it suits ordinal data, small samples and badly skewed scores. The independent t test, from William Sealy Gosset (1908), who published as 'Student', compares the means of two separate groups. Each common parametric test has a non-parametric partner: the independent t test pairs with the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947); the paired t test with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945); one-way ANOVA with the Kruskal-Wallis test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); and repeated-measures ANOVA with the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937). When the parametric assumptions hold, the parametric test is preferred because it has more power. Example: Riya compares the anxiety scores of 40 hostel students and 40 day scholars; the scores are interval and roughly normal, so she uses an independent t test. If a teacher had only ranked 8 students in each group, she would use the Mann-Whitney U test. Option d is right because the t test assumes normally distributed interval data and compares means. Exam trap: Frank Wilcoxon's name is on two tests, the signed-rank test for paired data and the rank-sum test for two independent groups (equivalent to the Mann-Whitney U), so check the design, not just the name.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares two independent groups by ranking all the scores together; it is the non-parametric counterpart of the independent t test.", "b": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) compares two related measurements, such as before and after scores, using ranked differences; it is the non-parametric counterpart of the paired t test.", "c": "The Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937) compares three or more related conditions by ranking scores within each person; it is the non-parametric counterpart of repeated-measures ANOVA."}}, {"q": "Sundowning or Sundown Syndrome is a phenomenon commonly associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: sundowning is a phenomenon commonly associated with dementia. Sundowning, or sundown syndrome, is a pattern in which confusion, restlessness, agitation, anxiety, pacing, wandering and sometimes shouting or aggression increase in the late afternoon, evening and night. It is seen most often in the middle and later stages of Alzheimer's disease, the commonest cause of dementia, and in other dementias; the same evening worsening is also typical of delirium, which often complicates dementia. Several causes probably combine. Damage to the brain's body clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, disrupts circadian rhythms and the sleep-wake cycle. Fading light and shadows make the surroundings harder to interpret, tiredness builds through the day, and there are fewer orienting cues such as visitors and routine. Hunger, pain, a full bladder, infections and some medicines can add to it. Management is mostly practical: bright light and activity in the daytime, a fixed routine, less caffeine and fewer daytime naps, well-lit rooms in the evening, a calm setting and checks for pain or infection. Example: Kabir's 80-year-old grandfather, who has Alzheimer's disease, is calm and chatty in the morning, but as dusk falls he insists he must leave for his office and tries to open the gate. Option c is right because sundowning is a recognised feature of Alzheimer's disease and the other dementias. Exam trap: depression also shows a daily rhythm, but in the opposite direction: melancholic depression is typically worst in the morning and lifts towards evening (diurnal variation of mood), so a disturbance that peaks in the evening points to dementia or delirium, not depression.", "traps": {"a": "Depression shows diurnal variation in the reverse direction, worst in the early morning in melancholic depression; it can mimic dementia (pseudodementia), but sundowning is not a feature of depression itself.", "b": "Alcohol dependence brings tolerance, craving and withdrawal; its severe withdrawal state, delirium tremens, begins about 2 to 3 days after the last drink, but sundowning is not a recognised feature of dependence itself.", "d": "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder of hallucinations, delusions, disorganisation and negative symptoms; its symptoms are not tied to the time of day."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not based on operant conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is b: implosion is not based on operant conditioning; it rests on classical conditioning and extinction. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour through its consequences: reinforcement makes a response more likely, punishment less likely. Implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) instead targets a conditioned fear. The client imagines the most feared scenes at full, exaggerated intensity, often enriched with horrific or psychodynamic themes, for a long session with no escape. Its logic comes from O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory: fear is learned by classical conditioning, and avoidance keeps it alive. When the feared cue (the conditioned stimulus) is met again and again without the dreaded outcome (the unconditioned stimulus), the fear extinguishes. Flooding applies the same idea, usually in real life; implosion is flooding in imagination, and it is rarely used now. Example: Arjun, terrified of snakes, is asked to picture for an hour snakes coiling round him and biting, while the therapist keeps the scenes vivid and blocks every escape; his fear peaks and then falls. The other three are operant. Contingency management gives reinforcers, such as vouchers, for a verified target behaviour, such as drug-free urine samples. A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives tokens for target behaviours that are exchanged for back-up rewards. The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) says a more probable activity can reinforce a less probable one. Option b is right because implosion works by extinguishing a classically conditioned fear, not by arranging consequences. Exam trap: all four are behaviour therapy techniques, so 'behavioural' does not decide the answer; ask whether the method arranges consequences (operant) or pairs and unpairs stimuli (classical).", "traps": {"a": "Contingency management is operant: reinforcers such as vouchers, prizes or privileges are delivered only when a target behaviour is verified, for example drug-free urine samples in substance use treatment.", "c": "A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) is operant: tokens, which are secondary reinforcers, are earned for target behaviours and exchanged for back-up reinforcers, as first done on psychiatric wards.", "d": "The Premack principle (David Premack, 1959) is an operant rule: a more probable activity reinforces a less probable one, as in 'finish your homework, then play cricket'."}}, {"q": "Regarding Ganser's syndrome, which of the following is true?", "concept": "The answer is b: the hallmark of Ganser syndrome is approximate answers. Sigbert Ganser (1898) described the syndrome in prisoners awaiting trial. The patient gives answers that are wrong but close to the right one, which shows that the question has been understood: asked how many legs a cow has, he says 'five'; asked what 2 plus 2 makes, he says '5'. German psychiatry called this vorbeireden, 'talking past the point'. With it come clouding of consciousness, pseudohallucinations and conversion symptoms, and when the episode ends, often suddenly, the person has amnesia for it. ICD-10 lists Ganser syndrome among the other dissociative (conversion) disorders, and DSM-IV gave it as an example of dissociative disorder not otherwise specified. It is rare and appears mostly under severe stress, such as detention. It differs from dementia, where errors come from real and lasting cognitive loss, and from depressive pseudodementia, where the person answers 'I don't know' rather than giving near misses. Example: Ravi, held in custody before his trial, is asked the colour of grass and says 'blue', and asked how many fingers he has says 'eleven'; days later he remembers nothing of the interview. Option b is right because approximate answers are the defining sign named after Sigbert Ganser. Exam trap: because the patients were prisoners with a reason to seem unwell, a long debate asked whether the syndrome was simply malingering, which is why 'feigning illness' is offered; the classic view treats it as a dissociative state in which the person is not consciously faking.", "traps": {"a": "Repeated, elaborate lying is pseudologia fantastica (Anton Delbrück, 1891), seen in factitious disorder and Munchausen syndrome; Ganser patients give near-miss answers, not tall stories.", "c": "Ganser syndrome brings clouding of consciousness, a dreamy narrowing of awareness, not full loss of consciousness; 'unconscious episodes' suggest fainting, seizures or coma.", "d": "Feigning illness is malingering, the deliberate production of symptoms for external gain; Ganser syndrome was long suspected of being malingering because of the prison setting, but it is classed as dissociative."}}, {"q": "A type II error occurs:", "concept": "The answer is c: a Type II error occurs whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted. In significance testing the researcher decides between the null hypothesis (no effect, no difference) and the alternative hypothesis. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) set out the two ways this decision can go wrong. Laid out as a table, there are four outcomes. If the null is true and we reject it, that is a Type I error (alpha), a false positive; its probability is the significance level, usually .05. If the null is false and we accept it, or more strictly fail to reject it, that is a Type II error (beta), a false negative: a real effect is missed. Accepting a true null and rejecting a false null are both correct decisions. Power, 1 minus beta, is the probability of detecting an effect that is really there; Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested aiming for at least .80. Power rises with a larger sample, a larger effect, less measurement error and a more lenient alpha, so for a fixed sample, lowering alpha to cut Type I errors raises the risk of a Type II error. Example: Meera tests whether a new revision method raises marks. The method truly works, but with only 10 students her result is not significant and she concludes it has no effect: a Type II error. Option c is right because a Type II error is keeping a null hypothesis that is actually false. Exam trap: the definitions are easy to reverse. Type I is rejecting a true null (option d, alpha, the false alarm); Type II is accepting a false null (beta, the miss). Think of the boy who cried wolf: first the villagers believed in a wolf that was not there (Type I), later they ignored the wolf that was (Type II).", "traps": {"a": "Accepting a true null hypothesis is a correct decision: there is no effect and the researcher rightly finds none.", "b": "Rejecting a false null hypothesis is also a correct decision, a real effect correctly detected; its probability is the power of the test.", "d": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (alpha), a false positive, the reverse of the Type II error."}}, {"q": "Which type of reinforcement is least resistant to Extinction?", "concept": "The answer is c: fixed interval reinforcement is the least resistant to extinction of the options given. Resistance to extinction is how long a learned response continues once reinforcement stops. Continuous reinforcement, a reward after every response, extinguishes fastest. Partial (intermittent) reinforcement makes behaviour last longer, the partial reinforcement extinction effect (Lloyd Humphreys, 1939). Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) mapped four basic partial schedules built from two choices: ratio (reward depends on the number of responses) or interval (on time passed), and fixed or variable. Fixed ratio gives a high rate with a pause after each reward, as in piece-rate pay. Variable ratio gives the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance, as in slot machines. Fixed interval gives a pause, then faster responding as the time approaches (the scallop). Variable interval gives a moderate, steady rate. Predictable schedules extinguish faster because the switch to no reward is easier to notice; on a variable schedule, long gaps without reward have always happened before. Example: Meera's college posts results every Monday, so she checks the notice board only on Mondays; when it stops being updated, she soon gives up. Her brother Kabir plays a game that pays coins after an unpredictable number of spins, and he keeps spinning for days after the coins stop. Option c is right because, among the four basic schedules, introductory texts give fixed interval as the easiest to extinguish. Exam trap: 'ratio interval' (a) is not a schedule at all; it joins the two dimensions. Key note: continuous reinforcement, which is not offered, extinguishes fastest of all; among the partial schedules both fixed ones extinguish faster than the variable ones, and introductory texts single out fixed interval as the easiest to extinguish, so fixed ratio (d) is the near miss and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Ratio interval' is not a schedule: ratio (number of responses) and interval (time) are two separate dimensions which, combined with fixed or variable, give the four basic schedules of Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner.", "b": "Variable interval reinforces the first response after unpredictable stretches of time, as in checking for a message; it gives a moderate, steady rate and strong resistance to extinction, because long gaps without reward always looked normal.", "d": "Fixed ratio reinforces after a set number of responses, as in piece-rate pay; it also extinguishes faster than the variable schedules, which makes it the near miss, but fixed interval is the schedule texts single out as least resistant."}}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "concept": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (1905) better; Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "traps": {"b": "Stages that mark new levels of cognitive development are Jean Piaget's (sensorimotor to formal operational); Erik Erikson's stages concern social and emotional conflicts, not reasoning ability.", "c": "Erik Erikson's epigenetic principle does assume a built-in ground plan, but each stage is defined by its social crisis; stages as pure biological programming fit Arnold Gesell's maturation theory or Sigmund Freud's libido-driven stages better.", "d": "Erik Erikson's stages are not symptoms that should 'appear'; everyone passes through all eight, and difficulty comes from a poorly resolved crisis, not from a stage failing to show up."}}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "concept": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of death and dying. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-born American psychiatrist (the paper spells her first name 'Elizabeth'), interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital and reported what she learned in On Death and Dying. She proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live until my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work pushed doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice and palliative care movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best seen as common reactions, not a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: told that his cancer has spread, Ramesh, 60, first insists the scans were mixed up (denial), then snaps at his nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up smoking if he can see his grandson born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Elisabeth Kübler-Ross.", "traps": {"a": "Eating disorder research is linked with names such as Hilde Bruch (anorexia nervosa) and Gerald Russell, who named bulimia nervosa in 1979; Elisabeth Kübler-Ross did not work in this area.", "b": "Adolescent behaviour is the field of G. Stanley Hall (storm and stress, 1904) and Erik Erikson (identity versus role confusion), not Elisabeth Kübler-Ross.", "c": "Coping with stress is Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman's (1984) transactional model of appraisal and problem-focused or emotion-focused coping; dying is a stressor, but Elisabeth Kübler-Ross studied the specific reactions of the dying."}}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the internal standard used to judge stimuli is called the adaptation level. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) proposed adaptation-level theory to explain why the same stimulus can feel heavy or light, warm or cool, loud or soft. He argued that we never judge a stimulus in absolute terms. Instead, the perceiver keeps a running neutral point, the adaptation level, pooled from three sources: the focal stimulus being judged, the background stimuli around it, and residual stimuli, the traces of past experience. A stimulus above that point feels heavy, bright or pleasant; one below it feels light, dim or unpleasant; one at it feels neutral. The level keeps shifting as experience changes. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. The idea later spread to happiness: Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) argued that people adapt to good fortune, so pleasures fade into a new neutral point, the hedonic treadmill. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his adaptation level has moved up. Option a is right because adaptation level is Helson's technical term for this internal neutral point. Exam trap: 'frames of reference' sounds like the same idea and is the planted near miss. Key note: adaptation level is often described as an internal frame of reference, so c is the near miss; 'frame of reference' is the broader idea that includes outside context, while adaptation level is the technical name for the internal standard, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Intuition is knowing or judging something quickly without conscious reasoning; it is not a standard or reference point against which stimuli are rated.", "c": "A frame of reference is the broader set of standards and surroundings, outer as well as inner, used to interpret an experience (Muzafer Sherif applied it to social norms); adaptation level is Harry Helson's specific name for the internal neutral point, so c is only the near miss.", "d": "Context is the external setting around a stimulus; in Harry Helson's theory it feeds the adaptation level as background stimulation, but it is not itself the internal standard."}}, {"q": "__________ theory maximized and __________ theory minimized the role of the unconscious:", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Trait theorists such as Gordon Allport and Raymond Cattell describe stable, measurable dispositions and say little about the unconscious, so trait theory cannot be the approach that maximised it.", "c": "Psychoanalytic is right for the first blank, but humanistic theory (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) only played down the unconscious; Rogers still accepted that experiences can be denied to awareness, whereas behaviourism set the unconscious aside entirely.", "d": "Behaviourist fits the second blank, but trait theory never maximised the unconscious; Gordon Allport even stressed that healthy adults are guided by conscious, present motives."}}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "traps": {"a": "True on its own: in CAMS, CBT alone (59.7% improved) and sertraline alone (54.9%) did not differ significantly; but because b and c are also true, choosing a alone is incomplete.", "b": "True on its own: CBT alone produced a good response in about 60% (59.7%) of children in CAMS; but a and c are true as well, so this single option undersells the findings.", "c": "True on its own: combination treatment (80.7%) beat both CBT and sertraline alone; but a and b are also supported, so 'All of these' is the complete answer."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "traps": {"b": "An enduring pattern of unstable, intense relationships, swinging between idealisation and devaluation, is a core DSM criterion for BPD, so it is a cardinal feature, not the exception.", "c": "A lack of a stable self-image is the DSM identity disturbance criterion and matches Otto Kernberg's identity diffusion, so it is a cardinal BPD feature.", "d": "Frequent, unpredictable mood swings describe affective instability (marked reactivity of mood lasting hours, rarely days), a core BPD criterion."}}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "traps": {"a": "Block design belongs to Samuel C. Kohs (1920), whose coloured cubes are arranged to copy printed patterns; David Wechsler later borrowed it as a subtest. It is a performance test, but not the one Porteus built.", "c": "Non-verbal cues are body language and facial signals studied in communication research; no classic performance test is built on them, and the phrase only sounds like \"non-verbal test\".", "d": "Drawing a person is Florence Goodenough's Draw-a-Man test (1926), revised by Dale Harris (1963); it estimates intellectual maturity from the details in a drawing, not from maze tracing."}}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "traps": {"a": "Delinquency has no role in IQ scoring; this distractor plays on the word 'deviation', which in statistics means distance from the mean, not deviant behaviour.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age is the ratio IQ of William Stern and Lewis Terman ((MA ÷ CA) × 100), the method the deviation IQ replaced.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores were the two halves of David Wechsler's older scales (dropped in the WAIS-IV); comparing them is profile analysis, not the basis of the deviation IQ."}}, {"q": "_____ is a sub process of _____:", "concept": "The answer is c: interviewing is a sub-process of psychological assessment. Psychological assessment is the broad process of collecting and combining information from many sources to answer a referral question about a person: Is this a learning disability? How severe is the depression? Which treatment suits? Its sources include the clinical interview, behavioural observation, psychological tests, the case history, school or medical records and reports from family. Psychological testing is narrower. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour; it yields a score. Assessment, as Gary Groth-Marnat stresses in his Handbook of Psychological Assessment, is the clinician's work of integrating test scores with everything else into a reasoned conclusion and recommendations. So testing, interviewing and observation are all sub-processes, or tools, within assessment; none of them contains the others. Example: Kabir, 9, is referred for falling marks. The psychologist interviews his parents and teacher, talks with and observes Kabir, gives an intelligence test and a reading test, reads his school reports, and then writes a report that ties it all together. The whole sequence is assessment; the interview is one step in it. Option c is right because it is the only option that puts a component (interviewing) inside the umbrella process (psychological assessment). Exam trap: testing and assessment are often used loosely as synonyms, so options that put testing above interviewing or observation look plausible; assessment is always the umbrella, and the other three are its parts.", "traps": {"a": "Psychological testing and observation are both sub-processes of assessment that sit side by side; testing is not a part of observation, although a tester does observe behaviour during a test.", "b": "Testing and interviewing are parallel tools within assessment: testing gives standardised scores, interviewing gives history and context, and neither is a sub-process of the other.", "d": "This reverses the relation: observation is one of the methods used within psychological assessment, not the larger process that contains it."}}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "traps": {"a": "50% is the area above the mean (z = 0), the whole upper half of the curve; by z = +1 another 34% has been passed, leaving about 16%.", "c": "25% is the area above the third quartile, which lies at about z = +0.67, not at z = +1.", "d": "10% is the area above about z = +1.28 (the 90th percentile), so it is too small for z = +1."}}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal fluency tests, such as the FAS (Controlled Oral Word Association) test, ask the person to generate as many words as possible from a letter or category in a minute; the Stroop asks for no word generation.", "b": "Visual perception tests, such as Arthur Benton's Judgment of Line Orientation, assess how a person sees shape and space; the Stroop assumes normal colour vision and measures the control of attention instead.", "c": "Memory is tested by tools such as the Wechsler Memory Scale or the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test; the Stroop stimuli stay in front of the person throughout, so nothing has to be remembered."}}]});
